BMW's first-quarter earnings higher than expected
The company reported sales growth in all major regions of the world, especially in China, between January and March.
In the same period last year, pre-tax revenue of BMW fell to 798 million euros when the first wave of the virus forced showrooms to close worldwide.
But BMW said last month that the recovery began in the second half of 2020 and continued to gain momentum as countries eased restrictions on epidemics and strong demand in China. Which is the main market.
The Munich-based German company said in March that it now expects to see a significant increase in pre-tax profits this year and plans to accelerate the transition to electric vehicles as the global economy moves. He is recovering from a health crisis.
Following a 13 percent increase in the electric car market in 2020, BMW now forecasts growth of more than 50 percent over the next five years, followed by an annual increase of 20 percent by 2030.
By 2030, half of its car sales will be electric models, the statement said.
BMW will release its full first quarter results on May 7.
