E-business requires VAT
This sub-decree is for the purpose and purpose of defining special conditions and setting out mechanisms for the implementation of value-added tax on digital goods and digital services that are e-commerce for use in Cambodia and supplied by non-resident taxpayers. Permanent establishment in Cambodia to ensure effective implementation in accordance with Article 75 of the Tax Law.
This sub-decree applies to the supply of digital goods or digital services electronically or to any e-commerce activities carried out by non-profit suppliers outside Cambodia to Cambodia.
Separately, e-commerce activities in Cambodia must comply with the provisions on Value Added Tax in force.
According to the sub-decree, tax registration for non-permanent taxpayers who do not have a permanent institution in Cambodia who conducts e-commerce is subject to the tax registration obligation based on the self-declared taxpayer classification. Determined by the Prakas of the Minister of Economy and Finance.
This listing applies only to VAT purposes.
This sub-decree adds that non-resident taxpayers who receive a supply of digital goods or digital services or any e-commerce activity to consumers in Cambodia are obliged to file a declaration and pay value added tax to the tax administration. The tax is due no later than the 20th day of the month following the month in which it is paid.
Non-resident and non-resident taxpayers who do not fulfill their obligations in the spirit of this sub-decree shall be punished in accordance with the laws and regulations on tax in force of Cambodia.
Government strengthens patent tax collection procedures
This Prakas is made for the purpose of defining the rules and procedures governing the collection of patent taxes on the business activities of the self-declared taxpayers, which are applicable to the self-declared taxpayers who conduct business in Cambodia.
Patent tax is an annual tax levied on business activities based on the type of taxpayer and the level of income.
The proclamation, published on April 8, states that taxpayers with multiple business activities must pay different patent taxes for each business activity.
For example, a taxpayer who engages in the business of exporting food, transport and hotel must pay patent tax for three different business activities.
Separate activities of the business activities are considered as a single business activity.
For example, a hotel with a nightclub, restaurant, massage parlor, gym or other activities that are an appendix to hotel activities that are under the direct control of the same taxpayer and are in the same location as the hotel must pay the same patent tax.
Taxpayers who have a primary business location, head office, enterprise, branch, warehouse, factory, workshop for the same business activities in the same provincial capital must pay the same patent tax, and taxpayers who have business activities in different provincial capitals must pay patents Depending on the provincial capital where the business is located.
The patent tax level shall be paid as follows:
Small taxpayers have to pay an annual patent tax of 400,000 riels.
The patent tax is paid between January 1 and March 31 each year.
The patent tax is paid at the tax administration where the taxpayer actually has a place of business.
Taxpayers who start a business in the first six months of the year have to pay the tax, pay the patent tax for the whole year, but if they start the business in the last six months of the year, the taxpayer has to pay half the patent tax.
