Wine production from mango, turmeric is positive
The wine, made from mango and turmeric, was first tested on March 9 in the Samaki Tambor agricultural community in Tbong Khmum province.
The community has 70 mango grower families, with an area of about 127 hectares planted with mango, turmeric, equivalent to about 30,000 trees, which can yield an average of around 30 tons per year.
Heng Piseth, director of the Tbong Khmum Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, told the Post on March 15 that this was an initiative of the ministry's leadership as well as the provincial agriculture department to find ways to help raise prices for products. Local agriculture, as well as income for farmers who are growing mangoes, creating new jobs and using local raw materials to the fullest extent possible.
He said that the production of wine from mango, turmeric will take 14 days, but after the trial production on March 9 so far, the results are remarkably positive.
He added that if there is a lot of support, he will help promote production for the domestic market as well.
However, he stated that this is only the first step, as he and the community have not yet discussed how to prepare a commercial wine processing business plan and study research on mango processing techniques to standardize packaging. Not yet.
Mr. Oum Sophea, President of Samaki Tambor Agricultural Community in Tbong Khmum Province, said that during each harvest season, there are only large quantities of mangoes, which causes the price to fall sharply, so he and the leaders of the Department of Agriculture discuss to prepare experts Come and help train techniques in processing mangoes into wine.
Mango wine production is gaining a lot of attention from farmers in the Samaki Tambor farming community.
He said that the first test production was produced in 11 barrels with a capacity of 20 liters per barrel containing 7.5 kg of mangoes and turmeric.
According to Mr. Piseth, there are currently about 2,000 hectares of mango plantations in Tbong Khmum province, yielding about 30 tons per hectare per year.
Mangoes now cost about 200 riel per kilogram, about a year ago, and most are exported to Vietnam.
According to Veng Sakhon, Cambodia's Minister of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, it exported 43,680 tonnes of fresh mangoes in 2020.
China, Thailand, Vietnam, Russia, France and South Korea are major markets for mangoes from Cambodia.
