In your opinion, is the removal of the EBA a solution to the political situation in Cambodia?
Sanctions are one of the EU's tools to promote peace, democracy and the rule of law, human rights and international law.
But the EU does not impose sanctions on countries that supply strategic equipment, such as the Democratic Republic of Congo, which is also an EBA recipient, or those who are good customers of the European arms industry, such as Egypt.
The EU remains silent on major human rights abuses in many countries for political reasons.
Sanctions often have consequences that affect the daily lives of most people.
In the name of defending political rights, the fundamental rights enshrined in Articles 22 to 27 of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights of 1948, which include access to food, health, education, employment and welfare, were violated. ...
In the name of democracy and human rights, they can even provoke civil war, as recent history shows us, especially with examples of death.
Punishment belongs to the logic of international relations, where the strong impose laws on the weak.
This is contrary to the spirit of the UN Charter.
But it will not live up to EU expectations.
In your view, what should the EU do to improve Cambodia's democracy and human rights?
First, the EU should be impartial when it comes to judging Cambodia's political life.
But this is not the case.
The EU has never condemned calls for racism and fear of foreigners by CNRP leaders.
They have never condemned the CNRP's practice of defaming and slandering government members.
The use of fake maps and fake treaties by the CNRP has never been condemned to make it look like the government is transferring parts of the territory to Vietnam.
In addition, the European Union continues to remain silent about the populist attitude and the popularity of the CNRP.
It calls those things vivid democracy.
The EU has always welcomed CNRP leaders who are encouraged to work hard in expressing their opposition.
The EU treats them as if they were their representatives.
The EU maintains all its criticism of the government.
The EU is not neutral.
EU officials have said they only protect values and principles.
But no one can trust such a statement.
The EU should understand rather than judge.
Political pluralism is a novelty in Cambodia.
It did not happen before, during, or after colonization until 1993, and it is not enough to have a beautiful constitution or an election to make democracy fair.
The level of education is still low and it provides the basis for the population.
There is no national unity on major issues and there are many divisive factors.
The EU, for example, could do a useful job of helping the Cambodian people better understand the realities of Cambodia's history, which has often been manipulated for political reasons by providing technical assistance to the functioning of democracy. Representative kindergarten that respects people and institutions.
So far, Cambodia has cooperated with the European Union.
The Cambodian side is genuinely willing to achieve many common goals.
Negotiations are ongoing and there is real progress on some complex topics.
One example is that the EU requires strict adherence to international conventions, which are not 100% respected by many beneficiaries of the EBA.
But even though it is seen as a double standard practice, the government is making significant improvements on a number of issues.
One example is in the social sphere.
When the EU demands such efforts, Europe forgets that there is a formidable challenge for Cambodia.
Any social progress, it involves the risk of the company, they can go to another country.
A good option for the EU and Cambodia is to continue constructive dialogue with mutual respect and with practical results.
But the EU should not forget that Cambodia is a sovereign state, so let the government implement and improve what it wants.
Many EU officials in Brussels seem to treat Cambodia as an EU member state.
They want Cambodia to have what it has.
For the welfare of the people.
Welfare can be achieved when the basic needs of the people are satisfied, such as adequate food, health care for all, and equal opportunities for all, with regard to education, the right to adequate housing. Work for a decent salary and live in a clean environment.
To achieve these goals, what is most important and necessary is time and political stability.
I would like to quote the former French Prime Minister, who said:
What does freedom of expression mean to the ignorant?
What does equality and fraternity mean for the afflicted?
However, Cambodia must find its own way to reconcile development and democracy in order to stabilize multilateralism and political dialogue.
At this point, it is possible, but there are conditions.
First, if all politicians agree on some of the key issues that make it impossible for the country to live in peace, territorial issues, stories of crime and genocide under the Pol Pot regime, and the liberation that has taken place Founded in 1979, explore and learn to live peacefully with neighboring countries and be neutral.
Second, all politicians must respect themselves and respect institutions and laws, and the third condition is that if there is a will for democracy to function peacefully, there will be no democracy in an atmosphere of civil war.
Environmental Hero Award winner warns of report on forest destruction
Continuing from the previous issue ... The Phnom Penh Post would like to end the story of the brief history and active forest protection work of the winner of the World Environmental Hero Award, Ouch Leng, for the third part in this issue after missing the fourth week of this month Before.
With descriptions of deforestation caused by the rampant timber trade of some billionaires who raised millions of dollars through logging, World Environmental Hero Award winner Ouch Leng has warned against releasing a new report. On logging and transportation activities from Cambodia.
According to the World Cup winner, his new report on deforestation in Cambodia will be released after the July 2018 national election to avoid allegations of political affiliation.
In addition to cheating or crawling through the forest to get to the logging area, the only way to reach the restricted area is to have an acquaintance or good relationship with those inside the area. Easy without worries.
Because the situation in the area is carefully guarded.
Not only did he call the timber trade area in Sesan a logging city, he also considered the area a large market with fun places for a group of timber traders.
Those who go outside can not have fun there if they do not have good relationships or know each other well with the people inside.
A large number of officials are also good because it is true, but some officials say that there is no logging, there is a small transport by motorbike and so on.
It does not represent the realities of the timber business.
I can not draw a picture of a timber truck until it is true, I took it to show evidence of deforestation so that the public can consider whether it is true or not?
Despite knowing that loggers have guns to protect their business, the winner shows no fear or fear of them.
Ouch Leng has already vowed that he will not withdraw from the mission to protect the forest, which could endanger his life.
He said that even the military police or the police who cracked down were not safe because of the weapons of the loggers.
Before becoming a well-known forest activist and winner of the World Environmental Hero Award, the hairy man who risked his life for a forest protection mission, his seniority, was also a human rights activist for Adhoc and LICADHO.
Mr. Ouch Leng has worked in the field of human rights for more than 10 years and the international organization earns more than $ 1,000 per month, which can easily support his family.
But obsessed with the forest and seeing deforestation escalate, the man left his human rights work and returned to work in the environment and forestry.
The world champion said that if he did not volunteer to protect the forest, he would not be short of jobs.
He could have been a judge or a commissioner and worked with the organization through his knowledge of law, but due to the devastation of the forest, the man could not remain silent.
Mr. Leng confirmed that:
Who does not love trees, and how can we live without a better environment if there are no trees, because trees help people.
Defending the forest for more than 20 years, Ouch Leng knows many big timber traders or tycoons who have been collecting millions of dollars from the timber trade.
The forest activist also referred to one of the timber tycoons who once boasted of the influence of his timber business, claiming that in his timber business, if Prime Minister Hun Sen and General The Deputy Commander of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and the Commander of the National Gendarmerie, General Sao Sokha, did not crack down.
Dare to sacrifice his life and energy to protect the forest, Ouch Leng won the 2016 World Environment Hero Award in the United States and was named the Game Changer. In Asia in 2017.
When I ask the winners, they vote for me millions online.
I am supported because I work hard to protect the forest, protect the environment in Cambodia, as well as on behalf of the world, because the forest is not mine alone, it belongs to all of us.
Ouch Leng worked for the human rights group Licadho from 1996 to 2005 and worked for the human rights organization AHOC from 2005 to 2011.
He then left to work for USAID for a year, working to protect the forest.
Living in a poor family in Phnom Penh, Ouch Leng attended Samaki School, but did not study part-time with them except for some public school hours and did not have appropriate clothing.
The boy's part-time studies sometimes stand by the window, but Ouch Leng struggled until graduating from Bakdup High School in 1990 and graduating with a bachelor's degree in law in 1997.
Reminiscing about the past, living with his father, riding a cyclo and his mother with a bad memory, begging, made the world champion winner burst into tears, reminiscing about the difficult times he used to live. Hungry with poor parents with six dependent children, three boys and three girls.
What a tragedy my life, all the teachers, the principal, everyone knows about my struggle.
However, Ouch Leng's parents still live a normal life, different from about 30 years ago, even though he is a little old.
At the age of 47, Environmental Hero Award winner Ouch Leng was married in 1999 with three dependent children.
The forest protection activist was originally from Bati district, Takeo province, a farmer's son.
Mr. Ouch Leng: Deforestation has increased 10 times while we are in a political crisis
Ouch Leng, son of a cyclo driver and a beggar, becomes a world champion
Last week ... Last week, the Phnom Penh Post highlighted the heroism of a brave individual, the winner of the World Environmental Hero Award, Ouch Leng, a well-known forest protection activist who dared to risk his life in connection with a dangerous mission. In one direction to protect the forests that are facing destruction due to the timber business of the billionaire.
His real-life story or biography and mission to protect the forest, which he fought to become a world-renowned celebrity and champion, will be further described this week.
Indeed, the struggle has paid off, Ouch Leng, who once struggled to make ends meet in a poor family, has now swum to the brink of happiness.
Although the World Cup winner is not a person living in a luxurious, civilized family who spends all his dollars like some wealthy families or tycoons, but for Ouch Leng's current family situation He can live comfortably and be able to continue to protect the forest.
As an expert in monitoring deforestation, he noted that the dry season is a good opportunity for traders in their timber business to transport timber.
From September or October, they go into the forest to cut down trees, and they start transporting timber from October to April.
The rush to transport goods before the Chinese New Year and after the Chinese New Year.
In addition, December 10 and January 7 or 1-2 days before is also a good opportunity to rush to transport timber, because the holiday is a holiday for government officials or individuals of various institutions, but for traders. The timber business is not their holiday, but it is their day to transport their timber.
The winner of the Forest Conservation Award states:
Until the national level, he can give the green light to the lower level, he can.
Because even the government knows about this timber business, it is corrupt in a consistent manner.
According to Mr. Ouch Leng, who claimed to have met directly with the chief of the logging company, the process of transporting timber went smoothly because of the timber traders. Bribed relevant officials in all institutions from lower to higher.
He also revealed that some of the big timber traders who are making money from their timber business all have high positions in government.
They cut down trees, set up timber depots in protected areas, and smuggled them out of the corridor into Vietnam, colluding with officials at the border.
With a ban on access to the national park, which is a national park for all, Ouch Leng noted that the reason for the ban was because of logging in the national park.
Hundreds of Vietnamese are hiding, logging and transporting timber to Vietnam.
I'm talking about the truth, the truth, if they transport the wood, I do not see.
I see me talking, I report to let everyone know, and the government knows, but he does not know, he does not take action, it is his business.
Sometimes we travel with truck drivers with timber traders to get live information.
In the middle of the forest, they don't think about the law, they don't think about human rights.
Ouch Leng says deforestation is on the rise
World Environmental Hero Award winner Ouch Leng, a well-known forest activist, has expressed grave concern about the growing deforestation in Cambodia, which is experiencing a political crisis and the absence of the opposition, which he considers a mirror. Of the government, because when there are MPs of this party in the National Assembly, just questioning about the interests of the nation, do not allow the ruling party to ride a horse.
Ouch Leng has decided that he will be willing to sacrifice all his physical and mental strength, even in the face of serious problems that overwhelm him, he will not back down. Complete the mission to protect the forest, even if it costs lives.
By working directly on forest protection missions, Ouch Leng learned that the scale of deforestation is becoming more widespread in Cambodia, such as the Sesan area of Stung Treng province, where he described the Sesan area previously had about 100-200 hectares only.
But Sesan has now become a huge market for the timber business, which the World Cup winner calls a logging city with thousands of hectares of timber depots.
In an interview with the Post on March 13, Mr. Ouch Leng expressed his unwavering courage in the forest protection mission:
Now it is up to thousands of hectares, which means that deforestation has increased tenfold.
And while we have a political crisis, no opposition party, no parliamentarians to debate issues related to natural resources as well as the forest, so the duke returned to collect deforestation and transport it to Vietnam freely.
According to Ouch Leng, in the current Sesan region, there are more Vietnamese than Cambodians, about 60-70 percent, who rush to collect forest natural resources from Cambodia to benefit their country.
The timber is transported from the Sesan area from 200-300 trucks a day, and they can earn millions of dollars a day from these exports, which is a huge loss to the national budget.
Opposition parties have also been working hard to get the government to focus on protecting forests and protecting natural resources.
Thus, there is no opposition party, only the government, the tycoon merchants from Vietnam invaded.
In Cambodia's forestry business, which seeks a handful of people close to the powerful to become billionaires, international award winner Ouch Leng also said some traders or tycoons are being sued. In court as well.
Some tycoons have summonses from the court with criminal charges, but the Cambodian district court has not bothered the rich individuals, as they are only trying to make their timber business a success. .
Deforestation in Cambodia, which is almost devastated, is considered by the award winner to be a cross-border forest trade industry.
He not only referred to the export of timber from Cambodia to Vietnam, Ouch Leng also referred to the United States, Japan and China, where Vietnam also imported timber to those countries.
Wood is not like drugs hidden anywhere, wood is transported to anyone, but he does not know how to take action because they are all officials under his command.
All the dukes he proposed.
Although some of the tycoons whom Ouch Leng knew were involved in the timber business and had donated money to help build roads and schools, he considered those donations not to be linked to crime. No forest.
Preventing or suppressing forest trade and making small contributions to the construction of something must be divided.
Do not protect the wood on the paper only before the election or protect the wood during Plant Day and when meeting with donors and tell them to protect the forest as well.
In terms of company development, companies that come to develop in social or economic land concessions, they only look at the benefits of forests and natural resources in the land.
The revenue from this dense timber business, he said, refers only to the timber that is transported from three areas: Phnom Prich in Mondulkiri, Kantuy Neak Virachey in Ratanakkiri, adjacent to Mondulkiri and Sesan in Stung Treng. And Ratanakkiri province.
Well-known forestry activist Ouch Leng also likened the area where the timber trade is free to a large city, calling it a logging city with karaoke venues, gas markets, and mobile phone shops.
