The winner must win with honor, and the loser must lose with all his heart.
The party that competes to win votes from the people must not infringe on another party.
If people, teachers, workers call me, His Excellency seems to be pushing me away from him.
I am very worried that people will insult me, so I work for the people, spend the people's money, so if I can not protect the will of more than 3 million people, I have to decide to walk out, no matter how much it costs.
Rong Chhun resigned from the NEC on November 20 after the CNRP was dissolved by the Supreme Court on November 16 over allegations that the party leader, Kem Sokha, had committed treason. The nation conspired with foreigners to defeat the government of Prime Minister Hun Sen.
Since resigning more than 10 days ago, Mr. Rong Chhun has no clear plans to continue his social work, even though he has been the head of many civil society institutions.
However, he has taken a clear stance in the direction towards continuing his social work after a short break, but he has not yet revealed what his new job is.
According to the plan, he will continue to work on the promotion of human rights and democracy, but he will not return to the position of President of the Cambodian Independent Teachers Association.
Leng Peng Long: National Assembly prepares car money for new MPs to replace former opposition party
A new coalition of lawmakers from various political parties who are lucky enough to share in the effortless distribution of the CNRP's 55 seats in about two weeks will walk into the palace. The National Assembly to present the people who own those seats after the main opposition party, which represents more than 3 million voters, was dissolved last week.
At the same time, the National Assembly has been preparing budgets, equipment, such as luxury cars, and the role of chairman of each commission to allow new MPs from political parties who are willing to receive seats from the opposition to serve the people. At the grassroots level, such as the 55 former MPs of the Cambodia National Rescue Party, the main opposition party in Cambodia.
In response to the preparations for the new lawmakers, the National Assembly also urged CNRP lawmakers involved in the use of the National Assembly's equipment or vehicles to return them to parliament as soon as possible.
In an interview at the National Assembly building on November 21, National Assembly spokesman Leng Peng Long fully supported the dissolution of the CNRP by the Supreme Court's November 16 ruling, accusing the party. The National Rescue Party, which won 55 seats in the National Assembly and thousands of seats in the commune council, the opposition party won by fraud, coming through illegal foreign aid.
Therefore, dissolving the CNRP and allocating seats to other political parties is the right decision.
