Now we dissolve the CNRP, but we have a replacement to come back to the parliament.
So I asked for a car from the former chairman of the National Rescue Committee to give to the next chairman.
In response to the resignations of the Vice Chairman and three members of the National Election Committee (NEC) from the CNRP quota after the dissolution of the main opposition party, the Standing Committee of the National Assembly has already prepared the law to select new members. Sit in the chair of the NEC member who resigned because the NEC is the institution responsible for the National Assembly.
If the NEC is not accurate and there is no neutrality in its role, the National Assembly will convene in front of the National Assembly.
Mr. Peng Long stated:
Because we follow all political parties.
The composition of the NEC is from all political parties.
Neutrality, neutrality is the implementation of the law, follow the law and be neutral.
Do not be biased, the law is neutral.
To follow the law, it sees the results, so it is no longer recognized.
Now there is more pressure on the NEC.
The NEC is accountable to the National Assembly.
If he does not do well, the parliament can invite him to explain.
The National Assembly is now waiting for a list of political parties that will accept the CNRP's 55 seats after the NEC has already divided them according to the number of seats they will send to the National Assembly. Hold a meeting as soon as possible to select NEC candidates.
This is Cambodia's democratic solution, and ultimately the people are the judges.
When asked about the possible difficulties with the procedure of performing the duties of some new MPs who have no experience from the party that has not won the election in the past, Mr. Peng Long also mentioned the advantages of the new MPs. Of FUNCINPEC Party of Samdech Krom Preah Norodom Ranariddh, praising that both Samdech Krom Preah and his members have experience as MPs.
Therefore, there is no complication in fulfilling the role of MP in the procedure.
He practices his party's politics, so it's normal, there are no problems.
Mr. Uth Chhorn, through 10 careers, came to the job of the spokesman of the Constitutional Council
When he was a young man in his family, he was the only one who dreamed of getting involved in politics, but his chances of getting involved in politics were far from him until after the fall of the Khmer Rouge regime in 1979.
Throughout the past three generations, Uth Chhorn worked only as a professor of physics and mathematics, before becoming a senior official and politician in the 1980s during the People's Republic of Kampuchea, Kampuchea State and Currently, the Kingdom of Cambodia, which has been under the leadership of the Cambodian People's Party under Prime Minister Hun Sen for almost 40 years now.
At the age of 70, Uth Chhorn had no serious health problems and his physical body looked like he was in his 60s.
Uth Chhorn is currently a member and spokesman for the Constitutional Council, the nation's supreme body for interpreting the law and is the final arbiter of the legal forms and proposals submitted.
As a young man, his interest in politics prompted him to pursue a law degree after graduating with a bachelor's degree and graduating with a law degree in 1975, which he claimed was his favorite subject.
Despite his dream of becoming a politician, the opportunity to enter politics is still far from his dream other than a career as a professor.
It was not until after 1979 that his wish came true as described above, and at that time he also pursued another master's degree in management, graduating with a master's degree in 1997.
Although he was educated and became a professor in General Lon Nol's Republic, he considered that the regime was not good for the people. Cheap makes the country war, waiting for help.
However, the Lon Nol regime alone did not frighten him to death like the Khmer Rouge regime of Pol Pot.
By the time he began his political career and was a senior official nearly four decades ago, Uth Chhorn had gone through 10 positions in his career.
Later, he was the head of the department at the Central Committee of the Cambodian People's Party for five years, and then he was the head of the financial supply department at the Council of Ministers for another five years.
Mr. Chhorn also rose through the ranks, serving as Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Economy and Finance from 1999 to 2001, and for 10 years until 2011 he was promoted to Auditor General of the National Audit Authority.
After the term of office of the Auditor General, he was a member of the National Council Against Corruption until 2013 before becoming a spokesman for the Constitutional Council.
Mr. Uth Chhorn said that the Constitutional Council strives to fulfill its role in accordance with the Constitution.
Although the Constitutional Council, the nation's supreme body, has been widely criticized for being biased in its role as a legal entity interpreter, law spokesman Uth Chhorn Protected the procedure for interpreting and finalizing the legal forms or cases that have been proposed and submitted.
In an interview with the Post on November 8, Uth Chhorn seemed unconcerned with criticism from various quarters, calling the criticisms an "opinion and expression" of the individual. The Constitution strives to fulfill its role in accordance with the Constitution with the utmost care, without prejudice to any party or political party.
But the work of the Constitutional Council is very careful.
All the work is to look at the law, look at the constitution, before issuing any law that is submitted to examine the constitution, it is very clear that place.
The Law on Political Parties, the Law on Elections, and the Law relating to the role of the National Election Committee, which divides the seats, the Constitutional Council considers to be unconstitutional, as the law states that all election work must be organized by the National Committee. Organize the NEC election, so the NEC redistribution of seats is a work in accordance with the Constitution.
The law is to match the constitution.
Thus, we see that it is in accordance with the Constitution, we have issued.
In the past seven terms of the Constitutional Council's seven-term election dispute, the institution, which is considered an impartial arbitrator, has received 114 complaints and that the Constitutional Council has properly examined The Constitution deals with the complaints of political parties before blocking protests.
Separately, the registration of voters, the campaign of political parties, election day and the announcement of results, as a result, the Constitutional Council recognizes the election process went smoothly.
When asked if the power of the Constitutional Council could be rejected or pushed back to the government as well as the National Assembly for reconsideration after the law was urgently amended, Uth Chhorn, a former senior government official, said: It states that the government and the National Assembly have a role to play in amending or drafting laws, but amending the law is necessary for society.
Second, the law relating to the National Audit Authority requires the Second Committee of the National Assembly to conduct an inspection if the National Audit Authority wants to investigate any irregularities, but since both proposals are unconstitutional, the Constitutional Council has objected.
In addition to opposing the law, the Constitutional Council also rejected several other laws, according to Uth Chhorn, but he did not specify them.
So, before making a decision, think a lot, think about whether the decision is wrong the next day or not, so do not dare to be biased.
He added that the decision to close the protest route made the Constitutional Council very careful before doing so.
A former political prisoner for more than two years, former Sam Rainsy Party senator Hong Sochua, who was convicted in connection with a sensitive border issue, is being held in a cramped Prey Sar prison, mingling with criminals for other crimes. More than a year and a half ago, he was sent to another cell with more than a dozen political prisoners who were his associates in the opposition for the last six months before being released.
