The 73-year-old monk used to live in a large mansion guarded by hundreds of high-ranking officials and bodyguards, then called his bodyguards, but now the left and right guards. Almost all of them have left him, but only a few officers and bodyguards remain loyal to protect and enter and leave a small house in the Tonle Bassac Borey.
Despite the changes, he did not seem to care about the small or large house and the hospitality, saying that he was happy even though he was in the small house. There is better housing than many other people.
Kong Koam speaks of leaving ruling party
According to Kong Koamdan, history is the reason why he decided to leave the ruling party without remorse.
He said that as far as he knew from history, Cambodia was under foreign ambitions, referring to Vietnam because after 1979 the government of the People's Republic of Kampuchea, of which he was also a senior official, was Installed by Vietnam, this neighboring country also colonized Cambodia.
Mr. Kong Koam said:
I'm just interested in Vietnam's ambitions.
We can say that Samdech Norodom Sihanouk, the leader of the tripartite, also opposed Vietnamese control, and Pen Sovann did not agree with Vietnamese control.
Chan Si, the next prime minister, Pen Sovann, also disagreed with Vietnamese policy toward Cambodia.
Based on this historical trace, Kong Koam decided to leave the ruling party after the establishment of a political solution, the Paris Peace Agreement of October 23, 1991 to end control over Cambodia.
Later, in 1992, he applied to leave the CPP to escape Vietnamese control.
Mr. Kong Koam confirmed that:
At 76, Kong Koam does not appear to have any serious health problems and currently lives in a mansion located on a large plot of land formerly the headquarters of the Sam Rainsy Party and the CNRP in Tonle Bassac.
As a veteran politician, Kong Koam was a senior official who dared to protest against the content of the CPP convention, which set out a strategy of military action, tripartite negotiations between Cambodians and Cambodians to maintain power.
Due to the use of such a strategic plan against the Khmer, he advised the leaders of the General Assembly of the Cambodian People's Party not to use military force against the Khmer and Khmer, while all parties have been negotiating since 1988.
Kong Koam entered politics under the auspices of the Cambodian People's Party in 1979 after Cambodia liberated the Khmer Rouge regime through two of his friends in his hometown of Kampong Cham, now Tbong Khmum province. He was also a senior CPP official who brought him to meet with CPP leaders Chea Sim and Hun Sen.
He served as a diplomat in Hanoi in the early 1980s and as Foreign Minister from 1986-1987, replacing Hun Sen, who succeeded Chan Si as prime minister.
Kong Koam was later ousted, according to his claim that the removal was due to CPP leaders accusing him of not supporting the talks, which were later given reasons for his removal. As negotiations begin.
He claimed that in fact he supported the talks, but did not support the use of military measures against the tripartite negotiations.
I do not agree, I do not agree with the strategy of using measures to achieve complete and final revolutionary victory.
I put back the slogan back: let the enemy live, let the Khmer not die.
Turning to his past in the Republican regime of General Lon Nol before the fall of the Khmer Rouge regime, this veteran politician was the former principal of Samdech Chuon Nath Secondary School, Phnom Pros Phnom Srey, Kampong Cham province in 1970-1973.
Before becoming a school principal, Kong Koam was a professor of French language and Khmer literature from 1965-1973 after graduating from Phnom Penh with a pedagogical degree.
He was not interested in politics as a child until 1979, when he was introduced to senior CPP officials by a friend and began his political career. Then come.
Kong Koam is also an influential leader in the CNRP.
He used to be a senator and president of the Sam Rainsy Party and resigned from both positions about a year ago due to his retirement age.
Despite taking a break from politics, the veteran politician, who was once outspoken in his criticism, has not left the political scene, with Kong Koam now appointed a senior adviser to the CNRP.
Kong Koam does not expect the ruling party to tolerate the opposition
A senior CNRP adviser, Kong Koam, a former political ally of Prime Minister Hun Sen, is not optimistic his party will survive.
Therefore, he does not expect this influential former friend to loosen his grip on the CNRP as a political rival for next year's election, meaning that the ruling party can eliminate the presence of strong rivals from the ring.
To combat such a fragile situation, former Foreign Minister Kong Koam, who has walked out of the CPP's political arena since 1992 after establishing political life with Hun Sen since 1979, has resumed his life. Politics with the opposition He presented a new political path for the opposition to resume political life in case the party is dissolved.
Kong Koam urged leaders, members and supporters to form a new movement or party by gathering votes in preparation for the 2018 election.
He told the leaders and members of Cambodia's main opposition party, no matter what the circumstances, do not return the plate to the Sam Rainsy Party and the Human Rights Party, their respective old parties, like senior officials. The government has said no, otherwise the vote will be scattered and returned to complete failure.
If the president is convicted, the CNRP will be dissolved, and a new party must be mobilized to mobilize CNRP activists and the people to compete in 2018.
The intention of the ruling party is to break up or eliminate now, after splitting Sam Rainsy, break up and Kem Sokha is now eliminated, because Kem Sokha is guilty again, he has gone to eliminate.
It is better to let the National Rescue Party win the election and they will not hand over power, it is worse than dissolving it.
Concerned about the dissolution of the opposition, Kong Koam also called on the ruling party, which he considers a plaintiff, to drop the lawsuit and return to discussing a solution, not looking to dissolve the CNRP. What is important is that the ruling party looks at the will of the people who voted for the CNRP in half of the country, which could lead to significant changes.
He added that if Mr. Hun Sen dropped the charges against Mr. Sokha and turned to a solution together, it would be most respectable and admirable.
The ruling party must be tolerant and tolerant, and the opposition party should not have a culture of water, fish, ants, ants, fish and continue to unite to bring this dangerous situation back to normal by this election.
Kong Koam accused the ruling party of having two strategies to defeat the opposition: disintegration and elimination, and that overthrowing the opposition was even more serious than disintegration.
He also said that in January, the ruling party would begin to split the vote of the Senate, the CNRP's commune council, to get them to vote for the CPP.
Separately, in the 2018 national election from February to May, the ruling party may use the strategy of separating the CNRP MPs by attracting them to surrender to the ruling party.
Regarding the accusation of Kem Sokha for treason, Kong Koam considers this accusation to be unfounded, but because the court was under the influence of politics, he was convicted and according to the newly amended law on political parties, if the president As a prisoner and the whole party supports the president who is a prisoner, the party may dissolve.
However, Kong Koam did not urge Kem Sokha to resign as party president to save the party from the dissolution of the Hun Sen government, which has been trying to dissolve since Sam Rainsy was president, despite his knowledge that the government Strong men continue to attempt to dissolve the CNRP under the leadership of Kem Sokha.
President Sam Rainsy resigned to save the CNRP.
But ask the court and the leaders of the ruling party to consider this issue in the interests of the nation, the interests of the people, the interests of democracy.
