Pung Chhiv Kek: Peace must be linked to human rights
Readers know the starting point of the peace talks between Prince Norodom Sihanouk and Samdech Hun Sen, Prime Minister and Head of Foreign Affairs of Cambodia during the 1980s.
One event that Dr. Pung Chhiv Kek will never forget is that one night a Phnom Penh delegation led by Hun Sen met her French husband at the residence of the French Ambassador to Angola.
The meeting starts from 8 pm to 1 am.
In fact, the Phnom Penh delegation and her husband did not meet in a small Cambodian house, as the newspaper reported earlier.
Today, she would like to highlight the positive effects that Cambodia has received from the Paris Agreement of October 23, 1991.
What does this day mean for Dr. Pung Chhiv Kek herself as well as her homeland?
Through interviews with LICADHO founder Licadho, who initiated the meeting between Sihanouk and Hun Sen, she thinks the agreement has brought some notable achievements:
Second, Cambodia held free and fair elections in 1993 and adopted a new constitution.
Third, the Paris Agreement requires any government born out of elections to promote and protect human rights.
This means that Cambodia must extradite and implement those extraditions.
Ms. LICADHO President reminded that in 1992, Cambodia has the Supreme National Council, which Samdech Ov chaired 12 members.
The Supreme National Council has made several extraditions at international conferences on human rights.
Therefore, since the first election in 1993, Cambodia has had four special rapporteurs monitoring human rights in Cambodia, as defined in the Paris Agreement.
However, it is not possible to measure how much Cambodia has complied with the terms of the agreement.
We have taken this agreement as a model and made many extraditions.
We have taken international law as a model so that we can make national law so that it does not contradict international human rights law.
She blamed the civil war in Cambodia for its devastating social and economic impact on human rights.
Separately, Cambodia is lagging behind its neighbors for 50 to 60 years due to the war.
For the nation to develop well, firstly, there must be no war, and in addition, there must be social stability, which means that there should be no discrimination against women, children, ethnic minorities, religions, people with disabilities, colors and genders. And so on.
For Dr. Pung Chhiv Kek personally, the Paris Agreement of October 23, 1991 means the day when the Cambodian people ended the war, liberated their right to life and to be able to develop further.
But she says both days are the same historical day.
Victory Day, January 7, 1979, was the day the Cambodians survived the Khmer Rouge, and October 23, 1991 was the day the Paris Peace Accords brought what is today.
June 4 is the day that Cambodians vote for their favorite commune chief.
She said that in the event of a political crisis, politicians should consider the model of peace talks and reconciliation, as Prince Norodom Sihanouk and Hun Sen had implemented on the October 23, 1991 agreement.
She says resolving the issue with violence will lead to serious losses, so only peace and non-violence will benefit both the people and the politicians.
She warned that quarreling with the same Cambodians was a cause of hostility and frightened investors and tourists alike.
Arguing leads to enemies around them taking advantage.
"Peaceful countries need to be closely linked to human rights, non-violence, freedom of the press and democracy," she said.
But it is not just Cambodia that has experienced violence in France or Germany.
A historic political dialogue dates back to December 2, 1987, between two top politicians, Prince Norodom Sihanouk and Samdech Hun Sen, former heads of Cambodian diplomacy in the 1980s.
Subsequently, the dialogue reached the Paris Agreement between all parties to the conflict in Cambodia on October 23, 1991.
Finally, a free and fair election was held in Cambodia under the auspices of UNTAC in 1993.
The fruit of peace was largely attended by Dr. Pung Chhiv Kek, President of LICADHO.
