Ieng Moly: Life in the Fire of War and Peace
Last week, the Post's readers were already aware of some of Ieng Moly's background and life in the fires of war and peace.
This week, the Senior Minister continued to comment on his political life with a number of political figures, especially Son San, former president of the Khmer People's Liberation Front (KPNLF).
The Front was born in October 1979 and is based on the Thai-Cambodian border.
Relations with the former leader began in France until the Paris Agreement talks on October 23, 1991.
Ieng Moly said there were a number of reasons why he became so close to Son San that he was fortunate enough to receive the post of secretary general of the Cambodian People's Liberation Front.
This weakened the resistance of the Cambodian People's Liberation Front.
The rule of the army does not allow the political leaders to trivialize the great merits of the fighters on the battlefield.
Son San is a thorough and good person.
But he was a deaf person, which led to a conflict with the commander in his front.
Son San During the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era of King Norodom Sihanouk in the 1960s, he was given the title of Son San.
The family had a good relationship with the royal family.
This event ignited the flames of war and led to the Khmer Rouge genocide of 1975-1979.
He left Phnom Penh for Beijing on January 5-6, 1979 on a plane organized by Khmer Rouge leaders.
The Khmer Rouge at the time intended to use Prince Sihanouk to proclaim to the United Nations in New York accusing Vietnam of invading Cambodia.
His Majesty traveled from Beijing to New York as promised with the Khmer Rouge leaders.
But while Sihanouk was in New York, Son San, the president of the Khmer Cultural Association in France, and all the members of the association celebrated by writing a letter to Kona and saying that Son San Gathered generals during Lon Nol's army, ready to help him and planned to drive Vietnam out of Cambodia.
He secretly wrote a small letter to the FBI, which secured him, asking them to help free him from the Khmer Rouge sent by Pol Pot.
After fleeing the Khmer Rouge, Samdech Ov wanted to stay in the United States or France, but at that time, France answered with conditions, and the United States did not answer.
While France and the United States looked down on Prince Sihanouk, China saw his importance.
Ieng Moly explained that China thought that only Prince Sihanouk was important to China because China understood that Samdech could lead the struggle against Vietnam.
During the 1980s, a tripartite government was formed to negotiate peace.
In this sense, the tripartite wanted to force Vietnam to enter the negotiating table by forcing Vietnam to acknowledge that it was an aggressor committing crimes against Cambodia as a landlord, while the Cambodian state saw Vietnam as a liberator.
That is why there was an important Sihanouk-Hun Sen meeting.
Importantly, Samdech Hun Sen and Samdech Ov have a common goal: to bring peace to Cambodia and form a legitimate government through popular elections.
Consequences of the Paris Agreement of October 23, 1991 According to Molly, the agreement did not completely end the Cambodian conflict, but it did provide Cambodia with a legitimate, internationally recognized government.
All participating countries have signed up to the recognition of territorial integrity.
Second, Cambodia has a new regime: a constitutional monarchy with a king but no power.
We need time, we can not force it.
Ieng Moly: Life in the Fire, War and Peace
Author of the book Life on the Road to Flexion, born in 1950 in Snay Pol village, Roka commune, Pearang district, Prey Veng province.
This book details the life story of a political figure who went through political events in Cambodia during the war and peace period.
This figure contributed to the creation of history as well as the observer and historian.
He passed the entrance exam at the Faculty of Commerce, Phnom Penh Business School, but did not finish. Due to unfavorable circumstances, he was forced to find a job at a glass factory.
After that, he came to work at Khmer Liquor Company and during that time he continued to study accounting.
Between 1970 and 1973, Moly worked in many private places and gained a lot of work experience and some money.
In August 1973, he contacted the Association of Accountants in France to find a school in France.
He did not go through the Khmer Rouge regime, but felt the pain of ordinary Cambodians.
On the other hand, students are prohibited by law in France from working for a fee.
I go in August on vacation, shops or companies in France close almost all vacations.
It wasn't until September that I got a job after spending a month helping to wash the dishes and prepare the house for the temporary occupants.
Under French law, Moly is not allowed to work because he only has a visa to study.
Fortunately, he had a French friend to help cover up his history.
But gradually he ran away from the law by seeking political asylum in France.
In the same year, 1973, Ieng Moly learned that Prince Norodom Sihanouk, who was in Beijing, had formed a front in an attempt to overthrow the Lon Nol regime, but at the time he was reluctant to join his front because he did not. Know who the real leaders of the Khmer Rouge resistance movement were.
Ieng Moly is starting to have a better life because he has the right to earn a living.
Not only that, he was appointed Chief Accountant with the ability to hire a maid as well.
The events of April 17, 1975 brought tears to his eyes as news of the Khmer Rouge victory brought the Cambodian people back to life under Sihanouk.
That is the news of the forced evictions and evictions of people from Phnom Penh.
News of such evacuations reminded Molly of hating Cambodian leaders who often hurt young people.
That is the main reason why he decided to stop being involved in politics in Cambodia for a while, focusing entirely on earning a living.
But when the Khmer Rouge collapsed on January 7, 1979, he was deeply concerned that the entire social infrastructure had been destroyed and the presence of foreign troops.
Meeting between Ieng Moly and Son San Ta Son San, a former governor of the National Bank of Cambodia during the Sangkum Reastr Niyum regime of Prince Norodom Sihanouk in the 1960s.
He was a Buddhist and used to be a symbol of the pure and undefiled.
He was invited to comment on Cambodian politics as president of the Cambodian General Association Abroad.
The General Khmer Association Abroad draws in most members, former prime ministers, ministers, generals, government officials, diplomats and young intellectuals.
Ieng Moly was introduced by two friends to Son San and assisted in the political work of his leadership.
The reason why he wanted to meet Son San was because of the news through the refugees and the devastation that the Khmer Rouge had committed in Cambodia.
During the conversation, Son San admitted that he had failed to advise Sihanouk and Lon Nol to negotiate a national solution in the early 1970s.
Moly also resigned as a private company accountant to spend more time in politics.
