Leur Lay Sreng: I enjoy my life
Speaking with pride, Elder Leur Lay Sreng stated that he had no Khmer blood at all, but his brain was more concerned with the interests of the Khmer nation than the Khmer people.
The 82-year-old Se Chen Chhao seems to be enjoying the course of his life, both in the past and in the present, especially in his last life, knowing full well that his three children are grateful to their father.
Last week, the Post reported on his activities and background.
Swimming and tasting all the flavors of life, hot and cold, success and failure, poverty and civilization are events that fulfill the meaning of his life.
Life's obstacles do not always bring negative consequences to people, but rather change the destiny of a poor person to become a high official in society.
As a rude politician, he has nothing to hide in sharing his personal history.
In a recent interview with The Post, Who is Who, he described how hard he had been since he was a child.
It was that effort that changed his life and destiny through hard work until he received a scholarship to study in the United States and held the position of Member of Parliament, Deputy Prime Minister, Senior Minister and Minister.
He was the happiest young man under the leadership of King Father Norodom Sihanouk in the 1960s, a society where people had dignity, civil servants were respected by the public, and teachers were given brides.
Unfortunately, the country plunged into civil war after the coup d'état of March 18, 1970.
However, Cambodia regained a taste of peace after the 1993 UNTAC elections.
Mr. Leur Lay Sreng was the Minister of Rural Development.
He still sees a huge gap between the rich in the city and the poor in the countryside, which he calls for better economic policies than at present through the use of Cambodia's existing national wealth.
He said that in order to expand the agricultural potential of the rivers and lakes, Cambodia must have water for the dry season and the rainy season, which is the responsibility of the provincial governor and the provincial development department of each province to be responsible for making fish water to support the needs of Citizens.
He reminded of the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era that the generals in the old society were more dignified than today.
The father of three is deeply concerned about the fate of young Cambodians today.
He said that the number of young Cambodians today, especially children aged 12-13 and above, is being drugged almost all over the country.
Their attitude of hating each other among young people who often cause fights during drums or at weddings is a negative sign for society.
Poor and illiterate people need to be taken care of, while gambling drugs, including lotteries and lottery numbers, are a scourge that harms society.
The problem of irrigating society is getting worse and worse and the government will not be able to solve it.
To address this concern, Leur Lay Sreng urged the government to use the law as a basis for finding a solution.
He says the reason for the daily migration is due to the need for a stomach.
The national justice system, especially the judiciary, has angered the poor.
The poor are being abused and the poor are getting hotter and hotter.
Having been involved in national politics for most of his life and going through many hardships in the past, Elder Leur Lay Sreng gave a final message to politicians of all parties: Only favor and understanding Have the idea of taking Cambodians as enemies and do not take revenge on each other so that Khmer land survives.
So far, there are only two parties that I see, the National Rescue Party and the CPP, and they have to be kind to each other.
