In March, his children will be celebrating their 82nd birthday in Phnom Penh, which he looks forward to.
Leur Lay Sreng: FUNCINPEC smashed like broken glass
Born in Kong Pisey District, Kampong Speu Province in 1935, he was a raw Khmer Chinese whose parents were refugees from Shantou Province, China, fleeing famine and war in China.
He has lived a miserable life since childhood and has lived through five regimes in Cambodia for more than eight decades.
He knew and witnessed many events, both when the nation was engulfed in war and the nation was at peace.
This is the life story of 82-year-old Leur Lay Sreng.
Leur Lay Sreng graduated with a diploma in Kampong Speu province and came to study in Phnom Penh at Sisowath High School in Phnom Penh in 1954.
Since coming to Phnom Penh, he has rarely gone to school because he is busy earning a living.
At the end of 1957, Mr. Leur Lay Sreng took the entrance exam for Russey Keo Vocational School.
After graduating in 1966, he returned to work as the general manager of a palm sugar factory in Kampong Speu.
Elder Leur Lay Sreng is a retired politician who has been involved in both politics and business.
In the 1980s, he joined the resistance movement on the Thai border led by former King Norodom Sihanouk.
He was a member of the Royalist Party FUNCINPEC under the leadership of Prince Norodom Ranariddh.
He served as a Member of Parliament in 1972 during the Khmer Republic under General Lon Nol.
He is a former Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Rural Development, Minister of Information and elected Member of Parliament for Kampong Speu after the 1993 UNTAC elections.
For the sake of patriotism, Elder Leur Lay Sreng abandoned his two sons and daughters to live in poverty abroad while joining the resistance movement on the Thai border and engaging in politics in Cambodia.
The retired politician spent nearly two hours telling the Post about his personal life and political life as he swam through countless obstacles on the Thai-armed border and the political battlefield. Country.
He recalled some events at the time that in 1970, the situation in the country was not good, the Vietnam War broke out with the United States near the Cambodian-Vietnamese border.
His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk, the Head of State of the Kingdom of Cambodia, traveled to France, allowing the Vietnamese, Viet Cong troops to camp and place medical supplies and other military equipment in Svay Rieng and Kampong Cham provinces.
The United States saw this and dropped bombs on Viet Cong camps.
This is according to the explanation from Mr. Leur Lay Sreng.
The problem was that at that time, his relatives did not allow the medicine to be delivered to the Viet Cong, which was based on Cambodian territory, which caused the leader of Hanoi, Vietnam, to burn and call His Majesty.
Therefore, Mr. Lon Nol's group was scared and went to Queen Kosomak Neary Roth, the mother of King Norodom Sihanouk, who was on the throne at the time, and begged him to help find a solution.
He also sent envoys to negotiate for His Majesty to return from Paris, France.
Therefore, on March 18, 1970, Lon Nol's army announced a coup to overthrow Sihanouk.
According to Leur Lay Sreng, Lon Nol had no intention of staging a coup to overthrow His Majesty.
Because he came, he refused to come back.
