No political stability, no economic growth
A resident of Kampong Thmor, Kampong Thom Province, Mr. Mey Van was born in 1969 to a well-to-do family of civil servants whose father was a civil servant in the Department of Fisheries of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries in Phnom Penh.
Like other children, at the age of nine, he passed through the Pol Pot era, being separated from his parents to work in the children's unit in Chamkar Leu district on the border of Kampong Thom and Kampong Cham.
At that time, there was no education at all, only forcing the children to work, both sick and healthy, and without enough food to eat, their knees were bigger, they were overworked, there was no medicine, and they were very weak.
After the fall of the Pol Pot regime on January 7, 1979, Mi Van returned to live with her parents in her hometown and attended Kampong Thmor Primary School in Kampong Thom province until the end of primary school.
In 1982, when his father came to work at the Department of Fisheries, the boy came to study in Phnom Penh in 1983 at Tuol Sleng School, obtained a primary certificate and continued his studies at Tuol Svay Prey High School until Boeung Trabek High School. .
Economics is a profession that he has not been very interested in since he was in school.
After failing the exam, he entered the medical school because he was good at mathematics and chemistry, but he did not achieve his goal.
Later, he took the entrance exam at the Institute of Technology, studying in Russian, and at the same time had the option of taking the economics exam in Vietnamese, and finally decided to pass the entrance exam at the economics school according to his parents' wishes.
1993 was the year that Cambodia held its first national elections organized by the United Nations, UNTAC, and also the year that Mey Van joined the Ministry of Economy and Finance after graduating from the current Institute of Economics at the National University. Manage.
There he met relatives and friends of Cambodians in France in order to learn about the civilized way of life.
Mey Van, now 48, is the director general of the General Department of Financial Industry of the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
With nearly three decades of experience, Mey Van has seen tremendous developments in the field, especially over the last two decades.
The Royal Government, which emerged after the 1993 national elections, has worked hard to prepare for ASEAN membership, with a number of qualifications and strategies in place to attract foreign direct investment (FDI).
Finally, an economic community was integrated at the end of 2015 so that Cambodia could take advantage of ASEAN policy.
In this context, the main principle of the government is to make investors trust the government by maintaining political stability, social security and attractiveness in investment, including laws, customs, taxes, physical infrastructure and human resources.
The government began to reform from a planned economy to a free market economy, treating the private sector as the engine of the economy while the country still lacked many laws at the time.
It is not easy for a country that lacks laws, regulations, and regulations to promote investment: Cambodia has adopted a softer strategy, limiting the issuance of guidelines by the Minister of Economy and Finance and the advice of some other ministers. Is applicable instead.
Along with gaining experience in making manuals, the Ministry also began to pass a series of laws.
Due to better political stability and security, as well as the liberalization of the United Nations, the UN has always been supportive of Cambodia, a member of the United Nations.
That is why Cambodia is able to attract investors in the garment sector, which employs more than half a million women workers.
Foreign investors see the sector as able to absorb Cambodia's young labor force.
Agriculture, garments, garments, tourism and construction continued to be strong enough to support GDP growth of 7% per year.
Tourism continues to have great potential through the implementation of an open skies policy, allowing direct flights to Phnom Penh and the Angkor area of Siem Reap.
In 2016, the Asian Development Bank (ADB) assessed Cambodia as an economic tiger and its value increased year by year.
In the past, Cambodia was number one in ASEAN and number five in the world.
The bank now estimates that we could be third in the global growth.
Mr. Vann said that in addition to the big vision, Cambodia has other interesting sub-sectors such as banking, microfinance, insurance, real estate and gambling, casino and lottery.
These four sub-sectors have been growing remarkably.
He said the provision of insurance services would bring benefits to Cambodians.
Casinos are also an important sub-sector that is allowed to do business along the Cambodian-Vietnamese border and the Cambodian-Thai border.
The second is the casino, which is a very good strategy to make the border area a peaceful and developed area, and Cambodia does not allow Cambodians to visit, Cambodians do not have access to the border.
The outlook for the Cambodian economy for the next 10 years depends entirely on political stability.
He added that at the same time, the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports should make a guideline for the university, which should allow the rector to work with the government to see the developing world.
Incentives are part of the government's direction and increase infrastructure.
Finally, the economist advised young people to seize the opportunity to study any subject they like and work hard on that skill before starting their own business.
Together to preserve Khmer literature and language from foreign aggression
A former professor of Khmer literature expresses deep concern over the lack of attention paid to the study, understanding, preservation and appreciation of Khmer literature and language, which may be the main reason for the Khmer people to lose their national identity while foreign languages and literature are Popular among young Cambodians today and the next generation in the era of globalization and modern technology.
Each society has its own national symbol, including literature, language and culture.
In some countries, trees or flowers are chosen to represent their nation.
As a Khmer slogan says:
It means that culture covers everything.
So when the language is gone, the culture is gone.
In fact, during the Khmer Rouge era, Pol Pot was abolished, the monks and religion were abolished, and that was the reason for the dissolution of the nation.
This is according to a retired professor who almost told the Phnom Penh Post in the Who is Who program in the past.
Last week, readers already knew the professor's background that he had studied Khmer and Balinese literature when he was a monk in the mid-1970s before the advent of the Pol Pot regime. April 17, 1975
That is why he loves and sacrifices for Khmer literature.
Not having studied and knowing one's own culture and literature is not only disrespectful, but also disrespectful of national literature.
Today, the trend of Cambodians is to learn English to a degree beyond learning the Khmer language.
Parents of today's students have different preferences than decades ago.
Many Cambodian families send their children to learn foreign languages and speak more foreign languages than Khmer at home and at school.
There was a female student who was fluent in French and French culture, and she was against what was Khmer.
At one point, when a teacher told me about the origins of Pin Peat music, Maori dance, she cried because her parents did not turn on the radio in Khmer and Khmer songs.
From small to large, she used to listen only to French news and French music. When she heard the Khmer music, she was upset with herself and felt betrayed.
According to Mr. Soeng, culture, language and literature are very valuable to the nation.
Khmer ancestors compiled and created their own alphabet with 33 letters and 23 vowels as the basis for writing all the words that Khmer people had to communicate with each other.
Khmer has had its own alphabet and language since ancient times, but some have not studied it and do not value it because they do not know it.
According to his research, the Khmer language has a history related to Pali and Sanskrit, because according to the inscriptions carved in Sanskrit.
According to the research of Khmer and foreign scholars, the Khmer people began to have the Khmer script before 661 7th century.
This is according to an inscription in Angkor Borei, Prey Kabbas district, Takeo province, which is an ancient Khmer territory.
In 661, the Khmer had their own script, because according to the research of scholars that when the Khmer know how to write and write on the stone, it means that the Khmer had letters before 661.
Scholars find the Khmer alphabet in the order from A, B, C to A, the same as the Sanskrit alphabet, or almost entirely in the order of the first and second verses.
In terms of Khmer traditions and civilization, the Khmer had their own culture and script before India brought Brahmanism and Buddhism.
It was not until the arrival of India that Brahmanism and Buddhism further strengthened the roots of culture to this day.
The influx of Buddhism from India means that 300 years after the Buddha's death, there was a third general assembly.
The Buddha entered Nirvana for three months, the first meeting for one year, the second meeting for 300 years, the third meeting, with the most knowledgeable monk leading the meeting and beginning to spread Buddhism.
In modern times, no scholar has changed the Khmer script.
But in the 1960s, a group of left-wing scholars tried hard to change the way the Khmer language was written into single syllables to make it easier to type on a typewriter.
Conservatives, however, opposed the left.
With different languages spoken in different regions and colonies in Cambodia, that is why Khmer ancient scholars have compiled grammar rules for writing so that all Cambodians can learn the same rules in the same language. Listen and understand as the official language of the nation.
It has a subject, a verb, and a subject similar to some foreign language.
At the Royal Academy of Cambodia, there are frequent disagreements over Khmer literature.
According to research, it is found that Khmer people write 50% original Khmer script, 50% Pali Sanskrit script.
According to the same research, the Khmer script was copied by the Thais and Siamese as their own script, and some of the letters were taken back by the Thais from Thailand through Thai influence as a neighboring country.
In this story, I still remember the explanation of Samdech Preah Sangkhareach Chuon Nath about the word criticism as a copy of the Khmer word satire.
Samdech gave an interview on National Radio, explaining that one day a group of Sisowath students came to worship him in the afternoon.
In fact, our original word is a mockery of us, taken back from Thailand as a criticism.
Professor Seng Phos recalled that after the liberation in 1979, Vietnamese, Russian, Spanish and German were encouraged to study according to the country's political system, which was flexible according to circumstances.
Regarding the Khmer language dispute, in the past, there was a group of gravity led by former leftist scholar Keng Vansak, who wanted to change it easily, but another group wanted to preserve it.
In this regard, the National Council for the Khmer Language has decided to follow the rules of the Samdech Preah Sangha Reach, but has not yet followed 100 percent.
He said that the gravitational group intended to eliminate the letter N and L.
Soeng Phos, a defender and conservator of Khmer literature, thinks that the gravitational group is not right.
He reminded that the Ministry that issued the national trademark should check the spelling because there are many typos.
Who allowed the label to be written?
Khmer children try to learn English so that they do not speak Khmer, no different from their families and children who learn French language and literature who understand French culture better than Khmer people.
The professor noted that the Chinese, Cham, and Vietnamese, who lived in Cambodian territory, always maintained their identities.
In addition, we go to buy Thai goods in Cambodia, but the consumer goods are all Thai letters, almost no English letters.
The Khmer Rouge used monks to dig canals because they were lazy and destroyed the organization
A recently retired professor of Khmer literature said he was one of 50 former Buddhist monks in Takeo province who were used by the Khmer Rouge to dig canals and were deprived of food by the Khmer Rouge. Insulted as a lazy person who sleeps and eats and deceives people to go to the festival to get food in the rainy season because they are lazy.
Professor Soeng Phos, 71, spent nearly two hours with the Post explaining the history of being a Buddhist monk during the Khmer Rouge regime, as well as his personal life, which he went through countless obstacles since he became a monk. The human resources that society needs.
Monk Soeng Phos studied at Wat Preah Puth in Phnom Penh for three years and passed the entrance exam at Puthik Suramarit High School in front of the Royal Palace.
He is currently studying for a bachelor's degree in Buddhism.
As a priest he also studied at the Faculty of Letters.
He studied until April 17, 1975, when Pol Pot's black-clad army forcibly evicted residents from Phnom Penh to rural areas across the country, including monks, reminding him of the tragic events. That bitterness is unforgettable.
Professor Soeng Phos said monks at all pagodas in the capital were fired upon at 9 a.m. on April 17, 1975.
We went with the monks and nuns at the foot of the pagoda.
We rested one night in case he came back.
The monks and grandparents in the pagoda continued to the west, and some forgot their belongings, turned around a little, but could not go back, all must go forward.
Many of the events he will remember will not be forgotten during his time under Khmer Rouge rule.
When climbing the old Stung Meanchey bridge, a monk dropped the serum next to a hospital bed and another monk helped push it.
I can see it with my own eyes.
He finally returned to his hometown in Phnom Chiso, Takeo province.
We slept three nights on the street.
At the grassroots level, the Khmer Rouge mobilized people to educate and teach memory.
Arrived at the pagoda and in Taing Yap commune, there are three pagodas: Wat Ang Andet, Wat Sleng, Wat Ang Chak.
After arriving in their homeland for a month and a half, the Khmer Rouge gathered a total of about 50 monks from three pagodas to a single pagoda, Wat Phnom Chak, Prey Kabas district, and measured the land to dig a canal.
The land is measured for a group of 12 people at a distance of 12 meters.
As for the monks of my pagoda, they asked me to dig a place about 20 meters, but it was more miserable.
They intended to persecute the monks in Buddhism and eventually to the abolition of religion in their regime.
Mr. Soeng Phos said that the Khmer Rouge considered the monks who evacuated from Phnom Penh to be enemies or that the April 17 priest was an enemy.
Monks evacuated from Phnom Penh have fewer rights than local monks.
He did not see with his own eyes that the monks were being murdered at the time, but apparently Buddhism was being insulted, calling the monks atheists and social outcasts.
After learning of the organization's order that the Khmer Rouge regime had no religion, the laity wept bitterly.
Such a situation requires all monks to build themselves on time.
Under pressure from the Khmer Rouge, the monks almost decided to retaliate.
A week later, he was sent to clear a bridge called Anlong Thmat Bridge in his hometown.
Marriage under the Khmer Rouge regime In late 1975, local Khmer Rouge groups recruited wives for their comrades in the village.
Local people whispered that if they refused to get married, they would be sent to fight as female warriors and would have to go to battle in areas far from their hometowns.
At that time, he agreed to get married.
There is only one bouquet of flowers under the watchful eye of a powerful organization.
The couple must declare their commitment to love each other for life.
Although he loved his wife, he did not like this unusual wedding.
One of the many things that happened to Soeng Phos was the case of the lost plow.
A villager was killed shortly after he plowed the plow.
But he was lucky to have God's help after he lost a plow.
I was finally favored.
For me, I believe God helps.
But one day a child spy called him to the organization while he and his wife were transplanting rice.
The organization is the village chief.
When they met with the village committee, they cut and engraved the letters for the Communist Party festival.
Fortunately, they only use letter engraving.
Later, the organization used him to drive a cart to Koh Kong province to transport charcoal bypassing Kampong Speu province for 7 nights and 7 days, and back for 7 nights and 7 days, crossing many small and large mountains.
His late return prevented the Khmer Rouge from sending him to Battambang, as most other residents died of torture and ill family and malnutrition.
Like anywhere else in the Pol Pot era, malnutrition was one of the leading causes of death.
The 71-year-old professor was once an unfortunate boy.
He told the Post about his tragic history as a poor child, who took refuge with his maternal relatives and paternal relatives until he learned the blessings of a pagoda and Buddhism.
He was born in Kdanh commune today and came to Tang Yap village, Prey Kabas district, Takeo province.
He was born in 1945, the year Japanese troops arrived in Cambodia and drove the French out of Cambodia.
Because the Cambodian health sector at that time was not developed, only healers.
After his mother died, he lived with his father, and then my father left the country because he was too sad.
After my mother died, she adopted me and left me as a baby.
However, after living with her grandmother for three years, the family situation was not good at all because her grandmother's husband stopped talking to the children and almost immediately appeared, which made the grandmother very upset.
At that time, the boy almost started school in 1953 at the age of 8, the first grade, which at that time was called the kindergarten class.
At Wat Phnom Chak, where the children are almost learning, there are only three classes, the first, second and third grades, and the first class he enters is taught by monks, and the second grade is taught by a lazy teacher.
But in the year that he studied for only three months, he began to have a strange fever.
His great-grandmother summoned a monk as a healer, but he did not recover from his illness and finally recovered after praying to Wat Sakdisith and Wat Phnom Chiso.
After fainting for 7 nights and 7 days, he suddenly regained consciousness after being treated with a combination of medicine by Nop, a traditional Khmer teacher in the village.
After recovering from his illness, he was admitted to the primary school in 1959.
After that, he had to take a high school exam in Prey Lvea called Soma High School.
Having sworn that if he recovers from a strange illness, his family will agree to become a Buddhist monk.
He eventually became a Buddhist monk.
We are proud of ourselves.
Thanks to this great religion, Mr. Soeng Phos has the capital of deep literary knowledge to help promote the national literature to the next generation.
Please look forward to reading Part 2 next Friday about the future of Khmer literature.
Tem Mean: Accept constructive criticism of boxing
Lt. Gen. Tem Men is the leader of many major sports activities, including members of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces Sports Council, Executive Members of Volleyball, Athletics, President of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces Sports Center, Deputy Secretary General of the National Olympic Committee of Cambodia and President of the Boxing Federation of Cambodia. .
In doing so much work, he did not think that everything was perfect, instead he was always open to constructive criticism, especially in boxing.
In a live interview with the Post yesterday, which focused on the busy and popular local weekly boxing event, Tem Men was also interested in and supportive of the audience. Like the masses to build and improve the sport of boxing to become more popular and quality, and the decision of the competition is transparent and acceptable to all.
Mr. Tem Men said:
In one week, we have 180 technical officers working as judges, referees and judges and executives working in six arenas on different TV stations that broadcast live and have 15 matches.
At the same time, the boxing federation of Cambodia, which trains nearly 100 clubs across the country, releases more than 150 players a week.
Mr. Tem Men said that in this competition, both technical officers, players and representatives of each club received all the allowances from the program according to ability and division of work and role.
Referees, judges, judges and officials receive between $ 12 and $ 25 per match, depending on the venue and position.
At the same time, any boxing club that trains and sends players to each event will receive $ 15 for the same Cambodian national competition and $ 30 for international bouts.
For players who have the opportunity to compete, the prize money is from 200,000 Riel to 600,000 Riel for the same Cambodian pair, while international competitors receive from $ 250 to $ 500 depending on the level of martial arts and Weight type as well as popularity.
Popular boxing activities, which are also traditional sports, entertain the people and are also an important source of income for the technical officers, players and coaches in the entire Boxing Federation of Cambodia, which we can say. Reduced poverty in part to help society.
He said that the decisions of the judges, arbitrators and the jury were more accurate than absolute, while the mistakes were very small and in the world the same thing happened.
The problem is that I observed that there were times when the players were similar, which is why the five judges were given a unanimous score of 4 to 1 or 3 to 2.
I acknowledge that there are some incompetent and incompetent judges who have committed wrongdoing intentionally or unintentionally, but at the level of punishment, there are first, second, third and dismissal from the commission, but in the past we have not punished Excluded, only at level 1 and 2.
However, Mr. Tem Men claimed that in the sports society, the level of awareness of the players, the officials on the committee are not as highly educated as other institutions, and most of the officials we train are from older players. Retirement, therefore, our management chooses education to the best of its ability to mediate through non-violent means with the greatest favor.
This is in line with national as well as international sports law, there is no violence or criminal punishment.
As for betting money or buying players to pretend to lose during matches in exchange for bribes, Mr. Tem Men stated that this is contrary to national and international sports law and the Royal Government has a ban, but these It always happens on the black market only and also difficult until the authorities take repressive measures.
However, as the director of boxing, he has stated his strong opposition to sports betting, and any official or boxer who commits a misdemeanor has clear evidence that we will abide by the rules.
He added that the federation continues to disseminate the law and ask for criticism from spectators who criticize what kind of points to help well and watch the principle that the federation wants players to compete in all channels in all arenas. There are no restrictions or discrimination, but for the local players, we have only two categories: amateur and semi-professional.
Thus, depending on the agreement between the players and the host, the federation only facilitates the game.
Regarding his personal life, Mr. Tem Men briefly stated that he was born in 1958, was born in Stung Veng village, Stung Veng commune, Mondul Seima district, Koh Kong province, and grew up and studied until 1975.
In the military, he worked and studied through various courses.
In 1992, he was given the position of Director of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces Sports Center to the present, with the role of managing 12 sports in the army and managing other major sports institutions.
Ou Virak: The ruling party is wrong to turn its back on the opposition
Ou Virak, a young political analyst, says the CPP should use its time in power in the fifth term to show political maturity and to show real reform on a number of national issues. It has not yet done so, rather than persecuting the opposition party to the detriment of its interests.
As the ruling party continues to sue opposition leaders, the prominent analyst has said that the lawsuit is only a minor issue, but that the big issue is mistrust of the Cambodian district court system. .
"If the tribunal is independent, despite the lawsuits, there is less concern because we know that the courts will make judgments based on the content and will of the law, the content and the will of the people," he said. Constitution.
Hi, if so, we're not worried anymore!
An independent tribunal trusted by all parties and all citizens is the key to social stability, because when there is a conflict, they do not have to worry, they run for a legal settlement mechanism, a court they can trust.
Opposition leaders Sam Rainsy, Kem Sokha and two other lawmakers, as well as more than a dozen opposition activists, are currently facing a series of lawsuits, some of which have already been convicted. Many consider these lawsuits to be political.
In Cambodia, the main national institutions that are independent, we do not trust it, which is a lot of concern.
In fact, the case against the opposition is not serious if the judiciary is independent.
But it became a serious case because the judiciary is not independent and this judiciary is not independent, others who have not yet filed a lawsuit are also afraid, worried, afraid to stop, dare to speak, dare to compete, at that time It affects the whole democratic process, and in general, such an impact will cause some powerful people to lose their horses.
Ruling party officials have claimed that there are no political prisoners in Cambodia, only politicians convicted of crimes, and that the lawsuit is a law enforcement exercise to strengthen the rule of law.
But for analysts like Ou Virak, the series of lawsuits against opposition leaders is not about strengthening the rule of law.
He said that if they really want to strengthen the rule of law, they need to change three laws related to the judiciary to make the judiciary strong and independent.
One opposition lawmaker, one senator and more than 10 opposition activists, as well as four Adhoc human rights officials and one NEC official, are currently being held in jail on various charges, which are national and international. Many see it as an accusation under the influence of politics that is the source of political heat.
Mr. Ou Virak, as well as many national and international circles, wants the politicians of the two elected parties to meet to resolve the political crisis that is heating up to create a good atmosphere before the 2017 election and 2018.
Mr. Virak said the two parties should take a step back and meet to resolve any differences.
So there is no recipe other than competition, competition is in our interest as a waiter, as we see two coffee stalls competing with each other, we know that we have delicious and cheap coffee, the service is smiling and because of who They are rude, we stopped coming in and it was not like that!
We have a choice, if there is no choice, it is difficult, so the competition of the people is the winner, we want them to compete, it is an important key.
The young man, who received a master's degree in economics from the United States in 2003, said:
Mr. Ou Virak expressed regret that Cambodian politicians did not tolerate politics, thinking only of using violent power and using violent words in all forms for the sake of their own popularity, which would eventually do. Let Cambodia fail because Cambodians are angry with each other.
Mr. Virak wants politicians to do politics so that the people are the winners, the nation is the winners, which means that the politicians are the runners and the people are the ones who choose.
Mr. Virak said that winning the election of any party means that it is an obligatory opportunity that people give to that party to serve the nation.
While opposition leaders and officials are facing a number of lawsuits in court, the ruling party has turned its back on the opposition, blocking the way for negotiations, which Ou Virak believes is wrong.
Because in the end, no one judges him more than the people, they are watching, so I look at both sides, national loss, loss is not the CPP gain.
If the CPP talks to the opposition to keep calm while he is in power, he takes that calm so that he can prepare for reforms to gain the trust of the people that he is willing, he knows the problem. With an idea to solve the problem, the people will vote for him again.
Thus, he can take the opportunity to quietly strengthen the government to strengthen itself.
His opposition party should take a quiet opportunity to strengthen itself.
What do people want from the opposition party?
The people want the opposition to be a party that he trusts to have better people, better ideas to reform, solve the problems that people are facing.
People do not want the opposition to sit and wait to attack them, they want the opposition to show that they are ready.
The former president of the Cambodian Center for Human Rights has stated that if he were the CPP, he would not turn his back on the CNRP, but instead would comfort the opposition so that the ruling party would have the opportunity to organize people to come up with new ideas. Make real reforms of what the people want to see.
If prepared, I look like a good competition.
However, the ruling and opposition parties are unlikely to embrace the idea of democratic competition that promotes national development, as Ou Virak suggested, as many see party leaders. The ruling party and the leaders of the opposition always think of their own interests and the interests of the party more than the national interest.
The assassination of Kem Ley makes Ou Virak more analytical
Ou Virak, the newly elected president of the United States, expressed disappointment and surprise at the victory of Donald Trump from the Republican Party.
In an interview with the Post on November 10, the former president of the Cambodian Center for Human Rights said he was concerned that what Tram had said in his political message was that it would affect the issue. Some human rights, especially what he said about the non-use of international mechanisms, is a basis that could lead to more instability in the world.
It could also affect the economic situation and garment workers in Cambodia if Tram does what he says, closing the US market or protecting the US market while a Cambodian garment Parts are shipped to the US market.
So the democratic process, the war, the strife, not the transfer of power is not a challenge in the United States, what is the challenge is the opinion of the elected person and how he will lead the country?
For migrants such as Ou Virak, ballots are sent home and voters can cast their ballots ahead of schedule and return to the polling station, where ballots are counted. .
As for the election process in Cambodia, Ou Virak, considered by many to be an independent analyst, sees the election process in Cambodia, although poor, as not a source of major problems.
But the big problem is the people's feelings, freedom, trust in the security of the people and the security of the nation.
In their homeland, politics, no one worries about imprisonment, street shootings, lawsuits, that's why politics in the United States is considered as fun, even if they argue. Criticizing each other for each other's tips, however, is just a matter of differences of opinion, not the differences of the adversaries who take each other as enemies, that is an important key.
According to Ou Virak, politics and propaganda in the United States have many bad tips, such as individual attacks, some racist attacks, insults and quarrels, and criticism. Really serious.
In Cambodia, while national institutions are not yet strong and independent, Ou Virak wants Cambodian politicians to be more mature than before. With the idea of solving national problems is just one key.
The 36-year-old analyst also said that Cambodian politicians should find a way to unite the nation and compete with each other.
However, these are only secondary issues, and the most important thing is that independent democratic institutions mean an independent constitutional council, an independent NEC, an independent military, an independent police, an independent judiciary.
If the transition from one government to another is smooth, it will make development a gradual but steady line, and no ups and downs will affect the development process. No.
Ou Virak was born on September 15, 1975, the year the Khmer Rouge killed his father.
Virak left his hometown with his mother and all his siblings in Battambang province to live in a camp on the Thai-Cambodian border in 1985, when he was eight years old.
He and his family were granted refugee status in the United States in 1989, when he was 12 years old.
Two of his brothers are currently living in Cambodia, one is living in the United States.
His elderly mother returned to live with her 94-year-old grandmother in her hometown in Battambang province.
But the young man is the youngest candidate to pass the state-level self-help program in the United States since 1993, when he was 18 years old.
Mr. Virak's job at the time was to teach those who could not help themselves how to find work and solve problems on their own.
However, Virak was not excited enough, not interested enough in that job and civilized life in the United States, because part of his heart was in Cambodia.
On his second visit to Cambodia, a year after graduating in economics and politics from the United States, Virak was interested in living a pilot life in Cambodia, beginning as a professor of economics at a university. Intellectual.
In addition to teaching at Pannasastra University, Virak also began to learn more about Cambodian society after reading several documents related to Cambodia while he was studying.
At the same time, Mr. Virak joined the Cambodian Center for Human Rights, which was then led by Mr. Kem Sokha, now the acting president of the Cambodia National Rescue Party, to gain work experience and to contribute to society.
However, Virak eventually became president of the Cambodian Center for Human Rights in 2007, after Kem Sokha resigned and formed a political party.
At the time, the Center for Human Rights' largest aid was cut off.
Mr. Virak resigned as president of the Cambodian Center for Human Rights in 2013, explaining that his departure was due to the fact that in 2006-2007 he intended to open a public policy research center because he had a strong interest in the subject. Than.
Vireak's successor in leading the Cambodian Center for Human Rights is Chak Sopheap, a young woman.
Virak is a political analyst who is considered by many to be an independent analyst who dares to speak the truth about the issues that plague Cambodian society.
But the main work he is doing as chairman of Future Forum, an official listed company, is to write analyzes to major institutions on political stability to advise individuals such as civil society, diplomats and partners. To advise them to understand the political process and political stability, as well as the progress of Cambodia.
Future Forum Company has accepted some funding but generally receives fees from consulting on a number of issues.
Of course, Ou Virak, who was recently sued by the CPP for defamation, did not want to do much analysis, but he wanted to teach a lot and write a lot, and that is why he set up the company. A forum for the future to gradually withdraw from analysis and human rights work, which often faces many challenges.
However, when Dr. Kem Ley, a well-known analyst, was shot dead, Virak had no choice but to come back and do more analysis.
Actually, as a strategy, I try to reduce speech, I want to sit down to write, sit down to teach, but with the loss of Kem Ley, I think it is necessary for me to continue to speak for our society ....
For Mr. Virak, he will not have to change his position in speaking, because in the past, the supporters of the opposition party have insulted me, the supporters of the ruling party have insulted me, the ruling party has sued me. Thus, for me, I continue to hold my position, I take national unity as a whole, I see wrong, I am wrong on both sides and I am not biased.
He said he did not love or hate any individual or party, but he loved the nation.
Ou Virak married a Cambodian woman in Cambodia in 2012 and now has a one-and-a-half-year-old son.
Stay tuned for next week's episode in which Ou Virak talks about the heating up political situation in Cambodia and the ruling party continues to turn its back on the opposition.
