The expert saw the shortcomings and expressed the view that more traditional sports culture should be added, and the competition techniques between athletes from the provinces and the capital or the competent ministries are too far apart, which leads to There is no transparency and the prize money to encourage the winners of this event is a hot issue that the Royal Government should reconsider in line with the will to improve the lives of athletes, that is, better sports life.
However, Mr. Rath Sandab praised the two points of Prime Minister Hun Sen, who said at the closing ceremony:
Every citizen should play a sport and a company or institution should partner with a national sports federation to grow.
Mr. Rath Sandab said that in his view, the opening and closing of the program were acceptable at international standards, but the performances of the traditional arts and culture were few and far between, as contemporary music seemed to do more. To be attractive, as opposed to regional sports content that focuses on traditional sports.
At the same time, the problem of showing symbols during the game that confirms the meaning is not seen at all, which is a point that should be studied and supplemented.
In addition, Mr. Rath Sandab suggested that for the next game or in the SEA Games, which Cambodia is hosting, if there are not many traditional sports competitions, there should be a program to show Lots of swords, long sticks, short sticks, krama tricks, jigsaw puzzles.
All are rich traditions of our nation.
He stated that according to my research and understanding, the sport that Cambodia hosts is obliged to include more traditional sports because as a regional sport, it is possible to express the culture of the host as much as possible. Continental and Olympic Games.
According to Mr. Rath's overall assessment:
In an interview with the Post yesterday, Rath Sandab explained the origins of Khmer traditional martial arts:
Khmer martial arts have a clear name and meaning.
He added that another clear basis of Khmer martial arts has three main content: first, the worship of teachers or reminiscent of teachers, second, the magic of the Pali silk, and third, the concert, all three of which can not be lacking. And now there are still effective activities.
He said that today, boxing matches in private arenas, although not fully respected by the law, still worship this teacher on a regular basis and have not used all that kind of worship.
He added that Khmer martial arts originally did not have one or more levels of white, black, yellow and red belts, they were marked by high and low levels, and did not compete in entertainment as they do now.
In ancient times, martial arts were only used to fight in wars.
Answering the question that the Ministry of National Defense put three types of martial arts, bokator, boxing and martial arts, how can you explain in your expertise?
Mr. Rath Sandab explained:
It is very good that the last option of our Ministry of National Defense as well as the Royal Government to sign an agreement to accept the three types of martial arts as the martial arts of the army: first, there is Cambodian boxing, second, bokator and third martial arts. Because the martial arts of each nation, the countries of the world do the same, just as China in the army must practice kung fu in Korea, each soldier is limited to two black taekwondo.
He further explained that in accordance with the rule of law, the agreement between the three sports federations should be a single national unity or a national sports federation in the same army because of boxing, bokator, martial arts. It belongs to only one Khmer, find a unique name.
He explained that according to Thai history, he created a Thai boxing name as a copy of Khmer martial arts from the time of Banteay Longvek, which we broke up, and some Siamese scholars took over, and their country is more peaceful than we are today. We know that progress is the slowest, and really, we have to unite within the nation before we can put it into international recognition.
According to UNESCO definitions and concepts, any martial art is man-made, proving its origins, that it is man-made, not divine, and that it is popular and well-received by local people. More than 80% familiar.
Related to the personal story of Mr. Rath Sandab, who is known to some in the general public, he is an expert researcher on culture, art and sports, and also the author of the book of the flag of the Kingdom of Cambodia, the song of the Kingdom of Cambodia, the national anthem, the source of ancient Khmer boxing. And other national cultures seem to have some secrets about private life that have not been revealed.
By asking him many questions on this issue, he revealed a little:
He was born in Phnom Penh in 1973 to a family with a father who was a civil servant and teacher.
In 1975, he was evacuated to live in Svay Rieng Thom province.
After 1979, he was educated and went through a difficult life, not unlike other children and young people, selling cakes and selling lottery tickets to earn money to study. Term.
In 1998, after the election of the second mandate, Mr. Rath Sandab was appointed Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Environment, and in the third mandate he was appointed as the Tourism Authority with the same status as the Secretary of State. Cabinet While his private life prefers to live quietly in a rented room alone, researching culture, art and sports.
According to him, he is currently writing another book related to the source of Khmer martial arts, which is scheduled to be published in early 2017 with the support of Vath Chamroeun, Secretary General of the Olympic Committee of Cambodia and Mr. Tem Men. Director of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces Sports Center and President of the Boxing Federation of Cambodia.
Seng Bunsong: Practicing martial arts at the highest level should understand the four elements: land, water, fire, air
Mr. Seng Bunsong, who is recognized as a boxer with a lot of experience through martial arts since childhood, was one of the great boxers in the 1980s on the Cambodian-Thai border and is now a professor of mixed martial arts and sports. Hapkido 9 Dan in New York, USA, mentioned a number of factors related to the development of sports, especially top-level martial arts.
The martial artist, who studied deeply until he became calm and not cruel, claimed:
With the development of himself through the study of Buddhist martial arts and Dharma, Seng Bunsong not only never used martial arts unethically or unjustly, but he always adhered to Embrace the Dharma of Salvation by following the path of meekness, courtesy, and respect for the common people.
Regarding his success and experience in the field of health promotion, he shared some experiences with the next generation through the Phnom Penh Post:
The martial arts professor explained:
He said that the understanding of the body, which is composed of the above elements, is called the understanding of the body, health and martial arts is the same as the theory of the Buddha, which is a broad concept for humanity in virtue. That also includes meditation, which is one of the foundations of sports.
In addition to being a martial arts professor, Seng Bunsong, now 66, is also an expert in nine black belt Hapkido martial arts in the United States. Kim Sean, a senior student and another martial arts researcher in Cambodia with 10 black Hapkido belts, also has similar achievements in sports research.
Mr. Seng Bunsong was born in Russey Chroy village, Prek Dambang commune, Muk Kampoul district, Kandal province. He has been practicing martial arts since he was a child.
He said that after 1979 he fled to a camp near Thailand and practiced a variety of sports, including aerobics, judo, taekwondo and boxing, competing for a living in Thailand.
From 1980 to 1984, he practiced boxing with some of Thailand's top fighters, but his first bout was with the same Cambodian boxer of his career, defeating his opponent, Sim Maithy, a Simak coach.
Due to his talent, the international MMA team selected him to compete in Malaysia, the Philippines, Hong Kong, Southeast Asia, especially non-communist countries and many other countries before being granted residency in the United States.
After being granted residency in the United States, Seng Bunsong married the same Cambodian wife there and had three children, including a son.
Due to his martial arts skills, living conditions in the United States, he was invited by many Hapkido martial arts schools to attend martial arts training and attend Hapkido seminars as a Green Master Master and subsequently became an outstanding martial arts professor. One with wide recognition and many students.
In 2001, wanting to promote sports, he spent his own money to open a workshop in Cambodia at the Bird Club in the city, chaired by Nam Ravuth.
Then, in October 2016, he recently re-opened a workshop in Battambang and the Olympic Stadium in Phnom Penh with the encouragement of Mr. Vath Chamroeun and his colleagues.
He outlined the future vision of sports that important people in some sports, both old and young, should unite to help the national sport grow.
He also recalled that in 2001, his best student was Mr. Vath Chamroeun, but now he has become an outstanding leader in sports.
Along with sharing this brief experience, Mr. Seng Bunsong also praised many outstanding people, such as Master Krama Meas San Kim Sean, who struggled to dedicate time to compiling bokator for revival. Khmer martial arts in the world.
Mr. Seng Bunsong's greatness is that his martial arts skills led him to build a beautiful life in the United States, and he still researches martial arts and documents to contribute to the nation when he retires.
Nowadays, modern science, while martial arts must also adapt to human society.
He stressed that even the modern International Olympic Games focus on education and skills training, not overcoming and leading to accidents, so the Olympics have reduced the number of events that lead to Dangers and obstacles.
Mr. Vath Chamroeun, a sports law expert who is currently the Secretary General of the National Olympic Committee of Cambodia, supports and wants human resources like Mr. Seng Bunsong to help the national sports and in his opinion will call for some veteran sports elders to comment for Progress in sports.
If you want to get rid of violence, you must get rid of mental illness first
All kinds of violence, both in war, in society and in the family, will bring people in that country to a serious mental illness, especially to women and children, and the leaders of that country should give priority. Mainly in finding ways to prevent and treat patients with mental illness, psychosis and anxiety.
This is according to Dr. Professor Kar Sunbonat, a psychiatrist and director of the National Mental Health Program.
Violent acts of abuse and death threats, drug and alcohol abuse of all kinds, which have been widely reported in the media, may be one of the many causes of mental illness.
As a result, when drunk and using drugs, they commit crimes.
Violence against pregnant women is something to avoid because it affects the health of both mother and unborn child.
Dr. Kar Sunbonat explained that pregnant women who are subjected to violence by their husbands and society, in addition to physical harm, will also suffer from anxiety, trauma, and depression, which manifest as symptoms of inattention. It is as if you do not eat, sleep or sleep, which will lead to the deteriorating physical health of the pregnant woman.
This is the impact on the future of the fetus.
Violent families often lose the warmth of their children, lack of care and adequate nutrition, it makes the baby suffer like anxiety, depression, trauma like their mother.
Please note that mental illness is not a reproductive disease.
People can avoid mental illness by maintaining a good memory, self-control, can control anger.
People who do not have violent mechanisms to control their anger and emotions will inevitably suffer from mental illness.
"While Cambodia has an unusually large number of psychiatric patients, the number of doctors and physicians is very small, disproportionate to the number of patients in need of medical services, which is 30 to 40 percent of the total," said Dr. Kar Sunbonat. Total population of more than 15 million.
There are 135 basic mental health service trainers to become basic mental health nurses nationwide.
The professor of psychology stated that human resources in psychology, especially psychiatrists, should be given priority for health services in Cambodia.
Such human resources should be in the minds of the government at the national level.
Another challenge is the lack of assistance and the lack of students with a passion for studying this important profession.
According to a document, the amount injected into mental illness accounts for only 0.02 percent of the total amount of the Ministry of Health's annual budget approved by parliament.
But he defended the health ministry's decision to spend the national budget to prevent and treat other priority diseases, such as AIDS and diabetes, which needed to be addressed.
So far, we must not think that the government does not care, we must think that the possibilities are limited.
Treatment The professor of psychiatry stated that the treatment of physical illness is different from the treatment of mental illness, because physical illness requires all the inquiries, we conclude that any disease should be treated only mental illness. The mind is not like that.
Mental illness, when asked, is the beginning of treatment.
An effective psychology for patients is to show a fresh face, gestures and use a soft voice in accordance with the patient's needs.
Just doing so sometimes relieves patients up to 70 percent.
There is no special tool for diagnosing and treating mental illnesses other than giving them strength and hope.
Sometimes mental illness is the cause of physical illness, sometimes physical illness is the cause of mental illness, and sometimes it happens all at once.
Only the psychiatrist can see.
The cure is to understand the suffering of the patient in conjunction with the compassionate heart, Mutita, the mantra of the Buddha.
He shows that wisdom brings happiness, ignorance brings sorrow.
Healing mental illness through the Buddha Mental illness, if analyzed according to the Dharma of the Buddha, is from a misunderstanding.
Misunderstandings can lead to mental illness.
Finding the cause of anxiety The mind is full of sadness If the monk can make the patient stop worrying and return with joy, then the illness is actually relieved emotionally.
The doctor praised the monks who performed sermons to educate the people.
He takes non-violent dharma like Mr. But Savong, educates people only on morality, do not respond with violence.
Buddhism and the monks have played a very important role in helping to share the psychological crisis with the people.
Therefore, it should be known that if you feel irritable, you should go to the doctor for help, have peace of mind, understand the truth, be able to understand each other, and Cambodians will be happy.
There are so many resources in Buddhism that many do not understand and simply make the sick in this country easy to understand.
He tried to treat many other French and foreign patients who the embassy came to see and found that they also accepted Buddhist treatment.
Once they have regained some of their skills, we start with a variety of forms of psychotherapy, especially CBT, which is easy for patients to accept in dealing with problems and other situations, such as patient status, family, society, so that Patients and others adapted, creating a cool environment.
In today's newspapers, it is observed that young men and women with love crisis have decided to commit suicide in many cases.
According to Sun Bonat, love crisis for young people has a deep will and dares to do what they want if they are upset or fail at something.
It is generally thought that self-worth is no longer human, it is better to die to avoid embarrassment, especially for girls who have lost their virginity.
He told how to save young people in love crisis by observing the face.
In this case, siblings or parents need to comfort them to tell the truth, give strength and provide solutions to make them hope and happy, then the family should refer to a psychiatrist.
In the case of Cambodia, it went through the Khmer Rouge regime.
The murderer and the person threatened with death all have the same mental crisis, which requires all members of society to understand each other by using the dharma to forgive the perpetrators.
Dr. Kar Sunbonat urges former Khmer Rouge cadres and war victims to seek treatment for mental illness and anxiety so that they can live as peacefully as the general public.
With the support of Norway and the World Health Organization (WHO), as well as the support of the Ministry of Health, psychiatric services have been expanded to all provincial hospitals, with at least one psychiatrist at each provincial referral hospital.
Since the 1995 academic year, the Ministry of Health has integrated a mental health curriculum for students from the fourth to the sixth year.
In order to motivate, prevent and treat not only money but also effort and try to control the mind and emotions well.
Send the last message to young people to see themselves where they are, what to do for their own future, if you love your country, what to do, do not be fooled by their needs and desires to see the way to the future.
In addition, preventing the recurrence of violence is to prevent the occurrence of mental illness in Cambodia.
Mental illness is spreading from old to young
There are dozens of diseases in the world that doctors are aware of and know the symptoms that occur in the human body and can be diagnosed medically.
But the disease that doctors operate on and do not find in the human body and is difficult to treat is mental illness.
Psychosis affects more than 80 percent of all Cambodians living after more than three decades of war.
These three types of mental illness, schizophrenia and anxiety are also affecting the elderly, adolescents and children.
Cases of mental illness can also have a serious impact on economic, political and social development.
This is a warning message from Dr. Kar Sunbonat, a well-known Cambodian professor and psychiatrist today.
Last week, the doctor discussed with the Post the Who's Who program in detail about his background and work on this dangerous mental illness.
According to the professor, mental illness is caused by a variety of factors.
In Cambodia, there are four main factors that cause people to have mental illness:
First, biological factors, including hereditary factors, abnormalities of the body, such as people with gonorrhea can cause depression or kidney damage can also lead to dementia.
Psychological factors:
People are born to understand themselves differently, some think they are lucky, some are pessimistic and some are optimistic.
Such self-anxiety causes that anxiety, which in turn leads to mental illness.
There are two types of factors: social factors caused by humans and natural factors.
Natural social factors are less common, such as hurricanes, lightning, volcanic eruptions, tsunamis, or wildfires.
People near the war zone are the longest victims of mental illness, psychosis and anxiety.
The insiders, who were not harmed but were killed in the fighting, also suffered from the same disease.
These instability is a psychological trauma to the people and the army on all sides, because it is the beginning of life with fear, hardship, lack of face, lack of back.
The professor explained that the post-war situation, although it was gone, had a devastating effect on the younger generation.
Even the rapid development of society has a detrimental effect because older people have not yet developed the understanding between children and parents.
That's why a group of teenagers, especially 11 to 20 years old, escaped from home and formed a new society that is attacked by dangerous drugs.
Another unfortunate thing for the Cambodian youth is the huge influx of drugs in a form that is almost impossible to suppress.
Drug-addicted youth are a major factor in undermining human and economic development in Cambodia.
The influx of young people who are obsessed with their parents in the new society has gathered and is being lured by drug dealers to this day, growing all over the country.
Profile and Specialization of Dr. Kar Sunbonat Professor Kar Sunbonat is the fifth of seven children in a wealthy family whose father is an education official in Pursat province.
His mother is a housewife.
He is the only child in the family who is curious and always on the lookout for answers to this dangerous disease.
He recalled that in 1970, when he was 17 years old, the whole of Cambodia was at war with the government forces and the Khmer Rouge.
The sound of small and large guns of all kinds was heard in the cities and in the countryside, which frightened almost everyone.
When my mother heard the sound of gunfire, my mother was terrified.
From 1972 to 1975, before the Khmer Rouge came to power, wanting to understand the mental state of people with mental illness, Sun Bonat continued his high school and medical school studies with a strong focus on psychological work. On the behavior of people with symptoms different from normal people.
When they pour rice to eat, they compete like animals, unlike humans, which makes me wonder why humans become like animals, making me try to understand the psychological by direct observation.
Please note that during 1974, Phnom Penh was under siege and no teacher or professor could explain mental illness.
In Phnom Penh, refugees were seen fleeing the countryside to Phnom Penh in the same panic as his mother.
On January 1, 1975, Phnom Penh was cut off and the Khmer Rouge fired 120 rockets from the Arey Ksat area east of the Mekong River into Phnom Penh.
At that time, everyone in Phnom Penh panicked.
People are so frightened that they think the sound of a motorcycle is the sound of a rocket.
After seeing such an event, he became more aware that what is a disaster causes fear, makes a person's memory become abnormal, loses control and can not find the truth, and often Understand the truth, small things to big things.
Later, the Khmer Rouge liberated the Khmer Rouge and attacked Phnom Penh.
At that time, there was violence, shootings, small or large gunshots fired at people, including myself.
I saw with my own eyes the tumultuous people of Phnom Penh, panicking, losing control and not knowing what to do.
Under the Pol Pot regime, torture was known to be so severe that people were scared, and some people spoke irresponsibly because they were too scared.
After the day of liberation on January 7, 1979, when the National Liberation Front, in cooperation with the Vietnamese Volunteer Army, came to liberate the Khmer Rouge from massacre, the Cambodian people were terrified again because they thought they were in a dangerous situation. One day.
Especially when seeing the presence of Vietnamese troops.
In the early 1980s, he noted that almost all Cambodians were traumatized, feared, mistrusted, and resentful, a major problem in national reconciliation.
Kar Sunbonat did not have the opportunity to study the subject abroad.
Because this issue is important to society, he tried to understand this problem and also understand the three types of diseases, including depression, schizophrenia and anxiety.
The behavior of schizophrenia is a behavior that is different from others, can not understand, and he is always surprised the wrong way, there is nothing, he is scared, or he accuses them of gossiping, they want to hurt him or not. No, he said someone ordered him.
Stay tuned to read about how to prevent and treat mental illness in Part 2 next Friday.
Monk Po Putao Hav: Vipassana as a way to find happiness in the mind
With only three years left, Venerable Po Putong Hao will be one century old, while he is now 97 years old.
Maintaining a long life of almost a century is a wonderful merit.
He is the living library of the next generation because this monk has gone through almost every generation of political regime in Cambodia, from the French colonial period to the present Techo era.
Each person's life stage has different journeys and backgrounds according to different times and environments.
But the history and mission of the life of Venerable Po Tong Haw is similar to some ordinary people and different from some of you.
However, in a recent interview with the Phnom Penh Post at Pavilion 13, Wat Ounalom, Phnom Penh, he gave a brief account of his background, saying that he was born in 1919 in Koh Thom district, Kandal province. .
Due to the difficult life of the farmer to earn money to study, he decided to study at many pagodas, such as Wat Pichey Sakor, Wat Teuk Vil in his hometown, and continued to take the pagoda of Preah Puth Mean Bon, Wat Lanka as a place to live. To study Pali as a priest.
Later, thinking that studying Pali was difficult to find a job, he retired from Preah Phnous to study French.
However, he decided to oppose the belief of locals who believe that to study or do business, only if there is a race or a Chinese hybrid, or if working in the civil service, there must also be a breed.
He has nine siblings and is the eldest child in the family.
Overcoming all obstacles in learning Finally, the life of a person who strives to learn really succeeds as desired.
In Phnom Penh, Putong Hav had the opportunity to attend Preah Sisowath High School, the first high school in Cambodia.
As an adult, he married in 1951 in Kratie province.
His father-in-law is the governor of Chhlong district, Kratie province.
In his early career, Chea Balat, deputy district governor of Kampot province, was transferred to the Siem Reap provincial police commissioner and director of the Siem Reap provincial prison.
Later, he moved from there to be the governor of Chhlong district, Kratie province.
Five years later, he moved to Phnom Penh to work at City Hall.
During the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era of the former King Father Norodom Sihanouk, he was appointed governor of Svay Rieng and Kampong Thom provinces, with authority over Battambang and Pursat and Pailin provinces.
Life as a civil servant at that time was a happy life with a decent salary and dignity.
Unfortunately, on March 18, 1970, General Lon Nol staged a coup to overthrow President Sihon.
Unfortunately, only six children were destined to leave Cambodia, while his wife and four sons and daughters did not leave, resulting in the tragic deaths of the Khmer Rouge in Kampong Cham. .
This is the main reason why the former governor of the old society gave up his aristocratic life to a Buddhist monk, sacrificing both physical and mental strength and wealth for the second time in India in order to thank his parents and the pagoda. All the monasteries and monks who sacrificed for him when he was a child.
Every day he has six children, four in the United States and two in Phnom Penh.
Out of grief over the loss of his beloved wife and children, he decided to become a monk in India under the Bodhisattva tree, where the Buddha was enlightened.
Buddhism is the number one good religion in the world if all Buddhists worship it properly.
It has been noted that most Buddhists in Cambodia memorize the Buddha's teachings but do not practice them.
Learn only theory but no practice.
Buddhism, if only you do not practice in vain, I know this because I learned Vipassana with a teacher.
If the understanding of Venerable Po Putao Hav Vipassana is the only science that can bring people who are worried to peace of mind, and this Vipassana makes people pure in heart and afraid of all kinds of sins.
The Venerable told the Post that the biggest goal in my life was to help the Cambodian people to participate in Vipassana for my grandfather, the meaning of life is to do what is sad and that is the most important thing.
Those who practice Vipassana will become new people with a good heart.
There is no money to grieve.
I am open every day, if anyone comes to do Vipassana, if I give money.
I wish to do it, because doing this is very good.
He explained that the nature of the poor always attracts only the poor, the nature of the ignorant will only attract the same ignorant people.
The virtuous, having built up good deeds, always meet the same good people who seek peace by not committing various sins.
According to the explanation of the Vipassana Dharma quoted from the book, Ms. Say Heang, the son-in-law of the monk Po Putao Haw, wrote, quoting the history of the monk's Vipassana, that in 1980 he entered the Vipassana meditation with the teacher. Goenka, an Indian twice in California, USA.
In 1981, he went to India to meditate with Guru Inga again.
Having a clear understanding of the Dharma, he decided to become a monk in 1983 in order to repay his mother, even though he was 62 years old.
He venerated under the Po tree, the site of the Buddha's Enlightenment Dharma in Buddhism in India.
That is why he was named Bhikkhu Po.
In 1992, Putonghao was appointed as a Vipassana teacher in Cambodia.
At the end of 1992, the monk Po Putao Hav opened the first Vipassana course at Wat Svay Pophe.
Later, he bought land in his hometown to build a Vipassana Center in Koh Thom District, Kandal Province, which was named the Cambodia International Vipassana Center.
This Dharma and Vipassana Dharma, regardless of race, caste, creed or status in any society, they can all study all the Dharma and Vipassana methods in order to be the fuel of lust, defilement, and impurity. Fear, anger, etc.
The 10-day Vipassana course is taught by a Vipassana instructor and requires students to meditate, concentrate, and eventually acquire wisdom by thinking correctly and eliminating all suffering. In my heart for a long time.
The goal of Vipassana is to find peace of mind, to enjoy doing good deeds that benefit both humans and animals.
Dr. Heng Vanda: Cambodia's education sector needs to inject more money
Higher education experts have urged the government to invest more in primary, secondary and tertiary education to strengthen the country's human resources in the globalization phase.
He said reforming primary, secondary and tertiary education at the same time was inevitable for the future of Cambodia's human resources.
He said that in order to facilitate reforms in higher education, students should be oriented from primary and secondary education first, because at the Ministry of Higher Education, there are three factors that need to be assessed: First, the selection of students Attend school.
Second, evaluate while teaching and third, measure students' ability when they graduate.
Strengthening the primary education sector will facilitate higher education by 50 to 60 percent.
He explained that students in higher education abroad are more self-employed than teaching from professors, which is different from the higher education environment in Cambodia, where students are forced to choose from E to study. .
The professor, who specializes in accounting, banking, auditing and taxation, said students' education at the primary and secondary levels was strongly linked to higher education.
In Cambodia, children in public schools are often criticized for being poorer than private schools in terms of school facilities, teaching ability, and students' learning ability.
Therefore, Cambodia's primary and secondary education needs to be cash-in, but cash-in is related to the national budget, because the national budget has to weigh what his government has in mind.
Dr. Heng Vanda entered the higher education sector in 2000 with the Vanda Institute of Accounting, located on Mao Tse Tung Boulevard in Phnom Penh.
From 2003-2004, he started to produce thousands of human resources with accounting skills, and now at least 80 percent of the accountants in public and private institutions are his alumni, while the entire education sector It has been widely criticized that public school corruption is ineffective in training human resources.
But from 2013-2016, within the framework of education reform under the leadership of the Minister of Education, Youth and Sports, Dr. Hang Chuon Naron, this sector seems to have changed its face and received praise from the public and the head of state. The government, too.
Professor Heng Vanda said:
The founder of Vanda Institute of Accounting acknowledges that the education sector in Cambodia before the 2013 reforms seemed to be experiencing a storm of strong criticism from parents, students, the media and organizations regarding the quality of schools, students' learning and Teacher's teaching.
Public opinion alleges that first. Teachers, students do not know what the market requires.
Second. Student inflation and third. Poor quality students.
He explained that Cambodian students could not be compared to Thai or Singaporean students because tuition fees at Cambodian colleges were 10 times lower than in Thailand and tuition fees at Cambodian colleges were 25 times cheaper than in Singapore.
However, Heng Vanda praised the education minister's reform strategy as fruitful and proud.
The standard scale has nine sections and 72 indicators.
The positive impact of higher education reform in the three years 2013-2016 under the leadership of the Minister of Education, Dr. Hang Chuon Naron, showed that the number of students who passed the A-level exam 11 In 2013, the number increased to 405 in 2016.
In addition, it is seen that students in middle school and primary school also work very hard, fearing that they will not pass the exam.
Heng Vanda called the minister a non-corrupt leader, a key factor in the effectiveness of education reform.
This minister makes the work highly efficient and professional.
He has a bright smile, which makes us think that his reforms from 2013 until now have had positive results, especially the bankruptcy exam.
Changing all the behavior of students and parents by stopping all bribes and taking bribes.
All I know is that Minister Hang Chuon Naron is a non-corrupt minister and I will not say anything else.
The previous ministers are also good at following the same national views, but Minister Hang Chuon Naron has one feature that is not corrupt.
The director of the institute, who has more than 20 years of experience in the field of education, said that some private schools have hired teachers from public schools to teach, which means that public schools have been strengthened in both discipline and ethics. Believe that if the next five years the government injects more cash, then there is no need to inject cash into higher education.
The idea of injecting cash into the education sector should be in line with the education philosophy of the Royal Government of Education for all of us to educate and how to make all Cambodians learn for life, education for life.
Mr. Vanda said:
Although the private sector has been actively contributing to the training of human resources, the possibility of expanding schools or hiring good professors is still limited.
He says one of the best ways Thailand has done is to avoid taxes from private schools and allow schools to use student salaries to pay for qualified teachers and school facilities. Of education.
Dr. Heng Vanda says that the collection of various taxes from the school business is a burden that makes it impossible for schools, institutes or universities to grow.
Therefore, schools are forced to increase tuition fees for students, most of whom are poor from the provinces.
The more you expand, the better the education sector, the stronger the students.
Biography of Dr. Heng Vanda Heng Vanda was born into a poor family in Koh Thom district, Kandal province, in a very poor family situation.
Living a very difficult life, he decided to leave his home in Phnom Penh to study, taking refuge in Wat Mohamandrey in 1968 during the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era under the leadership of Prince Norodom Sihanouk.
The study and living of the monks continued until the Khmer Rouge seized power from Lon Nol, the leader of the Khmer Republic on April 17, 1975.
Life in the pagoda is not enough, but he has already decided that he must use his knowledge to build a life of poverty alleviation, no matter how difficult the family life is, he has to keep his eyes and ears closed. Because if you still do not give up, you can not liberate poverty.
He recalled that while studying his daily food, Trakoun Boeung Tompun ate with the leftover rice from the monks.
However, the students in the presence of Samnang ate occasional delicacies until the funeral of Lon Nol soldiers who died in battle and required them to be brought to the pagoda for worship and cremation.
The last message is to share with students, do not give up on poverty.
Overcome Obstacles No obstacle can get us on our knees.
Lack of philosophy leads to violence and war
Last week, Heng Sreang, Professor of Philosophy at Pannasastra University of Cambodia, spoke about the importance and necessity of studying philosophy in order to find meaning and value in one's life in harmony with human beings, animals and nature.
Today, he explains, a lack of philosophy will lead to a social and economic deficit, and some countries may even reach a major catastrophe: war.
The 40-year-old professor told the Post that negative philosophies could cause some of the world's poorest and poorest nations to fall into chaos if leaders' decisions are made without common sense. .
Heng Sreang warned that leaders who lack a positive philosophy of people, animals and nature could lead the country to conflict, violence and even war.
He said that social education, philosophy, it has a great influence on economic development and the spirit of national governance.
In general, the form of leadership of a state is sometimes less important, but the most important thing is the will of the leader.
According to Heng Sreang, much of the war in the world today stems from the extremism of leaders, following the instincts of a group of people who force them to follow their own philosophy.
But when those who are forced to follow are opposed, it leads to opposition.
For Cambodian society, there used to be conflicts between communist extremist ideology and liberal ideology, and eventually foreign ideological conflicts plunged Cambodia into the flames of war throughout the 1970s.
When people lack philosophical education, they are the source of violent conflicts and wars that wreak havoc on human, animal, and natural life.
Lack of education and lack of forethought will lead to the destruction of a state or a nation.
Cambodia has been in conflict since the Angkorian era to the present due to the conflict between two or three parties with conflicting philosophies and cultures, which eventually led to the weakening of the civil war and foreign aggression.
Cambodia has now chosen a good philosophical option, multi-party, a political regime that the world has promoted as a consequence of coexistence with progress.
As long as they are considered enemies like this, they will certainly not be able to penetrate each other and the result can lead to destruction.
In Cambodia, leaders define their philosophy as wanting people to live in peace and development, while now Cambodian workers going to Thailand are being kicked and despised.
In Malaysia, people were beaten, broken arms, broken heads, broken thighs, broken spine and so on.
Our society is a society with dementia, selfishness and a disease of inadequate education.
Singapore is considered a regional dictator because a state does not allow people to demonstrate freely.
But the leaders of this country have a clear philosophy: they want a prosperous and clean society.
As for communist China, Chinese leaders want the Chinese people to have value and dignity in life, no matter where they live on earth.
After the philosophical goal was achieved, no country in the world dared to criticize the leaders of Singapore and China.
Philosophy is a discipline that teaches people to think critically.
This course will lead people to think about the meaning of their own lives and the meaning of national destiny.
To solve the above social problems, first, Cambodia must promote psychological education through religion through moral education and non-violence.
Second, use the role of the state in the field of education by teaching people to use thinking rather than living like a madman.
Third, to respect the law and apply it properly.
In addition, a culture of dialogue should be practiced to find common ground for the nation in the long run.
Mr. Heng Sreang: Studying philosophy brings me to know and understand the meaning of life
A professor of philosophy devotes many years of time and energy to studying philosophy, a skill that has almost no job market in Cambodia compared to economics, law, management or commercial skills, which can earn a lot of money. More than a dozen times.
In a recent hour-long interview with the Post, Heng Srang explained in detail why he had to sacrifice everything for philosophical knowledge rather than focus on other important professions.
It may have been his passion since childhood to require one to volunteer his love for the study of the philosophical skills and ideals of some of the world's greatest scholars.
Heng Sreang Hak has been keen since he was a child to choose this philosophy as a goal for his future studies, despite his parents' objections.
Heng Sreang called his study from childhood to the present a proud achievement for himself and his family after investing many years in studying at home and abroad in philosophical skills comparable to Discover a better life with a simple life, no ambition and kindness, full of compassion, tolerance, living in harmony with people, animals and nature in harmony.
Born in Koh Sotin district, Kampong Cham province, the hometown of his father, but grew up in Prey Veng province, the hometown of Heng Sreang's mother, he attended primary school in Prek Sandek village, Prek Changran commune, Sithor Kandal district, Prey Veng province.
The boy's education was not very thorough due to the lack of study materials and teachers at that time.
In the early 1990s, as Cambodia underwent political change, open access to free elections and the transition to a multi-party democracy, the education sector continued to improve.
Special study materials, textbooks are also plentiful, and talented teachers come to teach students.
After failing the exam in 1993, Heng Sreang left home to go to Phnom Penh to continue his studies.
In 1994, I passed the entrance exam at the Royal University of Phnom Penh. Philosophy is something I have loved since I was young.
The love of reading philosophy books, reading and writing poetry, reading stories and the history of the Buddha, the Master, and exploring the ideas of world-renowned philosophers has been instilled since childhood.
He was fascinated by the words and ideals of world-renowned philosophers, including Socrates, Plato, Confucius, Lazarus, and the Buddha.
Mr. Heng Sreang said that the more he reads, the more fun and curious he became, and the more he developed a love for philosophy, despite the fact that it was not popular with most students. Because this is a difficult subject to find a job in the future.
Mr. Srang said:
In today's Cambodian society, philosophy is not an easy subject to make money compared to economics, law, architecture and mathematical skills.
However, it did not stop Heng Sreang from falling in love with this philosophy.
He is a person who does things naturally and by his own will.
Learning philosophy is a choice out of love, regardless of what the philosophy is about wealth or benefits.
Today, I am very proud of myself.
There seemed to be everything inside me, and what I chose from the beginning, I was not disappointed, even though society did not need this subject very much.
General philosophy includes:
Political philosophy, social philosophy, legal philosophy, human philosophy and natural philosophy, where scholars see everything, making them happy and proud.
For some young people who do not fully understand the benefits of philosophy, they think that learning philosophy drives them crazy, in fact, it is not.
Philosophy is a very important subject in training people to think long and hard and to transform their personal lives into meaningful, meaningful, calm and enjoyable people, animals and nature. Around the body.
The professor explained:
World-renowned philosophers have taught people to live together in harmony with one another, to live without jealousy, without remorse and without violence.
He said that philosophy teaches people to think about the laws of nature, to live with nature and to harmonize with a moral and non-violent spirit.
Mr. Sreang said:
If we destroy the nature around us, the elements that sustain human and animal life, do we still have a human name?
How do those around us relate to us? If we destroy nature, will our lives be destroyed by nature?
This philosopher valued his life as an intellectual with a sense of right and wrong and tolerance for others, even though his life was not rich.
They can live with us, they have enough food, their children have the right education, society has respect for each other.
This is an important factor for me.
Choosing a lifestyle that suits you is essential.
But that choice must give peace to the people and animals around them.
The professor called modern things, including houses, money, cars, villas, just external ornaments, but if there is no emotional content, there is no harmony and no freshness, such a life is nothing. No sense.
World philosophers have taught people different ways of living, especially Western and Eastern philosophers.
In Europe today, especially developed countries, people are trained to think deeply in real scientific way, to think in terms of evidence, rather than to teach people to believe in something without evidence.
The Buddha's view in Buddhism is that everything has a relationship between cause and effect.
He teaches people to walk the middle path, the right way to live in peace.
In Greek philosophy, philosophy is the love of wisdom, the love of wisdom.
Thus, anyone who devotes time, energy, mind to study philosophy means that you want wisdom, want good ideals to live with others.
Aristotle, a Western philosopher, said that philosophy is the thinking of people who need to find a cause that works.
Suppose we see an object happen, we can not immediately judge that object.
According to Aristotle, we must find out what the object is from, what its causes are, what phenomena appear.
People in developed countries, especially in Europe, generally understood the philosophy of Greek scholars, taking the wrong balance and taking it as a basis for living without superstition.
Heng Srang said that Cambodia should also promote philosophical thinking so that people can think about the value and meaning of life in living together in the community while the world is enjoying modern technology, which is why Pollution, too.
Ignorance of philosophy will lead to violence and conflict in the society.
This is a crisis in the overall quality of education in Cambodia.
Campaign to improve health standards within the national framework should be jointly urgent
About $ 300 million a year flows out of the pockets of Cambodians into Thailand, Vietnam, Singapore and France through health services.
The main reason why Han people spend so much money is because they do not trust the health care services of Cambodian doctors in public and private hospitals in Cambodia.
This is the main reason why Dr. Som Leakena wants to change this negative view by providing an international standard health service at Khema International Hospital in Phnom Penh.
Dr. Som Leakena is the President and Founder of Khema Global, which just inaugurated Khema Hospital in Boeung Keng Kang on September 3.
Dr. Som Leakena explained that human health is more valuable than gold.
For those who think short between a piece of gold and good health, they will definitely choose gold, but for those who think long, they will definitely choose good health.
Those who choose gold are short-sighted.
It is true that the living conditions and health of Cambodians are much better than in previous decades, but compared to other countries, Cambodians' health is still very low.
It is this mental illness that causes other physical illnesses.
On the other hand, the diet of Cambodians has been contributing to the health of Cambodians.
Well, if we are not careful, it can affect our health.
There are many different factors ranging from hygiene to lifestyle that affect health.
Awareness of the health of the Cambodian people is far below the regional standard, which requires a campaign.
The HIV / AIDS campaign is considered a success in Cambodia because it has visited villages, communes across the country to explain the risks to the people.
In order to protect, we have to campaign that in order to protect Ahn, we have to do something about food, hygiene and exercise.
We must work hard.
Dr. Som Leakena explained that the influence of the media has led people to believe that Cambodians spend a lot of money every day on health care in Thailand, Vietnam, Singapore and France.
First, he does not believe in the professionalism of national doctors.
Second, negative publicity in the health sector.
I said ah prahok.
However, he acknowledged that in France, Singapore and Switzerland, the health sector could be improved because food was clean, roads were clean, toilets were public and transportation was healthy.
This psychology is very important in healing.
In order to build the confidence of Cambodian patients to trust Cambodian doctors, the whole country should have a campaign to build and strengthen the reputation of the Cambodian health sector in a comprehensive manner by strengthening the medical profession, rescue equipment and ethics of Cambodian doctors at the standard level. One that most people can accept.
He also expressed concern that the poor health of Cambodians could be one of the reasons for their long-term poverty.
After studying the health sector in Cambodia, it was found that most of the families of Cambodians spend almost all of their household income on health services, while European countries spend only 25% of their salaries.
This is according to Dr. Som Leakena.
Standardization Dr Som Leakena says his Khema Hospital has set a standard of efficiency in providing services to citizens and reducing overseas costs.
He says a team of doctors on ethics on how to communicate with patients with high moral ethics is necessary.
For hospitals, there are standards, but everything must comply with all standards from entry to exit.
He reminded people that treatment is important, but health protection or prevention is even more important.
Regular check-ups, regular prescription medications and quality medications, regular exercise and a healthy diet are all ways to prevent and control the cost of treatment.
His last message is: First, the national institution will find all ways to prevent all crimes of importing substandard drugs into the Cambodian market.
Second, to improve the quality of medical services, to strengthen the professional ethics of doctors and to provide appropriate salaries for both public and private hospitals.
Stop doing wrong, people are starting to flood their homeland.
All must be done to be able to change.
Dr. Som Leakena was born into a poor family in 1970 during the US-led bombing of Vietnam and Cambodia. Leakena had a mother who was a small businessman and a father who was a teacher in Takhmao City, Kandal Province.
Mr. Leakena has 13 siblings, 7 sisters and 9 brothers.
From 1975 to 1979, he had to go to work and eat together, and sometimes children who could not stand hunger also fought for unhygienic food.
Road travel requires walking and asking for a ride.
Arriving in Takhmao City, the parents decided to live in Doeum Kor village and gather relatives to build a house there.
Leakena entered the first grade at the age of 9 at a school near her home.
After graduating from bankruptcy in 1989, the young man passed the medical exam while he had no interest at all.
After graduating from medical school, Leakena went to work at a health center in Ang Snuol district, Kandal province, for three years, and a friend suggested that she continue her studies in France in 2000.
Studying is very difficult without knowing French.
I went to study for a year and changed my major.
I studied privately as well as at university and later went to work to make ends meet.
In France, he worked as a nurse assistant at a nursing home with doctors from poor countries such as Africa, Brazil and India.
Arriving in Phnom Penh, he worked with a team of French doctors, a private doctor, Royal Rattanak, for four years before he got married.
Khmer territory lost because we came back because we do not blame foreigners
Last week, Professor of History Sambo Manara shared the leadership ideas of the Angkorian kings who educated society about unity as a whole from top to bottom and as a key to uniting Cambodians of that time and building a prosperous nation. .
This week, he talks about the causes of division, loss of territory, war, floods and the deficit of the national economy.
According to Khmer history, wars in Cambodia since ancient times have been caused by two major factors:
The first is the inability to control the territory and the second is to cultivate the wrong educational ideas such as hatred, excessive ambition and selfishness.
Please give the following examples in Khmer history.
Between 550 AD and 850 AD, a civil war broke out between the kingdom of Chenla and the mountain kingdom.
The two sides fought for about 300 years before Chenla Tuek conquered the entire territory by uniting it.
People are born on both sides to cultivate anger, love, hate and vengeance, partisanship according to their ancestral ideas.
So why did the Chenla Tuek Empire invade and occupy the mountain kingdom?
That is because the mountain kingdom itself is out of control, its ability to defend itself is weak, and its rule depends only on other nations.
At that time, the more capable Chenla Tuk thought that they had to fight to unite the territory.
After more than 300 years of fighting in Khmer territory at that time, Chenla Teuk won the war in a state of weakness, the national food was almost depleted, the national economy was weak, the people lived in poverty, the society was chaotic, and the people lacked education.
The professor explained:
This indicates that the nation needs more than 300 years to triple the time to educate reconciliation about love, justice and trust.
Fan Zhiman was a strong king in the Funan period but lacked the ability to educate his children.
He made the economy and the country very prosperous, but at the time of his death all his sons quarreled and killed several generations of the royal family, causing the people to be in turmoil.
This is education to hate each other for selfish ambition.
War occurs because of wrong education.
In 1400, King Ponhea Yat, after coming to reign in Phnom Penh, died in a dispute over the division of territory into three.
The territory of Preah Srey Raja, the territory of Preah Dharmaraja and the territory of Preah Srey Soryavong, one in the east of the city, one in the center, and one in the whole of Pursat province.
In modern history, most Cambodians are directly involved in the war of the 1970s to early 1997.
How soft is Cambodia after more than three decades of war?
Cambodia has only known peace for 18 years to reconcile people's trust and provide an opportunity to re-develop the country.
During the war in the 1980s, the people of Phnom Penh advised their children that the Khmer Rouge was very bad, but the coalition government on the Thai border educated that Phnom Penh was all Vietnamese.
This is teaching to hate one another.
Mr. Manara said:
Samdech Techo Kdei, Samdech Ta Preah Norodom Sihanouk, both characters and the people of Cambodia can not bring peace if we do not want it.
The professor said:
There has never been a generation in Cambodia that has been more peaceful for more than 50 years, because we have had internal conflicts, ambitions, and almost no proper education until now.
Professor Sambo Manara said:
He explained that the Cambodian people are the army, the defense of the nation, the defense of the nation, as well as the diplomatic institutions and national leaders at all levels, who all have duties and obligations to defend their territory, not to rely on foreign aid in spirit and food. And do not blame foreigners, because they also need benefits.
It is true that it is difficult for Cambodia to regain lost territory, but we should not blame foreigners, but instead think about self-defense.
He said that young Cambodians today still do not have a clear understanding of their national historical ideas.
He continued that the Cambodian people must do their best not to lose their land and take care of what they have.
To date, living in Cambodian society has not been dignified and socially justified, as education at the grassroots level has not been harmonized at the same time.
What are the more than 100,000 teachers and parents of hundreds of thousands of students doing?
The media sometimes causes problems in society.
At mealtimes, he often spends time talking to his children about love, telling them what to do and what not to do.
But now it is different.
He blamed today's results on the influence of the Khmer Rouge regime, which Khmer Rouge leaders taught white paper children to hate one another.
Although Cambodia has a lot of wars and degrading social morality, Cambodia has also made a number of heroes to be proud of.
Some think that Jayavarman VII was a hero, but others say no because he was a king with two wives, and others said he forced them to build temples to weaken the national economy.
But according to Sambo Manara, a hero is someone who does something that no one else does, and his results are very positive for the nation.
Jayavarman VII was a hero who used the concept of Buddhist knowledge for the benefit of the nation.
Jayavarman VII did good deeds, liberated the land, and captured the Chams.
He strives for his nation.
Samdech Preah Norodom Sihanouk and Samdech Techo Prime Minister Hun Sen are also well-known figures in Cambodian politics, and the work of both actors is for the national cause.
In national history, Mr. Sambo Manara found that the Khmer people have been nationalistic since ancient times because they agreed to live, hardened, moved and ran.
Jayavarman VII, when he came to attack Angkor, he came to Kampong Cham, Kampong Thom, and sent troops to fight back.
