Ancient kings wanted Cambodian society to be united and united like the stones of Angkor Wat
The kings of the Angkorian period left a unique philosophy and sociology to lead and defend the Khmer kingdom to the next generation of leaders, demonstrating that philosophy in the way of building strong, lasting stone temples on earth. Assembling tens of thousands of stones together.
This is according to Mr. Sambo Manara, a professor of history at Pannasastra University of Cambodia and the Royal University of Phnom Penh.
In an hour-long interview with the Post on the subject of history, Sambo Manara saw how the ancient monarchs led the country in a scientific and sociological manner that should be of interest to historians and leaders of modern Cambodia.
He said that the kings of the Angkorian period chose a superstitious philosophy to lead the country, which led to the unification and strong economic development of the period.
Historically, in 807, Jayavarman II adopted the social science religion as a guide and development of the country.
He tried to mobilize people to love one another through the monarchy, which required the monarch and the people to unite and be completely united in building a society according to their status and role, without any controversy or spiritual conflict.
He, the supreme leader, received a blessing from the gods, and he thought that the people should receive that blessing as well, so he built 1,000 shrines on Phnom Kulen in Siem Reap province.
He used the philosophy of deity, giving value to the deity, leading to the creation of Amret water, which originated on the top of Phnom Kulen and flows into the Siem Reap River and the Tonle Sap Lake.
That powerful sociology was able to draw his people together in harmony under the King.
He understood that a stone, if it is outside the temple, no matter how hard it is, they do not care, because the stone outside the form of the temple is a stone that is not useful to the temple.
The top of the temple is above, the foundation of the temple is underground, and some stones are carved into art forms that confirm the daily life of the people and kings of that period.
The solid rock all agreed to be together, sticking together without cracking.
The stone, which is the foundation of the temple, agreed to lie in the ground without seeing the sun forever.
The second layer of stone as the third wall, the fourth as the top and the fifth as the top, also agreed on their respective roles.
A stone at the top of the temple is always affected by all kinds of hot, cold, rain, wind, storm, lightning, etc., like a leader who is willing to face all obstacles more than the people below.
But if the top is removed, the whole temple will be beautiful.
This was the philosophy of the kings of that time.
In particular, any stone that is not in the form of a temple, even if the animal is placed or damaged, will not be taken care of, but for the stone that is with the temple is a sacred stone that is recognized or given. The highest price.
If a stone is missing, it must be repaired immediately.
This is the philosophy of Khmer unity that existed in the Angkorian era.
The professor said:
The combination of solidity, strength, people, and software, the monarchy formed a development.
The strength of leadership in ancient times was the use of unity and consensus as the basis for the cohesiveness of the stone temples.
From the point of view of Mr. Sambo Manara, the king who ruled in the Angkorian era, he wanted to make the people love, respect and want the people to see his courage as a king with royal splendor.
His culture of sharing is another social concept that makes human society harmonious by accepting each other.
