During an hour-long conversation with the Post, Professor Sambo Manara also examined the leadership style of five Cambodian leaders in the past and present, as well as the strengths and weaknesses of leading and building the country.
What are the advantages and disadvantages of the five top national leaders in history during their rule?
He did not think too much of superstition, he tried to lower himself from doing national affairs with the nobles to work with the people of the middle class and with the lowest class.
In that sense, Jayavarman VII did not work only for the dynasty, but he sacrificed everything for the people.
In fact, he built hospitals, roads, bridges and halls and temples and temples not only for the gods but for the people.
His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk, his best point was to bring the nation to know the taste of independence and leadership in the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era by providing opportunities for young people to participate in politics and social work.
His worst point is how to deal with the left.
He has been cursed throughout history for slipping unconsciously into the political crisis of the Cold War in an ideological conflict.
Lon Nol's weakest point was his inability to fight the war and his withdrawal from the country.
He was a sincere leader who remained in the leaf house and died in the leaf house.
He sacrificed everything for his cause, even if his cause was not successful.
Pol Pot set an example by not taking advantage of himself, no matter how capable he was.
But the worst thing about Pol Pot is that he kills people.
Compared to other eras, the economic structure is stronger.
Peace in the Techo era was longer than during the reign of King Norodom Sihanouk in 1953-early 1970, and more developed.
The value under the regime led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen is peace.
But management is still in crisis, recognizing that everything is not just about governance, there is economic, political, foreign and domestic.
As a teacher and disseminator of past knowledge to young people, Professor Sambo Manara sees history as a reflection of current and future practice.
He wants historians, politicians and scholars to know that there are good role models in history, and that Cambodians have heroes who have created a lot of heroism for the next generation.
Even though he is in his 60s, he still has ambitions to share knowledge with the next generation through the subject of history, gathering the ideals of young people to understand social work.
If you want to cook, you have to find garlic and lemon.
Mr. Manara experienced a tragic life like other Cambodians.
He was placed in a mobile unit by the Khmer Rouge with a younger brother.
The second sister is disabled to this day because the Khmer Rouge used her to the point of breaking her spine.
When he returned from the Khmer Rouge in 1979, the most important thing was to build a family and he got married.
He is survived by six sons and daughters and six grandchildren.
However, it is one of my pride.
He graduated with a bachelor's degree in 1991 from the Royal University of Phnom Penh.
He holds a Master's degree in History from the University of Long Beach.
He is currently the Vice President of the Department of History at the Royal University of Phnom Penh and Director of the Center for Khmer Studies at Pannasastra University of Cambodia.
Mr. Sambo Manara is a teacher who has not changed his career for 35 years.
He studied in Phnom Penh, primary school at Preah Keo Morakot School, Toul Tompong School.
Then go to high school at Ponhea Yat School, Tuol Sleng now.
On April 17, 1975, his family was evacuated by the Khmer Rouge to Battambang province.
Who is Mr. Soeung Morarith, CEO of Chief Specialized Bank of Cambodia?
Mr. Soeung Morarith was born in Svay Chrum village, Prek Dambang commune, Muk Kampoul district, Kandal province.
He is the eldest child in a family of five.
In my first year, I worked hard to learn English for a whole year before I had the opportunity to work as a part-time teacher at a private English school, and at that time my first salary was only $ 38.
So we study in college in the evening, we work as English teachers to earn money to support their studies as well as living in Phnom Penh.
In Phnom Penh, Morarith stayed with his uncle until graduating in 2003 and began working in the banking industry in 2004 as a regular junior.
While working part-time every weekend, Saturday-Sunday, Morarith took a master's degree.
As both his work and his studies progressed smoothly, Chiff Cambodia Bank invited him to participate in the development, offering him the position of CEO of a foreign bank in 2013.
On July 11, 2016, the bank received in principle approval from the National Bank of Cambodia to transform itself into a commercial bank, Cambodia.
Those secrets include:
The first is to pay attention to the high commitment to work willingly and consciously want this institution to be successful.
Third, set work priorities.
During working hours, there are many jobs at the same time, but it is necessary to determine which jobs are the priority, making the high-profit institutions not take risks.
Fourth, work harder than talking.
Fifth, be patient and be honest with the institution.
Apply these six principles well so that you can be the director of this foreign bank.
There are currently 12 specialized banks in Cambodia licensed permanently by the National Bank.
There are 36 commercial banks.
Specialized banks are banks that can provide only one of the three main services of a commercial bank, including credit products, deposits or remittances.
As for the specialized bank, Chiff chose the loan service because through the loan service, the bank will earn interest on the loan.
Most of the customers of Chiffon Specialized Bank are business owners.
The young CEO has put in place an effective strategy to run the foreign bank smoothly as the sector tends to compete for human resources from one bank to another.
He said that more experienced employees move from one place to another due to higher salaries and higher benefits as foreign banks come to Cambodia to open a series of banking services.
Mr. Soeung Morarith said that the strategy of foreign banks is to attract more experienced Cambodian employees by double or triple the benefits plus the side benefits.
Chiff has the lowest staffing rates, and most of them go to work for the government, some for their own business.
Mr. Morarith stated that Chief Bank has its own strategy for retaining good employees.
The first is related to good governance, a good working environment and the provision of highly competitive benefits.
Second, additional allowances for major festivals such as Chinese New Year, Khmer New Year, Pchum Ben and the 13th month salary.
That is the competition to capture the market to attract customer support, as Cambodia has 12 specialized banks, 36 commercial banks, 58 microfinance institutions and more than 400 financial operators in a market with a total population of nearly 15 million. .
He said that in order for a product to be accepted in the market, the bank must have a flexible credit policy, which means that the bank allows customers to repay the principal early or partially without penalty.
The second is competitive interest.
That is, the staff is highly ethical and clean.
According to the National Bank's annual report released in 2015, Chief Specialized Bank had a market share of 1.4%, ranking second among the 12 specialized banks.
Rural Development Bank holds 2.4% of the total market share of specialized banks over 7.6%.
Soeung Morarith is optimistic about the rules governing and operating the banking sector as a whole.
He said that without these laws, the NBC would be difficult to manage.
The main laws passed include governance laws such as internal control, capital obligations, such as consumer protection principles and precautionary regulations.
In the past, it has been suggested that the Cambodian financial market could be an easy place for money laundering, but for Morarith there is no pessimism about such allegations.
He said that the system to monitor the inflow or outflow of cash in the Cambodian financial market is not too bad is acceptable.
In general, he is optimistic about the current development of the banking sector, stating that the assets of the financial system in Cambodia cover more than 90% of the banking system.
Mr. Morarith predicts that in the next five years, the banking sector will be more developed than it is now, as the first will be further strengthened governance through the National Bank's oversight regulations and laws.
The second is that compliance with those regulations, which can make the entire banking system run smoothly and efficiently, can attract a lot of public trust, meaning that the money people keep at home under the pillow may be reduced. .
However, he noted that customers now have a better life because when the loan rate is zero, the MPL 0 indicates that customers have a better life.
Om Seng Bora: Strong business leads to strong economy, reduces migration and increases state revenue
Last Friday, all readers knew exactly who Om Seng Bora was and what his background was.
This week, Seng Bora shares his personal views on helping boost economic growth through the strength of local enterprises.
Although small and medium-sized businesses are the backbone of the economy, the number of those enterprises is still small, which is one of the reasons why people are unemployed and forced to migrate.
In an interview with the Post, Bora said that promoting and supporting small and medium-sized enterprises was a good option for Cambodia, which could help boost the national economy in large part.
As an entrepreneurial coach, he can turn a person who has no business talent by birth into a successful entrepreneur without having to compete with his or her competitors to increase wealth.
Mr. Seng Bora gave a simple tip that if you want to succeed in business, you must first create a business passion by associating with like-minded people.
As the old saying goes, associating with a thief will become a thief, associating with a doctor will become a doctor.
After all, he himself began associating with business people by stealing ideas from those consumers and looking for a career opportunity that suited his abilities.
In addition, they need to learn the rules, strategies and rules of doing business, including the rules to compete for customers and market share.
Every successful entrepreneur in the world is inevitably competitive.
But fair or unequal competition is an important question.
But as he begins to engage with the business community, they begin to understand their values and the values of those around them, together building a business community that gives everyone a chance to grow. .
We can not escape the competition, but we compete for growth, not we win or lose.
Business Gambling It is necessary to have rules as a rule of the game, and the government and all relevant institutions have the responsibility to make that law and entrepreneurs to implement it.
For Cambodia, the government still needs more time to pass laws to protect businesses because it is a developing country.
All relevant institutions should work closely with business people, communities, associations, business clubs, etc. so that they can find out what they need to do to help local and international business people to run their business and be able to compete freely and Equality.
Mr. Sengbora said his club has trained about 240 business owners, training them on business plans, service selection, production and packaging, and managing revenue and expenditure.
Business barriers are a business's game to develop and grow financially.
Mr. Seng Bora's training program for entrepreneurial members consists of 13 programs for them to build themselves and their institutions, including accounting and financial statements, to build a strong business empire.
He said that in order to accelerate the economic sector, the Royal Government of Cambodia should act as a private sector stimulus by facilitating some necessary tasks.
Second. Facilitate tax law, business card, do not take too long.
Fifth. Transparency and fairness in competition and 6th. Prevent the import of tax evaded goods.
Mr. Om Sengbora urged all Cambodian entrepreneurs to be confident in their ability to grow by exercising their competitiveness in the business world by training their skills.
Mr. Bora also asked all Cambodian entrepreneurs to look for cross-border business opportunities, especially in the ASEAN region.
But there must be a concerted effort from all stakeholders to expand the scope to at least be a good partner with local investors.
In order to capture the ASEAN market share in the context of ASEAN AEC economic integration, Mr. Seng Bora believes that first. Must strengthen its ability to be a good international partner.
Second. Consider using good, modern technology in business.
Third, increase the reputation and standard of quality of goods and services.
Of course, ASEAN has its pros and cons, but we should not think about the disadvantages because we are in it and we should think about what we can do.
The speaker explained that in order to be a good partner, we need to build our institutional capacity clearly.
So we organize to gather so that we can do something big because we lack finance and lack human resources.
Encouraging domestic and foreign investment is important and necessary, but he urged the government to look into the institutions that have been cracking down on traders.
Mr. Bora stated:
Mr. Om Seng Bora: If you want to get out of poverty, you have to associate with businessmen
Born in Kampong Thom province during the Khmer Rouge era, he grew up as a child like most children after the fall of the Khmer Rouge regime in January 1979.
But what is different is that his family has been poor for the longest time.
He struggled in life as a young man and as a young man in the countryside.
Bora used to be a small cigarette seller, used to collect scrap metal, used to write down lottery numbers, used to run a noodle table, used to sell cakes, sold candy, sold a fan, was a card ironer and used to work as a water collector for jars to get money.
When you are involved in life, you have to help your family to realize that there are many difficulties.
Families with a lack of education are also less focused.
When it came time to think about money, I started to worry about myself. On the other hand, my family did not have enough money to live on, and I owed a lot of money, which made me feel very hopeless.
But angels do not block the path of good people.
The word "raising the bride" made the young man from Sre Seng Bora want to be the man in the white shirt.
From that day on, Bora fell in love with studying, wanted to learn English, and wanted to become a driver.
After failing the exam, Bora asked his parents to come to Phnom Penh because he wanted to continue his education in order to get a job like his role model.
At that time, this student was very worried.
In my heart I was hesitant to know what to study to be worthy of my desires.
Life in Phnom Penh is so poor that this young man worries and thinks, what can I do?
At that time, he thought of many successful people in the drama, the writer showed that all the characters were difficult at first, especially the Jin dynasty, and he wanted to be a Jin dynasty character.
The next idea is to find a scholarship exam opportunity.
His decisions and possibilities are different from most students.
He has taken exams abroad such as English, French, Japanese, Australian, American, but this student went to Prey Veng to study accounting at Vet Maha Russey University, an Australian government-sponsored university.
He put his heart and soul into studying while studying to get good results as well as good relationships with teachers and senior students.
After completing five years of study, he returned to Phnom Penh in 1998 to find work.
When he first started working, he felt that he was being looked down upon by foreigners, but later he realized that he still lacked work skills.
At that time, he was interested in becoming a strong Cambodian coach and working skills.
As an auditor, Mr. Seng Bora worked hard to ensure that the chairman was satisfied with the results beyond expectations.
At that time, in addition to being an auditor, he developed his team.
When he took over as manager, he wanted to have another reputation as a good leader to be a role model and must lead the team strongly.
Mr. Seng Bora said:
Wanting to be a coach, he set the rules for coaching.
Finally, there is recognition from other countries to visit Cambodia.
Employees are worried, donors are worried.
I study myself, my family, my neighbors.
I came up with an idea, a comparison between Chinese and Cambodian families.
He found that Chinese families in the community had a better life than Cambodian families.
That is because the Chinese know how to do business to increase family income.
Most Cambodian families farm and raise animals with lower incomes than Chinese families.
In the past, I hated entrepreneurs because they were richer, but now I have become an entrepreneur coach to do business like China.
Om Seng Bora has finally turned his life of hating the rich into associating with the rich and entrepreneurs.
He said that in a strong economy, it is strong in business.
Over the last decade, business activity in Cambodia has grown significantly.
Now Cambodians talk so much about business that no one has talked about it before.
There are more workshops, more education in schools, more media and training, more clubs, and the economy is developing faster.
Mr. Seng Bora found that business intelligence does not depend on skin color, white or black, nor is it genetic or genetic, it is important to train as a businessman, starting with a small business.
My skin is black, but I can train myself from workers to entrepreneurs to train entrepreneurs.
Mr. Bora added that the habits of Cambodians lack business ideas and if the business is small family.
This is an opportunity for you to contribute to change the perspective and promote the business creation movement by using the experience from a small business to an internationally recognized business to share how to manage a business, especially small and medium entrepreneurs.
For our country to be rich as long as our economy is strong and for the local economy to be strong as long as there are strong businessmen to be able to compete internationally with our neighbors.
He stressed that:
The starting point for economic growth is to start small and medium enterprises.
This case requires the motivation and facilitation of Cambodian business owners to produce goods instead of imports from abroad.
Mr. Seng Bora said he was able to turn unskilled people into successful entrepreneurs by cultivating a passion first.
Then cultivate the importance of entrepreneurship for oneself, family and nation.
Cambodia is not a money laundering paradise
A resident of Chamkar Leu district, Kampong Cham province, he used to live as a pagoda boy, living on the rice bowls of monks in Wat Chroy Thmor, Kampong Cham province, while he was in high school and stayed at Wat Ounalom when he was studying at a college in Phnom Penh.
Kea Boran holds a Bachelor's Degree in Accounting, a Bachelor's Degree in English and a Professional Accountant in the United Kingdom.
Kea Boran started his career in the financial sector in 1999.
He used to work as a bookkeeper, but he worked hard to develop until he became the CEO of AMK Financial Institution from July 2012 to the present.
The 37-year-old executive said in a recent interview with the Post that Cambodia has been better able to manage foreign investment flows than some countries in the region. .
He said that Cambodia is not a place for criminals to launder money because the Central Bank of Cambodia has passed a number of laws to regulate financial traffic, especially in the banking sector and microfinance institutions, although Cambodia is often suspected of being a money launderer. A convenient place for criminals to launder money from illegal activities while the country's legal system is weak.
The lending expert dismissed the allegations, saying the laws and regulations issued by the National Bank of Cambodia were adequate.
Boran said the National Bank of Cambodia had played a good role as a protector for all financial operators.
The NBC has been using all means to prevent all forms of financial crime.
Cambodia used to be ranked number one as a country with microfinance laws.
Under Cambodian law, remittances of more than $ 10,000 per day from one or more locations must be reported to the National Bank.
The National Bank has a department that inspects every year.
Although we are comparing with Singapore ... Recently, there have been cases of money laundering.
The financial expert seems reluctant to answer whether it is clean or unclean, but he says all investments go through the proper permits from the government, with a system in place to check the investment.
He said that all investments in the banking sector and microfinance institutions must go through a thorough inspection and report to the National Bank to ensure the provision of loans from banks and financial institutions to the people. It is a clean loan that is used to improve one's life.
Answering the frequently asked question that the banking sector and MFIs have been impoverishing Cambodians after getting into debt, and operators are becoming more and more wealthy. The ancients elaborated on this case.
Mr. Boran explained that customers themselves are also largely responsible for the use of money.
When they borrow money from the bank, if they use the loan properly, they will be successful.
But a scientific study conducted by AMK in 2007 confirms that the overwhelming rate of its customers is better.
This case can be measured by the type of food the client eats, the amount of rice he has, the number of children he can send to school, and the size of the land and house they own.
The growth or decline of a family's life does not depend entirely on borrowing money or not borrowing money, there are many factors.
So far, he said, financial services have covered 80-90 percent of the country's villages and communes.
Kea Boran is proud that the microfinance sector has made a significant contribution to national economic growth.
This financial sector is very important for Cambodia to move forward as long as this financial sector works together.
Therefore, without credit traffic, the country would not be able to move forward.
While there is progress, there are also some risks.
To prevent the financial crisis, the National Bank of Cambodia has been formulating policies to prevent Cambodia from following in the footsteps of the European Union or the United States.
In the past, the National Bank of Cambodia has just announced a capital increase for microfinance institutions, commercial banks and specialized banks, which is a guideline to strengthen the banking and financial sector.
No matter how good the law protects the sector, there are still some irregularities related to unequal competition.
Kea Boran does not seem to enjoy unfair competition with any unlicensed institution, which generally affects legal entities.
He explained the impact of unlicensed financial operators that because he did not get a license from the National Bank, he did not follow the principles or policies set by the National Bank.
For licensed institutions to report to the National Bank, they can know how big the credit pressure?
As for those who do not have a license, he can do as he pleases.
While Cambodia has a large number of financial institutions, there is also a lot of competition, so a strategy to attract customers is needed.
The market share is about 16-17 percent.
Gross revenue last year was about $ 30 million.
The institution is preparing to increase the capital to meet the needs of the National Bank to come into force in 2018.
Tep Vanny: Willing to sacrifice his life against house rape
When there are protests over land disputes and advocacy against various social injustices, especially the so-called black-shirt campaign for the right to legalize land management and the release of human rights defenders. People who have been unjustly detained often see this woman actively involved with many other activists, said Tep Vanny, a representative of the Boeung Kak Lake community.
She has been leading Boeung Kak Lake residents to protest land issues for nearly 10 years, since the Shukaku company used bulldozers to dredge villagers' homes, forcibly submerge villagers and forcibly evict them.
Due to the hard work of the people in the Boeung Kak Lake community so far, the Royal Government has decided to cut more than 12 hectares of land and give them a title deed gradually.
However, land activists in Boeung Kak Lake continue to form a network of land-affected communities in Phnom Penh, which has so far reached 46 communities and more than 20 communities in the provinces. In some cases, along with humanitarian programs, especially the black-shirt campaign in the community itself to demand the release of the four human rights activists and a NEC official who is being held in prison.
Ms. Tep Vanny said:
The 35-year-old woman has nine siblings and was born in Kampong Trach district, Kampot province.
From an early age of eight, she lived with her parents, who were well off because her father was a civil servant, but after a devastating accident in the 1990s, her family's livelihood deteriorated to the point where she had to pick up scrap metal. To earn a living and study.
But in the end, she decided to drop out of ninth grade and find work in Phnom Penh.
In 2003, she got married and moved to Boeung Kak Lake. In 2006, she worked as a saleswoman and a housewife without worrying about politics or social issues until she bought 5 hectares of land in Omlaing district. Thpong, Kampong Speu province.
Unfortunately, in 2010, the land in Kampong Speu province was confiscated by the Kampong Speu Sugar Company without any compensation, while in the Boeung Kak Lake area, the Shukaku company dredged the villagers' houses.
Seeing with her own eyes and suffering from social injustice, land grabbing and mistreatment of landowners at the time, she began a new life and was determined to sacrifice her life to protect her home and land. Boeung Kak area.
Ms. Vanny began studying land, human rights and related laws, as well as mobilizing people in the same affected villages, especially women, to join the advocacy campaign for their land rights.
As a land activist, she anticipates the current situation in Cambodia, saying that although Cambodia and the Cambodian people have been liberated from the Pol Pot regime for more than 30 years, they are still not free. From the suffering, both physical and mental, because the country's leaders have been in power for more than 30 years, but have not yet taken full responsibility for their people and nation.
Since Tep Vanny campaigned for land and housing for almost 10 years, she has faced many obstacles, such as beatings by the authorities, threats of losing her role as a housewife, family crises, and emotional distress. Under pressure, she has been jailed twice, taken into pretrial detention at the Phnom Penh Municipal Police 12 times and suffered multiple, almost life-threatening violence.
In this case, she states:
If there was no eviction, I would have lived in peace.
Ms. Vanny said that in addition to those challenges, she was also persuaded by the authorities to send her abroad so that she could stop protesting.
She was also offered a role in the opposition CNRP if she was interested, but was turned down.
Seeing her perseverance and courage in leading land protests and against human rights abuses, Vanny was awarded the International Human Rights Defenders Award in the Netherlands and the Vital Voices Award in the United States. From 2013.
However, Tep Vanny stated that she would continue to protest until all the people in the Boeung Kak Lake area had received their legal land titles, at which point she would turn to farming, animal husbandry and housekeeping. A good person with no intention of wanting to go to a position or position in any political party.
But Buntinh: Political crisis can be solved if politicians stop bragging
Venerable Bun Tinh is an activist monk who has always been very active in social work, like Dr. Kem Ley, who was just shot dead by gunmen on July 10. Strongly criticize the inactivity of the government.
Given the monk's background, due to his poor family situation and his willingness to continue his studies, Bun Tinh finally decided to become a monk on May 5, 2000 at Wat Varin, Varin District, Siem Reap Province. As of July 2016, the Venerable has been 16 years old.
The Venerable Priest said:
So in the pagoda we will be forced to study until we become enlightened.
In 2003, Venerable Bun Tinh received a scholarship to study at the University of Mumbai, India, and on January 15, 2005, he entered the university.
The Venerable worked hard until he graduated with a Master's degree in Sociology on April 19, 2011.
After returning from studying in India, he joined an NGO.
Until September 12, 2011, he worked as a professor at a well-known university in Phnom Penh with a decent salary.
The Venerable is known as a good speaker, good theorist.
But at that time, the Venerable asked himself, "If there is only a good theory, good at speaking, reading a lot of books, remembering the names of many authors, what good is it to the nation?"
He worked at a university where he did not want to be named. He was paid a lot of money, but he was not allowed to be a priest on social issues or political issues.
Finally, on July 1, 2013, he decided to resign from the university and become a community advocate.
The younger generation saw the problem of land grabbing and imprisoned their owners.
Seeing that, I said no, I still have to contribute as much as we can.
At that time, the Venerable posted a video clip about the arrest of Ms. Yorm Bopha overnight, and only immediately released the video clip saying that we took the campus to do politics because I was still working hours and was confused. Wear an institution card.
By mistake, wearing an institution card, making a video clip on Facebook, they just saw it, only a large number of CPP officials put pressure on the school to evict them, expelled them at night, at the wrong time of the administration, Chamroeun Por dismissed on Sunday, I said okay No, I don't need to be fired, I just have to go because I want to do social work, so I came out to work and prosper.
Since then, he has worked hard to focus on building justice and eradicating corruption and injustice in society.
In the past, Venerable Bun Tinh and many monks who are members of the Independent Monks Network have been seen protesting with the opposition and land activists and environmental activists to fight against abuses and social injustice. As a result, some government officials consider these monks to be members of the opposition.
However, Venerable Bun Tinh denied the allegations, saying that he not only had no role in the opposition party, but had never registered as a member of the party and had never received one. No benefit from the opposition.
But the monk's work focuses on social justice, and any party that protests against social injustice against corruption will join, not just the CNRP.
The Venerable emphasized that:
Second, I benefit from my society, because when a society has justice, a society has peace, a society with full respect for human rights, we all benefit.
Hi, no matter who the party is, as long as it benefits people, I will definitely participate.
At present, Venerable Bun Tinh does not have a pagoda to live in, because not all pagodas dare to register for Venerable Kong due to political pressure from above.
The monks often travel from province to province to perform their duties, sometimes staying at some pagodas for short periods of time or tying hammocks on the streets.
The Venerable has dedicated that he will not work for happiness alone if he wants to help Cambodian society until people dare to sacrifice their own happiness to serve society, we have succeeded in society, but not individual success.
The Venerable continued that the sacrifice of personal interests to serve the interests of society is not possible for everyone, and the Venerable thinks that whoever can do it should not criticize those who have not done this because the day Either they will do the same as the Venerable, because in the past the Venerable never sacrificed his personal interests for the benefit of society, but when the time came for the Venerable to do so.
Currently, the political situation in Cambodia is heating up.
Overheated arrests of opposition lawmakers and activists, officials of the human rights group Adhoc and the NEC Deputy Secretary-General, and an attempt to detain Kem Sokha, acting president of the Cambodia National Rescue Party and representatives Some opposition lawmakers are involved in cases that many consider to be politically motivated to serve only personal and party interests.
On July 10, Dr. Kem Ley, an independent analyst, was shot dead.
Venerable Bun Tinh, who has always worked closely with Dr. Kem Ley in social work, sees that the current political heat is due to the pride of Cambodian politicians.
He added that if he was given the right to be a decision-maker or to unite to resolve the political crisis, he would first have to persuade all Cambodian politicians to stop bragging.
Every day, the ruling politicians understand that Cambodia is their territory, they think that they can liberate their country, when the Cambodians are at war, where are you, why not come and fight!
Thus, this kind of will is called the will to take ownership of the nation.
The monk, who can speak and write in five languages: English, Thai, Indian, Burmese and Sanskrit, said: Cambodian politicians pretend to be national servants.
The experience that Cambodia went through from the 70s to the 90s, Cambodia used the tip of the cannon, bragging that it was good at fighting, but the end result was that the Cambodians met the tragedy of Cambodia, one side of the country fled to a third country.
Of course, the politicians are just the leaders, while the real defenders of the territory are the Khmer people of a kingdom.
He added that in the current situation, politicians must focus on national issues and put them on the table to negotiate, not to consider any Cambodian as their enemy, but to consider all Cambodians as a driving force. National Defense Society.
On the other hand, the Venerable wants politicians to know how to do politics in a new culture, that is, no matter which party they come from, they must know how to discuss national issues, while since Venerable Venerable Khmer politicians They don't talk much about national issues, they mostly talk about their respective parties.
Nowadays, the Venerable has never seen politicians take the common issue of the nation into the negotiation, thinking only of the interests of the party and the individual, doing politics to the point of stalemate and not knowing what to do with the individual.
It never goes to quarrel about individuals being imprisoned, thinking that national problems are impossible ... and the ruling party sees that it has run out of opportunities to gain popularity from the electorate, and therefore only arrests and imprisons dissidents. Let the people have no way to go, just vote for the ruling party.
The Venerable continued that for a political party that wants to succeed in a land where Buddhism is the basis, it is necessary to think, say and do the right thing.
Currently, the ruling party thinks that, firstly, when the opposition leader is arrested and imprisoned, the people who have lost their way will surely vote for themselves, and secondly, the ruling party thinks that splitting the opposition party so that the CPP can stay in power Continue.
Thirdly, the CPP made the people afraid not to participate in the political movement, he still won.
The Venerable said:
Initially, Venerable But Buntinh was only ordained for three months, but until now, he has been a priest for 16 years, but has not yet given up. Despite attempting to fight and buying clothes 4-5 times, as a priest, there are many opportunities to help society.
After retiring from the university, he participated in social work, focusing on the conservation of natural resources, which he originally used his own money.
When they run out of personal money, there are Buddhists, both local and foreign, who give them a suffix for working forever, whenever the Venerable needs it, that is when.
In addition, there is an American organization to help, but the budget has been completed and now the Venerable is preparing to submit a proposal to other organizations to continue to work to protect the natural resources that are being destroyed. .
In the future, if he decides to fight, he will become a businessman, and if he succeeds in business, he will become a politician, while most politicians in Cambodian society today do politics to gain a prominent position in government. To commit corruption.
However, until now, the Venerable has not yet decided whether to join any party in the future or to form a party of his own, despite the fact that someone asked him to form. The party and they are the ones who cover all the money that the Venerable thinks is a trap.
When asked for confirmation yesterday, was the Venerable scared while Kem Ley was shot dead?
The Venerable replied:
Kem Ley Cambodia is plagued by power addiction
After holding each position for 20 to 30 years, some people have stopped walking and are in wheelchairs and still continue to cling to power, refusing to retire, which is considered by Kem Ley as a power addiction. One of the four social ills that continue to plague Cambodian society in need of urgent treatment is making the country, which has almost disappeared from the world map, as prosperous as the developed world. Fast in the world.
Kem Ley, a researcher and also referred to by some media outlets as an independent analyst, gave a detailed description of these five social ills, which are at the root of many social problems. Cambodians today will be able to continue to plague this society in the future if they do not agree to solve the problem in time.
Mr. Kem Ley pointed out that the most serious social disease that has continued to occur in Cambodian society for many generations is the first disease of power addiction or power sickness.
Cambodia has never had a history of changing leaders peacefully like in developed countries, which is due to the power addiction.
The second disease is the disease of greed, wanting to be alone, wanting too much. Take the beach as your own, that is, want everything that is public property to be your own, regardless of others.
Kem Ley added that excessive greed, if there is no mechanism to prevent it, whoever comes to power would want to be alone like this for a long time, the whole country wants to be alone, want to be killed. After all, take it alone, aren't you afraid of ghosts?
The third disease is violence, which is the habit of using violence when you are a soldier, a policeman, a military officer, a prime minister, a minister, want to shoot, want to kill, want to shoot Anna, want to shoot, want to take Whoever kills and commits such acts ... Ah, I think that the habit of violence is so big that we have to treat it.
The fourth disease is a mental illness called the culture of revenge of Cambodian leaders from generation to generation.
The fifth is a disease that is not just a disease in Cambodian society, but it is a global disease, a disease of self-destruction.
This self-destructive disease is like deforestation, allowing overcrowded trucks to destroy roads, to become the head of state, the head of government, so what about the old self and the dying, what do you think? Thinking about destroying your child's honor like that ... not thinking about the future of your child, it means that people who are sick do not think about the future, the next person thinks only of themselves and destroys himself.
These five are social diseases that Kem Ley said must be treated urgently and treated wisely.
The treatment is to have the younger generation as the healer and the older generation as the role model.
By conducting research on both social and political issues, Kem Ley has identified the roots of many of the problems that are plaguing Cambodian society, including the wrong decisions of leaders. Because those decisions do not rely on data from scientific research.
He said that Cambodian politicians should use the data from research studies to formulate laws, formulate policies, and make decisions to govern the country.
He said that when people know how to use scientific data from real research, they can reduce conflicts a lot because one party can not say poor, one party can say because If the data indicates poor, it is poor; if it indicates rich, it is rich.
Kem Ley added that at present some people say that there are millions of Vietnamese, some say that there are no Vietnamese, which is a visual concept without taking data from real research. .
When there is such a problem in society, they have to hire a private company to do research to find out what percentage of Vietnamese are Cambodians, what percentage of Cambodians are Cham, what percentage of Chinese is a percentage of professional information. With science having the right evidence, doing so reduces the conflict that I want to see.
Kem Ley also conducted research on land issues.
The land issue, which is seen as a hot social issue, is spreading across the country, causing more and more people to suffer.
Mr. Ley said that the ongoing land crisis was due to a major mistake in government land management.
After 1979, each family received some 30-and-a-half-hectare two-hectare land, but the authorities did not issue a title deed to the people.
To resolve the ongoing land crisis, Kem Ley recommended that up to 90 percent of the land be resolved by the government, with each district and provincial cadastral officer conducting a survey to provide land titles to indigenous communities. All of them live in the 56 districts with 131 communes and 503 villages first.
Cadastral officials can do this for as long as six months, during which time the government has to suspend all ELCs, which often lead to land disputes with residents.
Currently, about 30 percent of the population has a solid land title.
The government must work with development partners to measure the land and provide a solid title deed to the remaining 70 percent of the population, which can begin work from May 2014 to December 2015.
Mr. Kem Ley emphasized that:
Land disputes, which in many cases have resulted in evictions without fair compensation at market prices and unresolved issues, have caused many people to fall from average to near poverty. They are forced to migrate.
In addition, it also has a serious impact on the environment and deforestation.
Kem Ley was born into a poor family in Ang Ta Kok village, Lay Bo commune, Tram Kak district, Takeo province.
His father, a professor, died during the Pol Pot regime, leaving him and his two brothers and sisters orphaned and living with his widowed mother since 1979.
Although his mother was only a noodle seller, Kem Ley struggled to get an education abroad until his sister became a doctor and his younger brother became a doctor. Worked in a private company.
In an interview with the Post as he had lunch after a break from work at a restaurant in Boeung Keng Kang recently, Kem Ley said:
Because his mother was struggling to make ends meet when he was in fifth grade, Kem Ley went to live with a noodle seller in Takeo to continue his education.
Kem Ley left Takeo province to stay at Wat Botum in Phnom Penh to continue his studies at the university.
He stayed at the pagoda until graduating from college and joined the Ministry of Health in the field of infectious diseases.
Kem Ley retired from government service in 2000 and went to work for the Cambodian Red Cross.
After finishing his job at UNDP, he went to work for a Thai company that did a lot of social work, especially helping the poor in Thailand, Cambodia, China and India, and then worked as a consultant for Ministry of Health, Global Fund.
Initially, Kem Ley did most of his research on social affairs, then on land, housing, human rights, and mineral resources, and most recently on political, economic, social, and cultural issues.
Kem Ley has set up a monitoring system for the Ministry of Social Affairs, the Ministry of Labor and the Office of the Council of Ministers regarding public administration reform, and recently conducted a research study on political and economic analysis for USAID. On the evaluation of the election project.
In addition, Kem Ley is conducting research for UNDP on the overall overview of UN agency programs in Cambodia, from state reform projects, ministerial projects, social projects, environmental projects, human rights, education. And health.
Kem Ley does not do research alone, he has a group of 20 to 30 young people per generation, he trained them gradually and do research.
So far, he has trained young people on research skills for five generations, with a total of more than 100 people, and most of them now work in embassies and international organizations.
Mr. Kem Ley said:
Kem Ley, who refused to reveal much of his personal problems because he thought they were not good for society, finally revealed that he got married in 2001 and now has four sons. The eldest is 12 years old and the youngest is 4 years old.
Kem Ley holds a degree in Public Health Research from Chulalongkorn University in Thailand and a degree in Social Sciences from the University of Malaya, Malaysia.
Kem Ley has no job or occupation other than research, but his wife sells second-hand pants at an apartment in Phnom Penh's Boeung Keng Kang market.
While his reputation is making headlines in some independent media, Kem Ley does not want to be called an independent analyst.
Mr. Kem Ley emphasized that the researchers were not independent, they were researching the social reality, they were only looking for evidence from the research.
Another person who is honest and helps a lot in society is His Excellency Son Subert, even though he is outside, but he is a gentle person, to be honest.
His role model for social activism is Lao Mong Hay, also known as an independent analyst by journalists.
But Buntinh: The current Cambodian society is a broken society that needs treatment
But Buntinh: If because of fear they are imprisoned and do not dare to participate in social work, society will be destroyed
The monk, the son of a poor farmer, did not even have enough to eat, but struggled to study until he received a master's degree abroad. He initiated the establishment of the Independent Monks Network for Social Justice on 28 August. 2013, one month after the fifth national election, in the vision of the movement to contribute to the protection of human rights and to increase social justice for real peace in the country.
Venerable Buntinh is best known for his active participation in protests by victims of land grabbing and house arrests. Ignoring the inactivity of the government, which is very different from other monks who do not care much about social and local issues.
After establishing a network of independent monks for more than a year, with more than 10,000 monks in five provinces registering as members, Venerable Bun Tinh turned the Independent Monks Network into a Buddhist organization to Peace, as the organization was legally registered with the Ministry of Interior on March 15, 2015.
The Venerable explained that the reason for the change from the Independent Monks Network to the Buddhist Organization for Peace was due to the fact that working as a network of monks was small in scope and not recognized by the authorities. Or the government.
Second, we want to receive social benefits, meaning we have the opportunity to receive help from donors.
When evolving into an organization, Venerable Bun Tinh did not expand its membership, but expanded his activities in each province by recruiting members from the past, some to work individually and some to volunteer. There are still many members.
Today, as people protest against land grabbing, social injustice, or the destruction of natural resources, more and more monks are joining the protests, most of them members of the organization. Buddhism for Peace.
Although these monks appear to have sacrificed both physical and mental strength to participate in social work, some monks and government officials have described the monks' participation in various protest activities as unethical and unethical. Also against the Buddha's commandments.
However, Venerable Bun Tinh explained:
Since his enlightenment, the Buddha has been on a 45-year-old mission to save people and nature, and there have been many conflicts in the time of the Buddha.
The king and the king quarreled, the king Pimpisar and the king Lachvi, the royal kingdom and the kingdom of Vesali quarreled, quarreled, never looked at each other, the Buddha intervened immediately and prospered.
The Buddha participated in solving many problems.
Another thing is that the people in the two kingdoms have a dispute over water for farming, holding swords and spears to reserve war to cut water for rice.
Seeing this, the Buddha hurried to join the settlement, and at that time, 1,000 monks also went to participate.
The Buddha settled the matter peacefully and drew people to religion to end the problem.
On the other hand, the reading and enlightenment of the Buddha are connected with nature, such as enlightenment under the tree, performing dharma under the tree, and Nirvana under the tree.
The Venerable continued:
Secondly, we see our Khmer history from the 1940s to the 50s, the monks struggled a lot, and we see some of our neighbors lose their literature to the Latin script.
But we Khmers can still preserve the Khmer language and Khmer script because of the active participation of monks in that generation.
The Venerable continued that if one wants to do a task successfully in accordance with the path of the Buddha, one must adhere to three things, the first is to think correctly, and the second is to say the right thing, to show a correct message to the people.
Therefore, it is necessary to speak the right language that our district needs people to participate in defending justice, and the people who are the perpetrators of justice can do it effectively. That's right.
On the other hand, in addition to being right in Buddhism, one of the great ways is to be right, because there is nothing wrong with going to protect the forest, because the forest is the breadwinner of the world for human and animal life.
Nowadays, pythons sometimes come to people's homes because they are homeless because someone went to destroy those habitats and even declared themselves to be right.
Now the weather is changing, the wind is raining, the rain is not normal, there is no drinking water, and I am willing to carry a 20-liter bag of water to give to people to drink twice as much urine, and I am blessed to work to protect the forest, leaving the water for them to drink is not the right thing to do. Blessings!
Venerable Bun Tinh sees that at present, the Cambodian Buddhists live in fear, firstly, fear of lack of economy, secondly, fear of social injustice, thirdly, fear of aggression, oppression, fear of border issues, and so on.
Seeing the dangers of society in this way, Venerable Sok Chet gave up his regular job with a regular income to work for the promotion of social justice because he understood that justice in society would not be possible. Alone, without struggle.
I understand that only when we do the right thing and show a certain result and we come out and do it can justice be done.
The Venerable was determined and ready to participate in this social work, no matter what he lost, because he had already prayed that if he was imprisoned he would not be imprisoned. He was afraid that if there was no pagoda, he would not take pity on him.
If, out of fear, they are imprisoned or killed and do not dare to participate in social work, the injustice in society will continue and society will be ruined because there is no cure.
The Venerable stated:
So if we do not work together to restore from all walks of life, this society will continue to crumble.
Currently, the Cambodian people are living in a new regime, a progressive regime, but nonetheless, the people continue to suffer from corruption and social injustice, which requires Cambodians from all walks of life, from clergy to politicians, to Buddhists. But participate in social activities to be able to restore this society.
In the past, some government officials have hit back at those who have been harshly critical of the government's inaction, saying that because they want to be famous and that if they do not criticize the government, foreigners will not provide assistance.
But Venerable But Buntinh said that what he wanted was social justice, peace and wanted to see Cambodians live equally, wanted to see Cambodians live with dignity, that is the big direction of prosperity, not want to be famous. Assistance as charged.
Venerable Bun Tinh joined the civil service in 1997, and in 1998 he enlisted in the 85th Army in Oddar Meanchey province.
But in May 1998, he left the army to go to the polls at the commune level.
He later went to work at the education office in Siem Reap's Varin district, his hometown.
Because doing these things is not of great benefit to the nation and the people, only to the benefit of the individual, he resigned from these jobs in these governments.
He stressed that what he was doing today was not to gain the popularity of individuals, as some government officials have always argued. Mao popularity, do it all together, Mao country, Mao country, join together, Mao, prosperity.
However, he said that in the end, advocates like him were not the ones to decide what to do to make the district prosperous and the people to live in dignity. No, only politicians.
Because of this, the Venerable always contributes to the support of clean politicians so that they can work to protect human dignity and defend the nation.
He was the second child of a poor farmer family in Uty village, Lvea Kraing commune, Varin district, Siem Reap province, with up to 11 siblings, two of whom died.
The Venerable Nhom Pros Nhom Srey is illiterate.
More than 10 years ago, Nhom Pros Nhom Srey of the Venerable was a laborer to remove potatoes for others, but now he has the land to farm and grow potatoes himself.
As a child, Bun Tinh did not study hard because his hometown was a battlefield where there were frequent clashes between the Khmer Rouge and Vietnamese-backed government forces.
It was not until 1995 that Bun Tinh fully attended school.
Due to his over-school age, Bun Tinh had to start from the fourth grade.
Until graduating from high school in Angkor Chum, Bun Tinh dropped out and returned home.
Later, Bun Tinh kept thinking that he could not study enough to find a job, so he wanted to find a way to study more, but there was no way out.
