Hor Nambora: Cambodia must join the four poles, China, ASEAN, the United States and the European Union
Last week, Hor Nambora, deputy secretary general of the Royal Government of Cambodia and a former senior diplomat, urged the government to treat Cambodian diplomacy.
This week, he shared his vision, vision and mission to promote international relations and cooperation in the cause of maintaining peace and expanding economic development by linking Cambodia to the Eastern and Western powers.
In the current context, it is widely seen that the four largest and most influential poles in the world are playing a key role in balancing power in the economic, political and security spheres: China, ASEAN, the United States and the European Union. .
Therefore, Cambodia's foreign policy must be balanced in these four poles.
He explained that although China and the United States did not agree on some points, they still sat at the table and talked for the common good.
In fact, last month, the United States also decided to rekindle old enemies in the Vietnam War in the early 1970s to influence and balance power in Asia.
Although Cambodia's foreign policy is neutral and non-aligned, which the Constitution requires is to adhere to the larger and more formal principles, bringing Cambodia closer to its great allies, China, ASEAN, the United States and Europe, is essential.
He saw the great benefits of having a good, traditional and long-lasting relationship with the Asian power of China.
Nambora explained that if only 0.01 per cent of the more than 1.3 billion Chinese people visited Cambodia, Cambodia would receive millions of dollars.
Without Chinese help, it will be very difficult for us to develop the country.
Similarly, Cambodia can greatly benefit from the ASEAN family of a total population of nearly 700 million and can also benefit from the United States and Europe.
However, in order to maintain good relations with those great powers, it is required to perform the core duties and responsibilities of the Cambodian Ambassador with high ability and talent in the cause of defending the national interest.
There have been recent cases of EU aid cuts being threatened, such as the ability of Brussels diplomats to use their defense capabilities to prevent the government from cutting off aid.
My strategy is to always have my partners work, I go to meet MPs, I go to journalists, I meet civil society networks in that country to help me.
Mr. Bora also expressed frustration with some Cambodian ambassadors for their weakness in finding solutions for Cambodian workers working abroad, both illegally and legally, such as in Thailand, China, Malaysia and Korea.
In order to fulfill his duties well, he urged social and internal affairs experts at all Cambodian embassies abroad to facilitate and find solutions to cases of abuse of Cambodian workers and citizens living abroad.
If our people are in conflict or oppressed, we must dare to stand up and defend.
In the face of constructive criticism of Cambodian diplomacy, on the contrary, he expressed his personal pride as a former Ambassador to the United Kingdom and Australia that he had helped to improve relations between ASEAN and Australia. Bring the two sides closer together and push for the ASEAN flag.
Bora is one of 12 Cambodian candidates to be shortlisted out of a total of 115 female candidates from 10 ASEAN countries.
Hor Nambora is scheduled to travel to Jakarta for an interview in early August.
He said the ASEAN Fund plays an important role in helping ASEAN leaders realize that society and culture within the ASEAN family are an important pillar that will contribute to ASEAN's political and economic work.
This means that our ASEAN campaign is still weak.
Bora hopes to become the executive director of the ASEAN Foundation.
This is based on more than two decades of work experience and ability as a Cambodian diplomat.
At the time, he was a personal representative of Prime Minister Hun Sen, lobbying with members of the UN Security Council.
In 1995, he was a consultant at the Cambodian Embassy in Bangkok, Thailand.
He came to be an advisor to Samdech Chea Sim while Samdech was the President of the National Assembly in the first mandate of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
In 1986-87, his first mission was to begin negotiations with UNTAC to hold democratic elections in 1993.
During 1982, he worked at the Tuol Sleng Prison Center as a researcher, while the People's Republic of Cambodia reorganized the prison under former Minister of Propaganda Keo Chanda.
His research in Tuol Sleng was aimed at setting up a court to try Khmer Rouge leaders after the liberation on January 7, 1979.
Nambora is probably the luckiest of the tens of thousands of Cambodians in terms of living, education and employment.
He was born into an aristocratic family whose father was a diplomat from the old society and continues to be a high official until today.
However, he never claimed his father's privilege to receive diplomatic and other positions.
Nambora is the second son of Deputy Prime Minister Hor Namhong out of five siblings.
The first brother, Hor Sothoun, is a former senior official at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation with his father, and the third brother, Hor Moniroth, is a former Cambodian ambassador to Tokyo, Japan, who is currently an official at the ministry. Tourism.
Bora, 59, has two daughters and they are unlikely to follow in their father's footsteps.
They do not like government work because government work is sometimes very stressful.
Hor Nambora: Cambodia's diplomacy has a disease that needs to be treated
A former Cambodian ambassador and the second son of Deputy Prime Minister Hor Namhong, the retired former Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, Hor Nambora, shared his personal experience as a diplomat. Cambodia for more than two decades.
Mr. Hor Nambora is currently the Deputy Secretary General of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
Last week, Hor Nambora spent more than an hour talking to the Post about the quality of life and work as a Cambodian diplomat who has to work with other countries in the region and the world.
Former Cambodian ambassador Hor Nambora has hailed his father as a diplomatic hero, but also seems to acknowledge that Cambodian diplomacy over the past three decades has been criticized by the public over his appointment as a diplomat.
Mr. Nambora explained that in the past there was a shortage, but now it is necessary to find new ways to solve the problem by reforming and making the Ministry of Foreign Affairs a model ministry for the future of young diplomats. The next generation.
He explained that there are many questions that the next generation of diplomats need to ask, such as: What do the young diplomats want to serve the ministry?
Do they value their abilities equally?
He says it is necessary for the Foreign Ministry to give officials confidence and hope for the future.
Mr. Hor Nambora confirmed that:
That's what we call transparency and quality.
People generally think of diplomats as officials who have a luxurious and luxurious civilized life, but obviously not as some people understand.
Hor Nambora complained that the standard of living of Cambodian diplomats was very low compared to the ambassadors of ASEAN countries and major countries in the world.
The difficulty of our diplomats is, firstly, his salary is low and secondly, it costs more.
Just buying a taxi ticket is also expensive.
So if we send our diplomats to work as diplomats, there is a shortage in their country, both in life and in clothes, they are in need, they do not want to be among them, and they can look down on us and it is difficult for them to talk to them equally. The face is very flat.
To strengthen the quality of Cambodia's diplomacy, Hor Nambora called on the Royal Government to make urgent reforms, especially during the tenure of the new Minister of Foreign Affairs, Prak Sokhon.
He suggested that the process of selecting each ambassador must go through the approval of the National Assembly, because the ambassador must ask the parliament to debate his psychology, strategy and objectives, as well as his merits.
If we are stuck, if we fall, we fall transparently.
Mr. Nambora said:
In my opinion, we have to consider his livelihood, Cambodian diplomats have to consider the quality of diplomats, we have to choose a suitable ambassador, even in our neighboring country, I see that before sending an ambassador Working outside, he passed parliamentary interviews, such as in Indonesia.
The former diplomat, who has flown to study and study in more than 100 countries around the world, said that recruiting candidates for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation must dare to recruit talented and experienced people elsewhere. Talented rather than selectively in the past.
He said at least the ambassadorial candidacy should be debated by the National Assembly's foreign affairs committee before sending it to the king for his signature.
As you know, in the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, it is not certain that there are enough qualified people to appoint ambassadors.
If so, the ministry should find people from other places who have sufficient qualifications.
Because we lack human resources.
However, Hor Nambora praised current Foreign Minister Prak Sokhon, replacing his father, who has retired as a highly capable minister who could improve Cambodia's foreign policy.
In this regard, the former Ambassador Bora urged the Royal Government, especially the new Minister of Foreign Affairs, to consider establishing an Institute for the Training of Cambodian Diplomats.
Mr. Bora blamed the past for the biggest shortcomings of Cambodia's diplomacy and the lack of training to gain knowledge and share knowledge with young diplomats in the future.
Not only that, using officials different from their talents is also an issue.
We need to find the disease clearly to treat.
We need to recognize that there is a physical disease.
The son of the former top Cambodian diplomat has expressed frustration with the Cambodian Ministry of Foreign Affairs for ignoring his initiative to make institutes train Cambodian diplomats like the poor. In ASEAN and Africa.
Bora mentioned the initiative when he was ambassador to Australia and the United Kingdom at a joint meeting while his father was head of Cambodian diplomacy.
This institute is to expand and update the knowledge of diplomats, that is, every day he must seek new knowledge through training every day, so that he does not do trivial work and must know how far the world has evolved?
The establishment of this institute is to understand the world as well as new technologies.
So far, Cambodia has realized that the capacity of diplomats is weak due to the lack of institutes for training.
Nambora, meanwhile, called on all retired former Cambodian diplomats to support the initiative, using their diplomatic experience to teach talented students in the field of international relations.
He had to compile a book to train the next generation to take turns.
Mr. Hor Nambora was an advisor to the National Assembly during the term of Samdech Chea Sim, President of the National Assembly in the first term of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
He called the lack of management in diplomacy a disease that needed to be treated.
If in their country, they have training institutes every week, take this department, that department to train, and that institute has good relations with all ASEAN institutes.
They exchange.
Diplomats in Cambodia, including Japan, China and Thailand or Vietnam, are seen as eager to support their investors, especially their citizens living and doing business in Cambodia.
At the same time, the Royal Government of Cambodia has in the past instructed its diplomats to work hard on the economy and attract investors.
When I was still working in England and Ethiopia, I published a bi-monthly English-language newsletter in Ethiopian to distribute to Ethiopians to attract investment.
