But for Cambodia, there is still great potential for agriculture if Cambodia has a good water management policy.
He urged the Royal Government of Cambodia to have a long-term strategy in the fields of education, politics, water, electricity and climate change.
A long-term perspective should be taken into account according to its geographical location.
Thus, there must be a national strategy for at least 20-30 years.
He found that Cambodian farmers now have a worse life than workers in garment factories.
For a garment factory worker who earns only $ 150 a month, working for 12 months is equivalent to a farmer farming alone on six hectares of land.
Cambodian farmers lack motivation, are not very attractive, eventually abandon their farmland and run for jobs in factories or migrate abroad.
To boost the agricultural sector, the government has provided a $ 20 million package to boost and produce fragrant rice, mixed crops and vegetables to replace more than 400 tonnes of vegetables a day from Vietnam.
The next project is to consider the market for products that are a common concern of farmers.
Lack of domestic employment is the reason why more than half a million migrant workers go to work in neighboring countries.
Answering the question, what does it mean for an economy when a country has a large number of migrant workers?
Dr. Mey Kalyan replied that because we did not have a job to hire them, but also some benefits, because to work for them to get a decent salary, and also know some discipline.
Cambodia needs enough time and investment in technology and how to manage communication marketing techniques by creating a system.
Have a long-term vision.
In general, there is a lot of positivity in understanding Cambodia.
The government's policy is to make things clearer and investors are more aware, but we have to move forward.
It should be noted that Mr. Mey Kalyan is the son of a teacher in a middle-class family in Kampong Cham province. Mr. Kalyan was born in 1953, the year that His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk led to demand independence from the French colony.
In 1971, he studied at Sisowath School and continued his studies at the Techno School from 1972 to 1974 in Phnom Penh.
During that time, increasing pressure from Khmer Rouge forces caused the social and political upheaval of Lon Nol's republic to erupt closer to Phnom Penh, and dozens of areas were bombed. Tons from the US military as well.
Mortar shells sometimes fell near his school, leaving many civilians dead with fresh blood in their eyes.
Having lived and studied abroad for more than three decades, Dr. Mikalyan has received many important work, which is a huge asset to share with the younger generation.
As the finance officer of an import-export company, he is responsible for developing and coordinating the financing package for heavy equipment and machinery that the export company oversees and operates overseas branches and affiliates. Research on investment project opportunities and processes that emphasize from agriculture to industry and services.
He met with Hun Sen, the second Prime Minister of the first term of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
Meanwhile, as an agricultural technician for the United Nations and the World Bank, Kalyan has rebuilt relations in Cambodia.
For personal reasons, he asked for a two-year suspension from the Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO).
He has also been assigned to work on JICA, a Japanese bilateral aid agency for agricultural and rural development.
As part of this work, he participated in identification missions in Cambodia and Myanmar and studied regional master plans in Thailand.
His main task is to study Thai agriculture, identify potentials and obstacles to agricultural development, introduce agricultural development policies, and propose projects and programs for agricultural development.
From 1988 to 2008, he was a Senior Economist in the Cooperation Program between the World Food Program and the World Bank.
For 18 years, he has participated and led many missions for investment projects, including strategic plans, finance, the sector and rural sub-sectors, as well as various economic development policies.
Work in policy and the rural sector includes the review of financial, agricultural and economic policies, as well as the various implications of national and development plans.
In total, he has participated in 60 missions covering 15 countries in Asia, 14 countries in Africa and one country in Eastern Europe.
From 2008 to the present, he has been the Senior Advisor to the Supreme National Economic Council, which has the same status as a Minister.
It is responsible for formulating policies for national economic development and advising the Prime Minister.
He has contributed to the institution's recent policy work, including the promotion of rice yields and rice exports, the Rectangular Strategy Phase III, the Industrial Development Policy Vision 2030.
Dr. Kalyan has been appointed First Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Royal University of Phnom Penh.
He speaks and writes French, English, Japanese, Chinese and Italian fluently.
Mr. Ngin Sao Rath from a disabled person selling noodles to a director of a dream organization
There are so many people with disabilities in Cambodia that we can see them in cities, on the streets and in the countryside.
They are affected by war, unexploded ordnance, accidents and various diseases.
Ngin Sao Rath was born on December 17, 1971 in Kirivong district, Takeo province, to a farming family of eight brothers and sisters.
He is the only member of his family who has been paralyzed in one leg since the age of five due to illness, and at that time Cambodia was in a state of disarray and was under the Khmer Rouge regime, leaving him incurable.
During that time, he did not study on time like today's children because he had to flee to other areas like other people.
However, after the end of the Pol Pot regime in 1979, his family came to Phnom Penh and took up the business of making noodles to sell, so he was able to enter the first grade at the age of 10.
He said that when he was studying, some of his friends took our disability for fun, sometimes he took off our pants and ran to play, but it was difficult to blame him because he was too young to understand what a disability is called discrimination. But my dad always encouraged me to study.
Despite his disability and discrimination, he persevered and helped his parents make a living until he was able to continue his high school education.
But one day, when he saw his sister working for a high-paying organization, he began to dream of joining the same job as her while he was looking for a job at the Cambodian Disabled People's Organization. Ambitious to sit on the board of directors to protect the rights and raise the voice of the disabled to the government.
Because of that dream, he tried to study English, study teaching at IFL and work at the National Center for the Disabled until he had the opportunity to receive a scholarship to study in Japan.
After completing his scholarship, Mr. Ngin Sau Roth began to participate in the movement for the protection of the rights and promotion of persons with disabilities with the Cambodian Organization of Persons with Disabilities during the end of 2001 as a board member, at which time the organization faced almost internal problems. Out of breath because there is only $ 4,000 left in the aid budget.
Seeing the fragility of managing this organization, he decided to run for CEO, which is one of his dream positions, and he achieved this goal in 2003 with his own ability.
However, he sees that the current situation of people with disabilities has improved because people with disabilities are being taken care of by the head of the Royal Government, who is also disabled, while Cambodia There are laws and conventions on the rights of people with disabilities, as well as quotas for state institutions that provide opportunities for 2% of people with disabilities to work.
If the central government officials do as well as Samdech, I believe that our disabled people will be very good.
He also urged central government officials to speed up the mechanism of providing 20,000 riels per month to people with disabilities, because the government has reduced the money for many years, but so far people with disabilities have not received a penny. Sen yet.
Under the leadership of Mr. Ngin Sau Rath, the Cambodian Disabled People's Organization currently has 65 channels across 25 capitals and provinces, has a radio station for the disabled in three provinces, cranes for the disabled, and has raised between 500,000 and 1 million from donors. Millions of dollars per year.
Seeing this work, this organization has received two medals of encouragement from the head of the Royal Government and many other certificates from the international community, while Mr. Sao Rath is also the chair of the ASEAN Disability Forum, which helps. Promote the enactment of the Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities and the implementation of the Law on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities in the 10 ASEAN countries.
Despite the many successes he has led the Cambodian Disabled People's Organization for the past 13 years, Ngin Sao Roth has not intended to hold on to this position forever, as he plans to turn workers into traders to provide Opportunity for the younger generation to lead this organization.
Yang Sophorn vows to protect workers' rights despite being shot in the head
This is just one of her many advocacy efforts to demand that the National Assembly postpone the passage of a law that some unions see as restricting workers' right to freedom of association. The right to freedom of assembly, strike, demonstration and so on.
However, the National Assembly passed the law on April 4.
But Ms. Yang Sophorn confirmed that:
The 34-year-old woman was originally from Chroy Dong village, Samrong Thom commune, Kien Svay district, Kandal province, the son of a farmer with six siblings.
She graduated from 10th grade due to poor family living conditions.
She learned to work in a board factory from the eighth grade, but because she wanted her brothers to pursue higher education, she decided to drop out of school and work as a garment factory worker in Phnom Penh during 1994. She is not yet 18 years old.
Despite her ingenuity and skill in sewing, which made her boss and team leader happy with her work, she still saw her employers abusing her rights and forcing them to work. Overtime on Sunday, the case made her start advocating for employers to respect labor laws and end oppression of workers.
Seeing her honesty and strong will to protect workers' rights, Sophorn was supported by workers to become the local union leader of the Free Trade Union of the Kingdom of Cambodia, then under Chea Vichea. .
She was soon encouraged to participate in national work and campaigns for the benefit of workers.
But after Chea Vichea was shot dead in 2010, she decided to leave the union and form her own union at the end of 2011, now known as the Trade Union Alliance. As chairman.
On the way to work for more than 10 years, she was subjected to violence and threats from law enforcement forces who cracked down on strikes and almost lost her life when she joined a protest with workers.
Despite the crackdown on workers' strikes, Sophorn still sees the situation of the workers at that time as better than now, as some protests now include cracking down and suing the strike leaders. The court, although the strike was in accordance with the law.
But on the issue of wages, she said it was better than before because the government provided the opportunity to negotiate once a year, while previously the opportunity to negotiate a wage increase took place every five years. No more than $ 5.
Workers may have the opportunity to negotiate for an increase in the minimum wage to $ 140, but this amount still does not support workers' livelihoods due to rising market prices and rents, and workers have to pay for water, electricity and labor costs. Journey and so on.
She believes that the government should conduct a study on wages in line with economic inflation and market prices so that workers can live decently, and the Ministry of Labor should strengthen inspections to reduce risks. Various on workers.
But if we find a problem and we do not dare to tell the truth, we always hide to help the factory to accept some bribes, our workers still have the same problem, that problem will not be solved for life because we do not try In enforcing existing laws.
Returning to her life journey, despite many obstacles in her work, she continued to show a strong position and continue to strive for the garment workers in Cambodia, while she received a lot of encouragement from other union leaders. The family and the international community, especially her two sons and daughters.
For the future, Yang Sophorn does not intend to hold any position in state institutions or be a member of parliament for any party, although she has more than 10,000 workers from 30 factories supporting her work activities. Even today.
She remains committed to continuing to work as a representative of workers in advocating for the benefit of workers in the interests of the workers and to remove the oppression of the workers by the employers.
But I am still happy, satisfied and want to do this job so that workers can live decently, I do not want to see the oppression and exploitation of the poor to the rich.
Mr. Chea Phalarin from Orphan to General Director of Amret
Mr. Chea Phalarin was born in Kampong Siem district, Kampong Cham province.
Mr. Thom knew and studied at Skun High School.
He graduated from the Faculty of Economics in Phnom Penh in 1991.
During the Khmer Rouge regime, he worked in the children's unit away from home from his parents.
He was born into a poor family, with his father working as a primary school teacher.
His father was captured and killed by Khmer Rouge guerrillas.
I help my mother make a living by buying textbooks.
My mother wanted me to stop studying because she had no hope.
I finally got to work when I weighed about 47 pounds.
From 1991 to 1995, he worked as a civil servant at the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
Mr. Phalarin then joined the GRET credit project in collaboration with the Ministry of Agriculture.
As a credit officer, he developed a credit product within the organization's project framework.
In 1997, he was promoted to CEO of Amret, which could become a commercial bank in the future.
As of the first quarter of 2016, Amret operates across 25 provinces and the capital with a network of 138 branches and more than 3,700 employees.
Amret currently has total assets of US $ 556 million, total capital of US $ 88 million, net profit compared to 33% of capital.
Loans in the hands of customers amounted to US $ 478 million, savings of customers amounted to US $ 227 million and a total of more than 406,200 clients.
Amret's loan portfolio and deposits increased by about 16% and 13% respectively compared to the end of 2015.
This growth reflects the public's trust in the microfinance institution.
Credit risk greater than 30 days was low at 0.16 percent, compared to the average credit risk of 0.74 percent.
In a recent interview with the Phnom Penh Post regarding the status of small and medium-sized loans, Mr. Chea Phalarin, General Director of Amret Microfinance Institution, recalled that the credit service situation more than 10 years ago is 100 different now. Percentage.
GRET, a technology research group for palm oil boilers and toilets, saw the real need of the poor: micro-credit for business expansion, and Gret began piloting a 100,000 riel loan. Interest is 5 percent per month, while interest-bearing loans from village lenders charge up to 10 or 20 percent per month.
In order to join the Royal Government to improve the lives of farmers, Amret must work according to specific principles and professionally to protect the interests of its customers from various risks.
Credit officers need to make sure that the loans that people take to build their families' livelihoods are in line with Amret's vision and mission.
However, if the company grows alone, but the client suffers, loses his home, sells his land, or uses child labor in his family, that is not Amret's goal.
As a proponent of moral and professionalism, Phalarin warned that inaccurate governance could have a negative impact on the population.
Each MFI or manager must have a clear code of conduct to provide credit to the people, not just to raise money for the company to grow, but to make its customers grow. Changes in his life have benefited the family so that the company can be sustainable in the future.
Chea Phalarin agreed with Prime Minister Hun Sen last month, criticizing a number of microfinance institutions or commercial banks that were abnormally impoverished, sometimes accepting ID cards or family books. Make a guarantee.
Amret's policy is in line with the policy of the National Bank of Cambodia (NBC) in raising capital while financial institutions are mushrooming in a competitive environment to provide professional and unprofessional clients.
The high self-capital of the company makes it stable, with the ability to repay debts when the company has a problem or loss.
The precautionary measures taken by the National Bank of Cambodia are intended to protect the public and to make the institution more ethical.
Mr. Chea Phalarin confirmed.
According to the latest data, there are more than 50 microfinance institutions in Cambodia.
National Bank's policy of raising the capital of any commercial bank with insufficient investment capital will fall into microfinance institutions.
Institutions with less capital must merge or be able to reduce the size of operations from the establishment level to ordinary operators who are not eligible for savings.
In these difficult circumstances, Mr. Chea Phalarin explained that if an institution has a capital of more than 1 billion riels and more than 1,000 customers, it must have an investment law for microfinance institutions and must comply with the regulations of the National Bank.
He said that if you operate as a microfinance institution must fulfill some of the tasks set by the National Bank.
If a bank does not have enough capital, it can be transferred to a microfinance institution, and for a microfinance institution to become a regular operator, it means that his activities are small and do not have a big impact.
Of course, banks or microfinance institutions can grow or lose, it is related to political stability and investment climate.
For Chea Phalarin, the political environment should be applauded.
He said the political situation was nothing to worry about.
So, if politicians want to quarrel, quarrel over the microphone, debate, there is no cutting and shooting.
It is good that all political parties turn to the negotiating table or sit in one place, the National Assembly, for discussion.
Mr. Phalarin said that if the country has a strong economy, eradicates poverty, gives people freedom, prosperity and political stability will be strengthened.
Thus, if the people are poor and they are tired of other things, it causes psychological problems that can affect political stability.
The message that the General Director of Amret Microfinance Institution wants to send to the younger generation is not to succumb to poverty and be determined to alleviate poverty by seeking knowledge.
Use the time and money your mother gives you to study effectively, save the most.
I have had the idea since I was in high school that if my family was poor, if we adapted to poverty, it would be even worse for us.
Ho Vandy: Life is meaningful thanks to my grandmother
Dozens of tons of bombs dropped by US troops during the war in Cambodia in the 1970s left hundreds of thousands of Cambodian children orphaned, including Ho Vandy.
The child's beloved mother died of B52 shrapnel in Chhlong district, Kratie province, northeastern Cambodia during 1973.
During the Khmer Rouge regime, Vandy was in the ranks of the children, who were tasked with making cow dung and fertilizer.
After the liberation on January 7, 1979, Vandy attended primary school in Phnom Penh until 1984 to 1986 to live with his grandmother in Takeo province.
He returned home in 1993 like any other Cambodian.
In a recent interview with the Post, Ho Vandy described his life as one of struggle and bitterness.
At a refugee camp on the Khmer-Thai border, Vandy was temporarily adopted by paramilitaries of Son San's resistance movement.
He then crossed into Siam village with the Thai army chief's family in Chonburi province, where he was admitted to a primary school near a Thai military base.
Due to his lack of cockfighting temperament, Vandy refused to enter the boxing ring, but asked the owner to return to the camp and move back to Khao Idang camp.
In Khao I Dang, he attended secondary school.
In the camps, they are required to learn medical skills, teach Thai and English to orphans in exchange for dietary supplies, including ovantin, milk, sugar and instant noodles.
Ho Vandy never imagined returning home to see his grandmother.
Finally, in 1991, news of the repatriation spread.
During the 1993 election, he worked for the UN on human rights and was sent to work in Preah Vihear province, working with prisoners and the courts to provide human rights training to soldiers and civil servants there.
