Starting the travel agency, he initially had only three employees: himself, his wife and his brother-in-law.
Mr. Ho Vandy said that the initial start-up was very difficult because, firstly, there was a lack of capital and secondly, there was not enough experience for the sector.
He recalled that 10 years ago, appliances such as phones, computers, paper were very expensive and there was no online ticketing system.
Compared to today, the tourism industry in Cambodia is 100 percent different.
Cambodia has a total of 600 travel agencies per 15 million states.
Thailand has more than 1,000 agencies out of a total population of more than 70 million, while Vietnam has nearly 1,000 agencies out of a population of 85 million.
This policy has emerged after joining the Clean Beach Club, which has more than 30 beaches in the world.
However, Cambodia's tourism sector still faces some obstacles, such as the lack of commitment to work together and the lack of openness by professional officials.
Continuing to pursue a policy of cultural tourism, ecotourism and ecotourism as the main destination to attract foreign tourists is reasonable, but does not mean that everything is purely cultural or purely natural.
In general, cultural tourism and eco-tourism only attract old tourists, but young tourists may have other ways to attract.
Diversification from creative tourism is a must.
Cambodia's island development policy, on the other hand, needs to encourage domestic and international investors to access investment rights.
But if he gets the right and does nothing, it means that the flow of tourists is restricted.
Confiscation of tourists means disrupting the flow of money they want to spend.
So if we stop this money from circulating, it means that we are disrupting the same economic flow.
The president of the tour company urged the authorities to strictly enforce the law because some tourists are often seen taking Cambodian territory, trafficking drugs or committing child pornography.
However, after Cambodia became a member of many international organizations, Cambodia also helped prevent and crack down on sex tourism, until guesthouses, hotels were labeled as having no sexual services or sexual intercourse. On children and so on.
Tourism has contributed significantly to the national economy.
Then tourists need food and accommodation.
Cambodia has some shortcomings in the field of tourism: Cambodian guides do not receive a decent income compared to Korean guides.
The government's reluctance to attract foreign investors will also lead to a loss of benefits for local investors.
Mr. Vandy warned that the attitude of exploiting tourists by buying and selling fraudulent products and souvenirs also needs to be avoided.
Even though Cambodia is a member of Clean Beaches, it is observed that the lack of waste management capacity in Cambodia's beautiful islands will be severely affected by people throwing garbage into the water or the beach.
The authorities should have the authority to maintain cleanliness and the environment in that area, especially to prevent the dumping of garbage in the open field.
Tourism services are closely linked to political stability in the region as well as in individual countries.
The success of the life of the director of this travel agency based on five factors: First, adhere to morality, ethics, etiquette and virtue.
Third, build a good relationship with the family, especially the wife.
Hoeun Sopheap is committed to protecting the forest for life
The award is an initiative for outstanding local and indigenous communities from around the world who promote innovative solutions for indigenous peoples and communities.
In addition to this award, the Prey Lang Community Network also received another $ 25,000 award from the New York-based Foundation on September 26, 2013 for forest conservation work in Prey Lang. Which suffered severe deforestation.
Prey Lang forest protection activist Hoeun Sopheap has been actively involved in forest crime patrols and advocacy as a representative of the Prey Lang community, especially in Kampong Thom province, and has the courage to speak out against the lax actions of the Prey Lang community. Authorities inactive in preventing forest crime.
Since becoming an orphan, his father had to climb palm trees to make palm sugar, sell it and enter Prey Lang to find non-timber forest products such as vegetables, fruits and roots to support his education and the livelihood of a poor family.
Mr. Sopheap says:
Seeing the deforestation in Prey Lang at the time, he decided to join the Prey Lang Community Network in 2006 to conduct a direct forest patrol to collect illegal timber and chainsaws and hand them over to authorities. Local as well as relevant institutions.
Prey Lang covers four provinces: Kampong Thom, Stung Treng, Preah Vihear and Kratie, with more than 200,000 communities living in 339 villages in six districts that depend on non-timber forest products for their livelihoods.
Prey Lang has 530 species of plants, 60 species of mammals, 226 species of birds, 67 species of mammals and 67 species of mammals.
This is according to the website of Prey Lang Community Network.
The Prey Lang Community Network has been operating since 2000 without the cooperation of the authorities or with the help of any association.
They have also been accused by authorities of being unlicensed communities and threatened and obstructed during forest patrols.
Through quarterly forest patrols and community meetings, Mr. Sopheap saw images of forest crime in Prey Lang, such as the granting of economic and social land concessions, the granting of factories or companies. Some set up logging sites in the forest by the government, illegal logging activities of traders and some local authorities, and activities where traders hired local people to cut down trees directly.
While on a mission to protect natural resources, Hoeun Sopheap and other community networks have been under frequent threats, receiving summonses and arrest warrants from the military police and Sandan district authorities, accusing him of being a gang member. Opposition.
As part of the efforts of the Prey Lang community network, partner organizations and relevant institutions, the last remaining 300,000 hectares of forest were declared a protected area by the head of the Royal Government in early 2016.
In Mr. Sopheap's view, he was not optimistic that the government would be able to protect Prey Lang from deforestation by traders 100 percent if the government did not include the Prey Lang community component in its working group.
But if the community network participates, he knows the real situation, he knows the problems that arise, he knows what is lost every day, because we are the owners of the forest, if the Ministry of Environment protects it alone, it is far away. It is full of action, it is corrupt from all those groups.
There are many illegal activities, but their arrests are very small.
Returning to his personal position, Mr. Sopheap is committed to using the time he has in his life to protect forests and natural resources forever, whether as a community worker or as a forest crime investigator for a partner organization.
Bun Yin: The key to my success is respect for discipline
Born in 1956 in Phnom Penh and educated in Phnom Penh during the Sangkum Reastr Niyum regime of former King Norodom Sihanouk and the Republican regime of General Lon Nol.
His parents are from Krouch Chhmar district, Kampong Cham province.
The advent of the Pol Pot regime on April 17, 1975, prevented thousands of Cambodian students from completing their professional courses in banking and finance, including Bun Yin. Unfortunately, not as highly educated as today's students.
Bun Yin was unlucky enough to study abroad like other students, but spent almost half of his life in the banking and finance profession from 1981 to 2016.
CIMB is a regional bank headquartered in Malaysia and has hundreds of branches in almost all ASEAN member countries except the Philippines and Myanmar.
In an interview with the Post, Bun Yin revealed the secret of the key to his career success as a financial services provider in Cambodia for more than three decades: that discipline is a key principle and a stepping stone to success. Go from low to high with responsibility as a staff leader.
Commenting on his work history and private life, Mr. Bun Yin stated that he started working in the banking profession from 1981-1992 in the Central Bank of Cambodia during his tenure as Deputy Director.
In January 2015, he was promoted from Deputy Director General to Chief Executive Officer.
Regarding the banking profession, especially management, Mr. Bun Yin said that he always encounters many big and small obstacles, but he knows how to solve all problems successfully.
Currently, his bank is not only competing for business, but also for recruiting staff.
In the past, banks only competed with the same commercial banks, but now commercial banks compete with microfinance institutions.
There are currently nine microfinance institutions in Cambodia with almost the size of commercial banks.
Responding to the view that lenders always make money and borrowers from banks or microfinance institutions are getting poorer, Bun Yin explained that in his own observation, the role of banks is not Not only to make a profit from customers, but also to advise customers.
When it is effective, they profit.
That is how we can grow together.
An optimistic manager, Bun Yin has reflected on the growth of the banking sector over the last 10 years, saying that the growth in the banking sector was unexpected.
According to the 2005-2015 figures, the number of commercial banks increased from 19 to 47 and the number of microfinance institutions increased from 16 to 58.
Cambodia had no stock market in 2005 but now has a stock market.
Thus, this growth, it has grown significantly.
According to the data, Cambodia is a civilized country and banks are a tool for economic growth.
Ah, it all reflects the progress of our country.
Thus, if the growth of the banking sector to more cashless payments, it can help expand the scope of transparency better, and then the society, the reflection of justice, I believe it can happen. Up then.
As a strategist and long-term visionary, Yin understands that the number of banks and financial institutions in Cambodia is not enough to meet the needs of more than 15 million people.
Cambodia still has room for growth in this area and has been providing low interest rates to customers.
To help protect customers from risks, the National Bank of Cambodia in March issued a decision to increase investment capital for financial operators.
Mr. Bun Yin praised the National Bank for requiring commercial banks, specialized banks and microfinance institutions to increase their investment capital, as growth has reached a stage where not only quantity but also quality should be considered.
With more capital, banks can be able to do business and insure against any potential risks.
The National Bank wants to promote that work by having all commercial banks protect their customers.
However, Bun Yin praised the development of the banking sector in Cambodia in the past.
In the event that Cambodia continues to be as peaceful as it is now, investors will definitely need to invest in long-term consumption, helping to create jobs and boost economic growth.
According to the 60-year-old, in the coming decades, the momentum of the advancement of banking and finance will be no different from the growth of the 20th and 21st centuries.
As productivity increases, I believe development progresses.
CIMB policies provide trust and empowerment and value, as well as trust in local staff.
That's something I should be very proud of, as well as for my staff.
Has a total capital of about 50 million US dollars.
It has lent more than $ 260 million to customers and received more than $ 300 million in deposits.
The CEO said he was optimistic about the future, stating that he believed the work would go well because my staff were all Khmer, able to talk to each other and honest with the workplace. And the main bank is not a machine or money, it is important that people work, how can they be honest with us?
Because they want to fulfill the ambition of making rural Cambodia a green development area in all seasons and make farmers become millionaires in the countryside and want to change the old-fashioned politics that clashes only with personal matters. Instead of discussing national development policy issues, Dr. Yang Saing Koma decided to resign from the position of CEDAC Executive Director to join the newly formed Local Democrat Party.
After completing his PhD in Agriculture in Germany in 1995, Dr. Yang Saing Koma returned to Cambodia and became involved in agriculture and rural development through a number of international organizations from Japan and France, as well as being a professor. Lecturer at the Royal University of Coconut Farming.
But two years later, in August 1997, Komar decided to form a Cambodian organization called the Cambodian Center for Agricultural Research and Development (CEDAC).
CEDAC works directly with farmers, helping to train them in modern, high-yield farming skills.
In an interview with the Phnom Penh Post on April 5, 2016 at the Local Democrat Party headquarters in Phnom Penh Thmey, Sen Sok, Mr. Komar said:
Currently, Cambodia, with a total population of nearly 15 million, is about 85% farmers.
But the country's agricultural sector is still in crisis, forcing many farmers to migrate to cities for work or venture across the border to find work in some countries. .
According to Komar, the current crisis in Cambodia's agricultural sector can be divided into two, the first is the human resources factor and the second is the factor around human resources.
The big problem is that it has to do with the tradition of Cambodian farmers, who mostly grow rice just for their livelihood, as this is the era of a free economy, a market economy where people need very high incomes, so they have to transform themselves from farmers who produce just Make a living just to be a farmer, a trader, a producer for sale to the market.
It requires modification of personal development, knowledge, skills, attitudes, behaviors and how to work big.
Hi, our farmers still lack a lot, and this lack is because we do not have human resources, there are no experts to work with them closely.
The slim, tall, fair-skinned man said that CEDAC, a small organization, did not have the resources to send enough experts to work with farmers across the country. Work on a project with foreign assistance in just 3 to 5 years.
This is the first set of problems, and the second set of problems, he said, farmers need outside help around the production environment, especially investment in irrigation, transportation, capital and other issues. Others to help farmers have sufficient production capacity.
Currently, Cambodia has up to four ministries working in rural areas: the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, the Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology, the Ministry of Environment and the Ministry of Rural Development.
However, Dr. Yang Saing Komar sees that none of these ministries are capable of directly assisting farmers.
The problem is that not all ministries do their job well, which means that work is still very limited, especially the Ministry of Agriculture itself does not have the capacity to send good officials and experts to work. Directly with the farmers in the villages and communes yet, we still lack a lot of human resources to work directly with the farmers.
Mr. Komar found many strategic tips on how to help Cambodian farmers across the country, to help Cambodian agriculture grow, to help Cambodian farmers who want to pursue a successful agricultural career.
Komar wants to incorporate these key findings into a national agricultural policy program that focuses on rural development by empowering farmers to become entrepreneurs to become food producers. Earn high income and to supply the whole society instead of importing to increase exports.
In the past, he used to advocate for these ideas through the government through the Ministry of Agriculture, but less effectively, so he decided to resign from CEDAC to join the Local Democrat Party.
More effectively, Komar explained that in the past, when he mentioned the key points that could help the agricultural sector grow, it was recognized as good, but they were less busy and less willing to do it because they said that if they Not doing is okay.
Thus, it will be possible for people to have a second choice, it may be more pressure than just us advocating through us at civil society organizations.
Currently, Komar is in charge of training local leaders of the Local Democrat Party.
The Local Democrat Party is recruiting village and commune leaders and training them to strengthen their ability to become good leaders to participate in the 2017 Commune / Sangkat Council elections.
In addition to fulfilling his ambitions for the growth of the agricultural sector and for farmers to become millionaires, Komar's involvement in political parties also wants to show people that politics It is normal for ordinary people to be able to do politics, it is not necessary for the elders to have children.
In the upcoming Commune / Sangkat elections in 2017, the Local Democrat Party will contest only in 100 communes and is expected to win as commune chief in 20 communes and lead all 20 communes. That will become a model commune with democratic development, local development with good governance, focusing on a number of areas to make people prosperous, live a dignified life without fear.
The Local Democrats stand out more than the CPP, which has been in power for more than 30 years, and the CNRP, which rose sharply in the 2013 election in that it focuses on the values of unity. Justice and non-violence.
When we campaign, during the election, it is up to each of us, but at the end of the election, each of us must think of the nation and society together.
In addition, the Local Democrat Party focuses heavily on rural development.
Mr. Komar stated:
Cambodia grows until our human resources grow.
The ongoing political heat comes after the leaders of the two elected parties used divisive rhetoric, which children called old-fashioned politics. And individuals to gain fame, self-popularity only, there is no benefit to the nation.
Yang Saing Koma was born in 1966 in Banteay Chakpol village, Popel commune, Tram Kak district, Takeo province, to his father as a teacher, but died during the Khmer Rouge regime.
Komar was married in 2006 and now has three children, two daughters.
Komar likes to follow the political situation, especially the situation in society from an early age, because he started to like reading newspapers when he was literate.
Komar considers himself one of the most successful men in life, having completed his PhD and during his time in Germany he was awarded the Outstanding Student Award three times by working at At CEDAC, he also won the 2012 Nobel Prize in Asia.
However, he is a person who wants to do something new and big to help society.
So, first of all, I want to see every farmer have the opportunity to become rich, to become rich in other countries, just like in Europe, in Australia, farmers are rich, even in Thailand, they start.
In addition, Mr. Yang Saing Komar wants every citizen to have good health care services and wants to see every Cambodian child, both rich and poor, get good education, have good teachers Schools are as good as in developed countries.
Mr. Vath Chamroeun: Cambodia is preparing to host the SEA Games
Mr. Vath Chamroeun evolved from an athlete to an expert in sports activities
Mr. Vath Chamroeun can be considered as a person who has achieved great success in life, he tried to develop from an ordinary athlete to become an expert in sports activities of the National Olympic Committee of Cambodia and has a position The Royal Government is the Under Secretary of State for the Ministry of Tourism.
Before becoming the manager of such a high-level sports institution, Mr. Vath Chamroeun started as a trainer in various martial arts such as karate, zambo and wrestling and used to be a representative of Cambodia. Participated in the 1996 International Olympic Games in Atlanta, USA.
Mr. Vath Chamroeun's father was a blacksmith in the pre-Pol Pot regime, and his youngest son lived a miserable life under the Pol Pot regime.
He grew up after 1979 and became involved in sports from an early age. During 1983, he gradually became involved in sports until he became an expert in sports, while the whole family was bloodless. Not sports at all.
With practical experience in sports activities, Mr. Vath Chamroeun has studied General Knowledge, Master of Business Administration and Sports Administration, Sports Science, Sports Science, Sports Medicine and is also a Professor of French.
In an interview related to sports activities, Mr. Vath Chamroeun said:
In 1987, Chamroeun won two black karate belts and competed in the Soviet Union for the first time in 1989.
In 1994, one year after the UNTAC-led coalition government led by two prime ministers, the Ministry of Education sent Vat Chamroeun to wrestling in North Korea for two years. Years back and forth, saving good credit for both theory and practice.
In 2001, Vath Chamroeun studied sports and theory in France, earning a degree in sports teacher, sports psychologist and general sports teacher.
He went on to study at the University of Breton in the United States, earning a master's degree in administration and business, a master's degree, and a professor of French from the University of Phnom Penh in 1995.
Mr. Vath Chamroeun was an outstanding student of literature throughout the country in 1989.
Regarding the current state of sports, Mr. Chamroeun explained that due to the economic growth, people have a better life, and this wealthy people have led to an increase in sports activities.
Mr. Chamroeun said that Cambodia currently has 47 sports and the public is actively participating in sports.
He said that in the movement of sports, the progress of activities depends on the preferences of people in the area or traditional customs, so the progress is not the same.
He said that in the ASEAN region, which has 10 member countries, Cambodia has been ranked eighth and ninth since 2009.
In the vision of Cambodia and strive to push Cambodia to move from 7th to 5th place in more than 7 years, which is a master plan that we have already prepared and waiting for approval from the Royal Government In that, sending athletes to train abroad and hiring foreign experts to train locally is an important factor to pay attention to.
Sports in which Cambodia can win international medals are mostly individual disciplines such as wrestling, wushu, wrestling, taekwondo and tennis, and Cambodian football is now becoming more and more popular. Let football advance to the semi-finals of the ASEAN Games, the SEA Games.
However, Cambodia has some popular sports that have not yet won medals but are still popular.
The first challenge is human resources: our coaches do not yet have the standards to be supplemented by internationally developed languages and techniques.
Second, the management structure, such as training grounds, stadiums, and sports equipment, is not as modern as in other countries.
Regarding the prize money for athletes who win international medals through inter-ministerial prakas or sub-decree, Mr. Vath Chamroeun explained:
Vietnam, Laos, Myanmar, Brunei have less prize money than us and Thailand and Singapore have more bonuses than us, but the prize money of some countries is mostly from the private sector, which sells the rights of outstanding athletes, and Cambodia has gradually And like Sorn Seavmey, the private sector helped tens of thousands of dollars after winning the historic Asian Taekwondo medal.
Regarding marketing relations, seeking sports support from the private sector, Mr. Chamroeun stated:
As for the condition of the National Olympic Stadium, it is a strong old building with a large yard, which is the work of the famous Khmer architect, Mr. Vann Molyvann, which was built during the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era, but is currently being torn down to build a new building. Mr. Vath Chamroeun explained: "Modernity has almost completely lost its original character."
He said that our national stadium in the center of the city used to have a lot of land, and economic development, it affects each other because the land is high, they are used as a useful economic income in sports.
He added that the circumstances were different than before, Phnom Penh had only tens of thousands of people, but now there are more than 2 million.
Everything is developed accordingly, which does not affect the sports technique, because the venue does not change.
This is an economic factor that must respond to the country's growth.
Regarding the construction of a new modern stadium called Moradok Techo with more than $ 150 million in Chinese aid, Mr. Vath Chamroeun said:
Mr. Vath Chamroeun added that China helped build the stadium as a grant, it is no secret because China used to help build stadiums for Laos to Myanmar before they hosted the ASEAN Games. .
But it is small, not big, modern, like in Cambodia, with an Angkor-style, ship-like style, which shows the ancient Sino-Khmer relations, which is a treasure for our whole nation to use until the future.
He said that the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports has a job that does not overlap with the Olympics, but to manage the national team, coach, train and send to the Olympics to send to international competitions through programs that are already set at the regional, continental level. And world class.
Vath Chamroeun was married in 1999 to a two-time black belt judoka and now has three children, one girl.
His wife is currently working as a lawyer.
Nuon Phy is an outstanding woman who works to help poor children
Due to poor families, many children have taken up the business of collecting garbage and other waste in some areas in Phnom Penh, such as along the river, the former Stung Meanchey landfill site and the landfill site in Dangkor district.
The children do not go to school as they have to dig up piles of rotten rubbish every day to help support poor families.
Seeing this tragic situation, a woman named Nuon Phiman, a former civil society worker, decided to quit her job and set up her own organization to help educate and alleviate the suffering of children's families. Those poor.
Ms. Nuon Phy is the founder of the People's Promotion Organization, a native of Kampong Cham.
She was born on January 20, 1971, the youngest daughter in a family of two siblings.
She now has two sons and a daughter with a Canadian husband.
Throughout her life, she also experienced the taste of pain due to torture, starvation, forced labor day and night during the Khmer Rouge genocide for 3 years, 8 months and 20 days, when she was in Childhood yet.
Despite surviving the Dark Ages after 1979, she did not have a better life because of the hardships of daily life due to poor family life and the separation of her parents and siblings. The distant district left her two-year-old niece for her to take care of.
During that time, she, who was only 10 years old, lived with her sick mother and niece, taking time off from school to help her mother with her business and at night copying and selling story books.
But before he died, he told me that no matter how poor you are, you have to struggle to finish school.
While struggling to make ends meet and earn a living for herself and her niece, Phiman received word from her sister that she had been evacuated to Khao Idang camp in Thailand, and she went to see her sister there. To let you see your parents' faces.
It was then that she began her involvement in social work, working for the United Nations Human Rights Commission, and went on to study English and other skills.
In 2002, Nuon Phiman resigned from civil society and started an organization called PIO to establish a school and set up a school near the former Stung Meanchey landfill to help poor children living in homeless places. Street and children pick up trash After she met a group of homeless children walking around the river to pick up trash and scramble to pick up the chicken bones she had eaten and throw them in the trash to eat.
She described with a sigh:
When I see this, I feel very sorry for them, because I remember when I was young, when I did not have my parents living alone, I struggled hard to earn money to buy food.
Ms. Phiman graduated from the University of Management and received several scholarships to study abroad.
She said opening a school that initially had to be self-funded near the dump site was a good thing for children of poor people who could not afford to go to public school.
Initially, the school building for the People's Promotion Organization, where Ms. Phy Mean paid for her own children to study, had only four rooms, and the children did not have a place to stay to set up a table and chairs. Place after school to arrange a place to rest.
In addition, the People's Promotion Organization receives a license from the Ministry of Education to teach in accordance with the curriculum, and students who graduate from this school are recognized by the Ministry.
Today, Ms. Nuon Phiman has been providing education from kindergarten to Bakdup, providing both food and health services to about 1,400 children in the Stung Meanchey landfill, slum construction in Borei Keila and Borey Santepheap 2 in Phnom Penh.
Her organization also supported 50 HIV-positive families.
Due to being able to fulfill her dreams and do social work to the point of setting up her own organization to help poor children get an education and avoid littering, Nuon Phiman was selected. She was a CNN heroine in 2008 out of 5,000 contestants at the time.
Despite her many successes and the admiration of the wives of government leaders, she continues to do humanitarian work willingly to help these poor children, with no intention of turning to government jobs or Doing nothing else.
She continued:
Minister: Not deforestation in Cambodia causes climate change
Minister: Let me be honest, deforestation in Cambodia is not causing climate change.
Last week, readers really knew who the Minister of Environment was.
In an interview with the Post, Environment Minister Say Sam Al blamed the eight industrialized nations for the biggest emissions of climate change and global warming in this context. Cambodia is required to be a non-industrialized country to participate in protecting the environment within the regional and global framework.
The eight industrialized nations, or G8, are France, Germany, Italy, Britain, Japan, the United States, Canada and Russia.
Currently, climate change and global warming are global concerns, but Cambodia, a small, sparsely populated, non-industrialized country, is being driven by the eight developed countries and the international community to contribute to global warming. Earth and climate change by stopping deforestation and protecting biodiversity.
At the same time, emerging economies such as Brazil, South Africa, India and China have not slowed down their economies because it is in the national interest.
The minister said that Cambodia should do everything to serve the national interest like those countries.
In fact, we are still in a negative circle.
According to the vision of the Cambodian Minister, there must also be a share of shared responsibility.
But in a developed country, he has to be clear.
In Cambodia, the independent media and the international community have frequently reported on the massive logging in the provinces where the state has granted land concessions to investment companies with the intention of turning tens of thousands of hectares of forest land into agro-industrial land. .
But massive and uncontrolled logging has led the international community to blame Cambodia's deforestation as contributing to climate change, hurricanes, erratic rains, as well as lightning, droughts and floods.
He said that, in fact, land concessions had to be cleared to grow rubber.
Cutting down some wood is necessary.
For example, cutting 20 to 30 trees in one hectare of land does not cause the loss of the entire forest.
Not on my Cambodian side, I still have more forests than foreign dignitaries, but this is very unfair to me.
He warned that the West, civil society and NGOs should not come to teach Cambodian children to understand that way.
We have come a long way and we should see the light for the Cambodian people to move forward as a whole society.
Although Cambodia is less consensual with the international community and local civil society, Cambodia needs to make some reforms by redefining the National Park and Wildlife Sanctuary.
The reforms come after the Royal Government of Cambodia's policy of granting economic land concessions did not bear fruit.
The ELC policy has angered tens of thousands of families over the encroachment on their land by a number of local and foreign developers.
The Minister stressed that the protection of Cambodia's natural resource group is a task that needs to be addressed and must be done in stages.
In connection with this long-running dispute, the Minister requested the head of the Royal Government to set up a commission to review all land concessions and finalize them within a year.
This work is complete, with more than 200 companies owning land under the Ministry of Agriculture and the Ministry of Environment.
At least 40 land concessions under the Ministry of Environment and the Ministry of Agriculture have been abolished.
After reviewing the land concessions, the Ministry of Environment reduced the number of years of investment and resolved the issue of social land concessions for some people, in addition to the Royal Government's Regulation No. 001, which cleared more than 1 million plots.
The Minister stated:
Cambodia now has a population of 15 million.
In 50 years, we will probably have 30 million people.
So we need to seize the opportunity to ensure food security for the Cambodian people.
Arguing until you forget where you went or what?
Responding to a question from CNRP lawmaker Oum Sam An, he wrote an inquiry letter to Prime Minister Hun Sen last week on suspicion that some 40,000 hectares of land concessions in Ratanakkiri province bordering Vietnam were controlled by the Vietnamese People's Army. Minister Say Sam Al explained:
However, he confirmed that in Cambodia, we have never allowed foreign troops to stand in our territory, which is a common principle and under our constitution.
Regarding the necessary measures that the Minister must take is to collect all legal and illegal timber, all fraudulently put into state property.
Currently, a commission headed by General Sao Sokha, Commander of the National Gendarmerie, is continuing to investigate and take action.
Now, let us not let the commission headed by His Excellency Sao Sokha work first, and we have a decision as to how far this will go.
As for the timber that has already been cut in the concession or outside the concession, a commission set up and chaired by General Sao Sokha and the Ministry of Environment thinks that the timber should be sold at auction and that it is a good idea to raise money for the state. .
However, no report from the Commission on the Suppression of Illegal Logging has yet been seen.
Previous research has found that some companies have cleared forest land fraudulently, so the Ministry has repealed it and imposed fines.
Some companies are not at fault, just lower the price of rubber to investors do not have the courage to continue.
The Minister assessed that according to the original idea of the economic land concession policy, it is a good idea of the Royal Government of Cambodia to turn the remaining degraded land into non-industrial agricultural land by planting processed crops and considered It is another economic factor to drive growth, but unfortunately implementation has encountered many obstacles that need to be resolved to end all conflicts.
But at least 20-30,000 hectares of land in Cambodia has been planted with rubber, both by companies and privately owned.
Management of Land Concessions and Natural Resources The Ministry of Environment has already transferred the type of land for agriculture to the Ministry of Agriculture.
The Ministry of Agriculture has also transferred all animal conservation land to the Ministry of Environment.
Therefore, the state needs to re-evaluate which lands are absolutely untouched and which lands can be reserved for future use to ensure food security for Cambodians.
The Ministry of Environment plans to designate Prey Lang as a wildlife sanctuary.
As for the land that is the village of the people, the state will allocate it to the people by registering it clearly.
To make this work more accurate in the near future, the Ministry of Environment will hold a workshop by inviting civil society, NGOs, experts and all relevant ministries to advise on proper land management, reconsidering so as to Determine the location so that there will be no more conflicts.
The minister acknowledged that much of the conflict in Cambodian society stemmed from land grabbing, with some residents colluding with local authorities to steal state land for sale, and some companies encroaching on residential land. People, too.
Cambodia is not only a forest, which is a national treasure, but also many other minerals such as gold, gems, oil, gas, gravel, etc. that need to be well managed to stimulate economic growth.
He said the task ahead was to take action against the illegal dredging business, which generally caused the riverbanks to collapse, which he acknowledged was wrong.
Mr. Sam Al suggested that all sand dredgers should cooperate with local authorities and the Ministry of Skills to extract resources in a technical manner, and that this sand will flow in again.
He stressed that the illegal sand dredging business must be stopped.
Sand is a resource that can serve the national interest, but there have been cases in the past where some of his brothers pumped until the bank collapsed, affecting people's homes.
What is the distance from the bank, when is the pump?
Other impacts that the Ministry of Environment and relevant ministries should address include Laos' Don Sahong dam, for which Cambodia has not yet made a clear decision on the dam because it has not yet been studied. The positive and negative effects of that dam.
As for the controversial Cheay Areng area in Koh Kong province, Mr. Sam Al made it clear that the government had no plans to build hydropower in the Cheay Areng area, although Cambodia was in need of low-cost electricity. And civil society does not need to spend time arguing, leading to wasted time.
We lost a lot of opportunities to think about it and solve it.
I think it would be better if we took the time to solve other problems for our society.
The opposition of some NGOs seems to contradict the needs of the Cambodian people.
Cambodians in almost every constituency often ask their MPs to lower their electricity prices.
The electricity that Cambodians are using comes from buying from neighboring countries.
"Everywhere you look today, the tide of protectionist sentiment is flowing. It's worth it," he said.
When we build energy sources in our country, we aim to reduce the price of electricity.
According to the needs of the people, the government is building power sources, building power lines, which is scheduled to reduce electricity prices in 2017-2018, which has already been announced by the Prime Minister.
I have my own point of view, I have to be self-reliant and I have to have a will to stand on my own two feet.
This Doctor of Biology is committed to protecting the environment and resolving all disputes or disputes between the people and all land concession owners. Say Sam Al, 36, is the youngest Minister of Environment in the fifth term of the Royal Government of Cambodia. Of Cambodia as well.
He is the son of Samdech Vibol Sena Pheakdey Say Chhum, President of the Senate of Cambodia.
He is the second child of two siblings in the family of a former teacher in Kampong Cham province's Koh Sotin district.
His father was a former teacher who volunteered for the National Liberation Army from the massacre of the Khmer Rouge in the late 1970s.
Say Chhum was the former governor of Kampong Speu in the 1980s.
He is a senior CPP official with the rank of General Secretary of the Cambodian People's Party and Vice President of the CPP.
At present, he has the title of Samdech Vibol Sena Pheakdey Say Chhum.
Samdech Vibol Sena Pheakdey Say Chhum has two children, a girl and a boy, Mr. Say Sam Al.
Sam Al entered primary school in Phnom Penh when he was five years old.
Say Samal was very poor when he was a child and often returned home with zero marks because he was a child who liked to play more than he studied.
In 1993, at the age of 13, Cambodia's first UN-backed election year, UNTAC, his family sent him to Singapore to study from 6th to 9th grade, learning both English and Chinese. Chinese and Malay as well.
In Singapore, he did not disappoint his parents, he worked hard until he passed the 6th to 8th grade and continued to the 9th grade, and then continued to study in the 10th to 12th grade in Australia. Biological orientation.
Say Sam Al also went on to pursue a bachelor's, master's, and doctoral degree in biology in Australia.
Mr. Say Sam Al completed his thesis on floodplains, especially on animal and plant life in the Tonle Sap Lake area of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
Mr. Sam Al came from Australia to collect data quarterly on the Tonle Sap Lake with sub-national fisheries administration officials and fishermen.
Due to Cambodia's lack of human resources, the Royal Government of Cambodia needs to recruit students studying abroad to serve the nation.
Mr. Say Sam Al returned to Cambodia in 2008 after completing his PhD in Biology and joined the Office of the Council of Ministers as Chief of the Tonle Sap Lake Authority.
Subsequently, Mr. Sam Al was promoted to Deputy Secretary-General and Secretary-General of the Office of the Council of Ministers in the fourth mandate of the Royal Government of Cambodia.
For the fifth term, Dr. Biology took over as Minister of the Environment at the age of 33 in 2013, replacing Dr. Mok Mareth, the former Minister.
He is married and has two children.
His main aspiration in life is not to be a politician or a civil servant, but his main aspiration is to be a researcher or a professor at a college abroad.
However, he is very happy to do important work for his country.
The minister has stated that he has no intention of holding a bigger position in the future.
My position here is on the Cambodian people, on the CPP, on the prime minister.
Just understand.
But coming to serve this nation because I have many brothers and sisters who died during the genocidal regime to liberate the country, and during that liberation there were also massacres during that regime.
By the 1980s, there were dead people to prevent the regime from returning, and I just saw it as my duty to help build the nation.
Mr. Say Sam Al believes in the younger generation of the CPP who will strive to serve the people.
He says he will set a good example for the next generation.
If I do not do well, the youth in the CPP may also lose their chances.
Environmental reform The task that the young minister needs to accomplish in this fifth term is to reform the environment in Cambodia.
For Mr. Say Sam Al, it is not difficult if everyone makes a commitment by turning all issues into opportunities for all.
Reform depends on human creativity.
For me, I have to sacrifice everything to make this work move forward, because now I look at it, it is different from the previous generation or the 80-90 generation then to bring peace to the country, they have to sacrifice their lives. As a soldier, as a policeman, as a brigade, you have to sacrifice your life.
All these problems are created by people, we are the same people, we can solve them.
Yeng Virak: In politics, we take our destiny in our hands
The 11-day detention in Prey Sar prison at the end of 2015 was considered by Yeng Virak to be a good experience in his life, which made him stronger and more motivated to advocate for There is respect for human rights and justice in society.
Yeng Virak, a former executive director of the Community Legal Education Center, is currently president of the Local Democrat Party.
According to the plan, in the first phase, the party will train its members in 200 communes to compete in the 2017 commune elections.
In an interview with the Post at the party's headquarters in Sen Sok district on February 29, Yeng Virak said:
Thus, we train about 200 members of our commune, probably 10,000 members, and we will select about 1,500 candidates to run in the commune elections.
In this first step, the Local Democrat Party will focus on only five provinces: Kampong Speu, Kampot, Takeo, Prey Veng, Svay Rieng or Kampong Thom, but will also focus on some communes in other provinces.
Mr. Virak said that the establishment of the Local Democrat Party also has the same concept as other Democrat parties: respect for human rights, the rule of law, the people.
But the Local Democrat Party is based on values, the first is unity, the second is justice, the third is non-violence.
To achieve this mission and this goal, we must cultivate good leaders and build the foundations of true democracy in our country.
In addition, the Local Democrat Party is different from the Cambodian People's Party and the National Rescue Party, which has seats in the National Assembly. We make internal democracy within our party. Its members make policy decisions on key party decisions.
We focus on so-called decentralized democracy, which means we take the big picture.
The party will train local activists to have the capacity, ability and resources to solve their local problems.
The president of the Local Democrat Party is only acting as a facilitator, a capacity builder, a facilitator for members and the people, not a person who has the right to make decisions or make arbitrary decisions like other party leaders.
Yeng Virak, who has a mustache and looks like a foreigner, has been the Executive Director of Community Legal Education Centers for 13 years, where he has been active in educating people in the provinces. Laws about the basic rights of citizens as stated in the law.
In addition, the Center also provides legal advice and legal assistance to people affected by various abuses, especially the violation of land and labor rights, which are hot issues in Cambodia. For many years.
Although many people who have been trained in the law can use that knowledge to protect themselves and prevent many abuses, when Yeng Virak began to analyze the overall. What I have done is useful for the community, for our target group, but to a limited extent.
Because of this and because of a lack of trust in the CPP and the CNRP, Virak and other civil society activists discussed and formed the local Democrat Party.
Mr. Virak said one by one in a soft and gentle manner that it is not enough to have only moral moral power in the economy, we have to have the authority of the authorities and only the actions of political parties that lead us to Hold the power of the state, the power of authority is the territorial authority, which we can fulfill another role as the authority to solve all the problems at the local level.
He said that his group formed this political party to solve the problems of local people and also to solve national problems.
The formation of this new political party was explained by Mr. Virak as an option that we can organize such a party to have internal democracy within the party, the principle of members, etc., and the leader acts as a facilitator, builder. Your ability to create characteristics for members like that.
The Local Democrat Party already has specific plans to train members from 200 communes and how to select 100 communes to run in the 2017 commune elections. In the 100 commune contests, the party hopes to win at least 20 commune chiefs.
Then, the party must prepare to lead the winning commune / sangkat village to be seen as a model commune / sangkat village.
In the 2018 national election, the party will make every effort to ensure that no party gets more than 50 plus one votes.
Mr. Yeng Virak confirmed that:
If the Local Democrat Party wins the election, it will form a national unity government, giving the party with seats in the National Assembly the opportunity to lead the government with a clear division of responsibilities.
Yeng Virak was born on January 8, 1961 in a middle-class family in Phnom Penh with five siblings and is the middle child.
Both during the war and until now, Virak continued to live in Cambodia, suffering from the Khmer Rouge regime like any other Cambodian.
But after the overthrow of the Khmer Rouge regime, Virak went to the Soviet Union to study pedagogical training in agricultural machinery for five years, from 1981 to 1986.
When he returned from studying in the Soviet Union, he did not work as an agricultural machinery technical trainer, which he learned, and was assigned to the Ministry of Agriculture's Agricultural Machinery Technical Office.
After working in government for five years, he returned to work for an NGO, in 1991, when the Cambodian side signed a peace agreement in Paris.
Due to his knowledge of English, because he tried to study part-time during his time in the government, Virak found it easy to find work in NGOs.
At the time, Yeng Virak was not the only one detained in prison, and Kem Sokha, the current acting president of the CNRP, was also detained.
He said that the arrest of him in prison reflects the fact that those who have suffered injustice in society do not just happen to the people, even to him as a leader of civil society organizations who work hard. Working to serve society also suffers from injustice.
However, it is something that gives me strength, because I understand that I am not a criminal, I am not a murderer, I am not a criminal. Thus, I believe in the goodness that exists within the bounds of the law in the exercise of our freedoms.
As a young man in high school, Virak never wanted to be a politician, but he wanted to be a teacher because he loved and respected his teachers because teachers had a clear program to teach students without There is no control or restriction from above.
But even though he did not work as a teacher in school or college, he did train as a legal community development trainer.
But later, in 2015, Virak changed his mindset and took another step into politics because he thought working in civil society could help people less. While you need a lot of help.
He said that many social problems need to be solved systematically, can not solve one case after another, so he came to join the establishment of this local Democratic Party.
However, the formation of political parties is not new to Mr. Virak, because during his time working for civil society organizations, he also trained citizens to understand civil and political rights.
The man with a simple life states:
San Chey is active in the fight against corruption
When talking about social issues, good governance and anti-corruption in the provision of public services, one always remembers some independent researchers and experts, including San Chey, Executive Director of the Cambodian Social Accountability Coalition (ANSA). He has been very constructive in his criticisms and has given many suggestions to the Royal Government to improve those gaps in the past.
In addition to reading novels and folk tales, he often travels with relatives to see the state of border development since childhood.
Chey also has a bitter history of going through the genocidal regime of Pol Pot between 1975 and 1979, during which time he only grew up but saw aspects of persecution and forced labor. Forced to live with hunger and evacuation during that time when the Khmer Rouge broke away from the southern border.
I heard gunshots and televised conversations about the situation on this battlefield. Sometimes the bullets were fired through the roof of the fort, and then I remember every meal, especially at noon. The group rang the bell and the people ran hand in hand, each holding a plate of rice to eat porridge mixed with cassava at the kitchen.
Although he grew up in Sre Chamkar on the border, he worked hard to learn both Khmer and English, even though in 1988-89 high school students were still required to learn Vietnamese and French. Anyway.
After graduating from high school in 1992, he continued his college education at the Royal University of Phnom Penh in Phnom Penh, during which time he did not have much money or support from his family, only his own efforts. By running a motorbike taxi and later working as an English teacher while he was a student with knowledge of this international language.
After graduating, he and a friend set up an English language course and set up a civil society development organization, but due to his passion for human resource development, social issues and an interest in governance and anti-corruption. He also decided to establish an organization called the Khmer Institute for National Development (KIND).
It was then that Mr. San Chey had the opportunity to lead a group of young people to meet His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni to inform him of the needs of the youth on the anti-corruption law and to campaign for support. From the Prime Minister and politicians involved in the anti-corruption law, while a number of civil society organizations have also campaigned to collect the fingerprints of 1 million people.
The Anti-Corruption Law was enacted in March 2010, which aims to improve the efficiency of all services, strengthen governance and the rule of law in the administration and administration of the state, and maintain basic cleanliness and justice. Necessary to develop the national society and reduce poverty.
In addition to the Anti-Corruption Law, Cambodia has also established the Anti-Corruption Unit to fight corruption in all its forms, in all sectors and at all levels throughout Cambodia, by taking action to educate, prevent and implement anti-corruption laws. There is public participation.
Those who have the power to decide, those who have the power, those who have the power to command their children can only commit corruption, so until the implementation of the anti-corruption law is well amended, the law on declaration of assets, protection of second witnesses applies. The Penal Code, which deals with corruption, is effective.
Mr. Chey now has a lot of prominence in the media, he criticized constructively and commented on the shortcomings of the Royal Government in the implementation of laws and analysis of social issues such as the implementation of road traffic law. New tax collection in the transportation sector, infrastructure problems and so on.
When asked about your outlook on life in the future, do you want to change jobs or enter a political position in the government or be a politician?
Mr. Chey answered without hesitation that he still wanted to be an independent adviser, an observer and advising the Royal Government on the gaps in governance, law enforcement and employment, regardless of the party-led government. Any politics.
Nguon Meng Tech: Remember! If the domestic labor force is full, the country will grow faster
Economists urge government to use natural resources cautiously
Mr. Nguon Meng Tech, a Cambodian-American expatriate, is currently the Director General of the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce, headed by Tycoon Kith Meng, President of the Royal Group.
Mr. Meng Tech was born in Saang district, Kandal province.
He was born in 1953, when Cambodia gained independence from French colonial rule, and studied in the Sangkum Reastr Niyum of King Norodom Sihanouk.
Nguon Meng Tech left for the United States on August 4, 1980.
Less than a year there, I joined the US Department of Health for more than four years and moved to California and studied there for four more years.
Meng Tech's life does not seem to be as difficult as that of some Cambodian expatriates.
In the United States, he studied electronics, but with little interest, Meng Tech returned to social work.
Mr. Meng Tech said that was when Duke Kith Meng invited him to be the director general of the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce to this day.
Nguon Meng Tech married his first wife in the United States with four children.
He now lives with his second wife and has a daughter.
As a social and economic expert, Meng Tech offers the view that Cambodia is a long way from building a country in a peaceful environment.
He said that the Cambodian economy could move forward, depending on the political stability factors that need to be maintained forever if Cambodia wants to run after its neighbors in the next 20 years.
Meng Tech told the Post last week that both the state and the private sector must work together to enforce the law to attract foreign investors.
For more than 10 years, the Cambodian Chamber of Commerce (CCC) has played a part in attracting foreign investors.
For foreign investors, those who have never seen Cambodia in person often look at Cambodia with dark eyes, but for those who have arrived, it is understood that Cambodia has great economic potential, even though Cambodia has just emerged from the war.
Regarding economic policy, the Royal Government of Cambodia has identified at least four pillars to support the national economy, including agriculture, tourism, garment exports and construction.
However, these sectors are not as strong as expected, and the four sectors will face some challenges in the future.
Mr. Meng Tech said that Cambodia's agricultural sector was still small-scale.
Farmers need the Royal Government to help them with irrigation, irrigation and ensuring a reasonable price for rice.
The tourism sector, on the other hand, should manage and maintain the revenue from ticket sales for the Angkor Wat temple in Siem Reap properly.
Separately, the garment export industry may also face challenges in the future, when garment quotas are terminated.
Mr. Meng Tech confirmed that:
Factory owners are struggling to make a profit, while protesting workers are demanding higher wages.
Mr. Meng Tech stated that in the past, this issue has been the subject of accusations between factory owners and workers, which has generally led to protests against each other, to the detriment of both parties, but the biggest problem is that the mediator is causing problems. Those.
I have always observed that workers protest for a decent salary, and I think that $ 160 is not enough.
He called for the prosecution or removal of barriers posed by corrupt officials to take advantage of factory owners and workers.
In Meng Tech's view, Cambodia is not the only one of the four pillars of the economy, in fact, there are forests, oil and gas concessions, gold and many other islands that have the potential to boost economic growth.
But it is unfortunate that these resources are not being used properly due to corruption.
Asked about his views on ELCs and forest concessions, Nguon Meng Tech expressed concern about ELCs granted to foreign companies.
He said the state should take it out of the hands of foreign companies and give it back to local investors, rather than giving it to foreign companies that could bring problems for the future.
He can have land, run a business, create jobs, reduce migration.
If we allow workers to go abroad, our economy will weaken and development will be slow.
Mr. Meng Tech said that for Cambodia's islands, if invested properly, it will benefit all investors, the government and the people.
Meng Tech says those who invest money must make a profit, but it also needs to provide jobs for people around or far away to work.
While Cambodia is experiencing a booming economy, business disputes are rife.
Meng Tech urges a commercial court to resolve trade disputes as soon as possible.
He stressed that commercial disputes should not be judged by civil courts.
Currently, civil courts have no expertise in adjudicating commercial disputes.
There must be a commercial court.
The economic development and political situation should be well maintained, especially the political situation before and after the upcoming 2018 national election.
When asked if the ability of CNRP President Sam Rainsy to become the Prime Minister of Cambodia?
Suon Serey Ratha, a young but determined politician accused of spying for the ruling party to split the opposition, said he had formed the party. To split the real voice, but not just to split the CNRP, which betrayed the will of the people, but also to split the voice of the CPP, which has been in power for more than 30 years but does not serve National interest as well.
The politician described the allegations as "painting" that he did not care about, but instead did his best to make the Cambodian people a winner.
Suon Serey Ratha is the chairman of the ruling Khmer Rouge party, which was just registered with the Ministry of Interior in October 2015.
The Khmer Rouge is a new party that has transformed itself from the US-based Cambodian People's Power Movement, which has been relentlessly criticizing the government and opposing the Vietnamese aggression.
Since its inception four months ago, the Cambodian ruling party has expanded to 11 provinces and plans to expand to 25 provinces by the end of 2016.
Mr. Serey Ratha told the Post about the party's vision and personal life in an exclusive interview at his party headquarters, a stacked apartment in Tuol Kork district last week. This February 15, the political mission of the ruling party is to do politics for the people to win and for the people to win is to advocate for reform of the organization. Vote.
So we look at the form of recruitment, especially the old secretary general, it just like copying the old mandate to the new one and so called the new NEC, but the old way of working, if any Elections organized by them according to the law, it is just a general election.
In order to achieve the goal of doing politics for the Cambodian people to win the first step, the Cambodian ruling party has five principles to fight, the first is to demand an independent NEC.
Independence is not a political party sharing quotas, it is the nine people who join the NEC. Is independent from the beginning without being a member of any political party.
The second is to remove the names of foreigners, Vietnamese who have not yet become Cambodian citizens from the voter list, because there are many Vietnamese who have not yet become Cambodian citizens who have their names on the voter list.
The third is to demand that the Cambodian people living and working abroad, who now number in the millions, have the right to vote.
Fourth, it is necessary to demand the reform of the election law, to stop allowing political parties to nominate candidates, because if a candidate nominated by the party at the time of the election of members of parliament, it becomes a member. Party MP.
Mr. Ratha wants the people to nominate the candidates who want to run, and the party must accept the candidates proposed by the people, and the fifth is to determine the mandate of the candidates who want to run. Prime Minister.
Although the CPP and the CNRP have now become the strongest and most prominent rivals, Serey Ratha said the two parties could not resolve national and international issues. The hardships of the people, he did not go to join these parties and form a new party.
Mr. Ratha said he formed the party to split the voices of parties that take politics as a business and when the election they want their party to win, but they do not care how the people feel Okay.
The CPP has always been the voice of a party that does not serve the national interest.
As for the CNRP vote gathered in 2013, it was a vote for a party that betrayed the will of the people.
As I said before, if the Cambodian people still cheat the thief lovers to help the nation, the Cambodian people who want to change are patriots, but only choose the thief-loving leader, so we want to collect Patriotic people elect the same patriotic leader.
On May 28, 2015, the Court of Appeal issued a final verdict sentenced Suon Serey Ratha to seven years in prison on charges of treason in connection with the distribution of hundreds of roses and leaflets. From August 2013, which was claimed to have incited armed violence to overthrow the government.
But immediately in July 2015, the King granted a pardon to Serey Ratha at the request of Prime Minister Hun Sen, amid widespread rumors and accusations. It has been rumored for years that the man, a dual Cambodian-American, was Hun Sen's spy to split the opposition.
The 44-year-old went on to say that what is more special than the CPP and the CNRP is that the Cambodian ruling party has come up with new policy ideas. Let the people listen and consider.
The Cambodian ruling party has determined that it is necessary to do politics for the people to win, and for the people to win, the people must know the truth.
As for the CNRP, which the people tried to send their hopes in 2013, they finally betrayed the will of the people. Their supporters eventually betrayed the nation.
In recent months, the opposition has seen a series of persecution, including the arrest of a senator and 13 activists in prison, arrest warrants and evictions. Mr. Sam Rainsy leaves the National Assembly The beatings caused serious injuries to two female representatives and the removal of Mr. Kem Sokha from the post of First Vice President of the National Assembly.
However, Mr. Serey Ratha considered that the situation that happened to the opposition party was a political game between the opposition leader and the leader. Lead the ruling party.
Therefore, the Cambodian ruling party will expose and reveal all these facts to people across the country from 2016 onwards.
Suon Serey Tha left Cambodia to study political science in the United States in 2007.
Upon his arrival in the United States, he formed an advocacy group called the Khmer Action Committee for Justice and Equity, which later became a political movement called the Khmer People's Power Movement. .
Later, under threat from the Phnom Penh government, Serey Ratha was granted residency in the United States.
Currently, in addition to his political career, Ratha has a stake in selling goods online with friends in the United States and France, and he has been doing other business in Thailand since Before leaving for the United States.
Despite being lucky enough to live and have a prosperous business in a civilized country, Serey Ratha still wants to return to help the country through politics, which he says is his nature. Mr.
However, he said that his childhood dream was to become a lawyer to defend the poor for free.
But he later changed his mind, thinking that being a lawyer could not help millions of people, wanting to return to civil society, hoping that civil society organizations could help more people.
After working in a civil society organization for almost 10 years, Mr. Ratha saw that some civil society groups are civilized, both sides.
Only politics can cure cancer, a chronic disease in Cambodian society that can alleviate the suffering of the Cambodian people.
Mr. Serey Ratha called himself a non-aligned politician because his parents were pure farmers and did not point to any elders or veteran politicians.
Then it spread to Cambodians in Europe, to Cambodians in Canada, to Cambodians in Australia, so everything came out of my mind, so I came up with ideas and invested in them, until I had the money to do it, I believe. That as long as you have an idea to do anything, you will succeed.
According to Serey Ratha, the only real rivals of the ruling Cambodian People's Party are the CPP and the CNRP, while other parties, including the newly formed parties, some of which do not even have a headquarters. Also, it is not a partner with his party.
So we have the capital, we have the resources, we are qualified to compete.
Mr. Suon Serey Ratha was born on September 9, 1973 in a farming family in Khnay Romeas village, Bavel district, Battambang province.
Mr. Serey Ratha married a Cambodian woman with the same tragic life in Thailand on December 27, 2012, with only six people attending the wedding: a mother, a mother-in-law and siblings. Two cousins, including him and his wife.
He now has two children, a one-and-a-half-year-old girl and a five-month-old son.
In the end, Serey Ratha sent a message to Cambodians across the country, including Cambodians abroad, that politics in Cambodia is not without a solution, but from politicians. Khmer rice in the past did not want to settle.
I liken it to a doctor taking care of a patient so that he can take care of himself.
So, the Khmer ruling party is here to cure the disease, not to let you raise any more politicians.
Mr. Serey Ratha stressed that the Cambodian ruling party is to make people understand their duties as citizens and for people to dare to use their power to move for change, and this power is not It is not a gift, the power of the people is from nature, from the day of birth to the day of death, but now the most important thing is that you understand that you have power and dare to use your power to change.
Ke Sovannarot starts from a girl selling watermelons to become a member of parliament
Everyone's life is different, some live comfortably from childhood to adulthood, but others have to work on their own or have to help their parents in various occupations from an early age. Children come because of poor family life.
For example, Ms. Ke Sovannarot, who used to be a girl who sells watermelons and oranges, sells them at the bus stop and in the market to earn money to help her family and to support her own education until she successfully becomes a representative of tens of thousands of people in Siem Reap because of the struggle in her bitter life.
Ke Sovannarot, a CNRP lawmaker from Siem Reap, was born on September 12, 1962 in Kampong Chhnang province, the eighth child in a family of nine.
As a child in the 1970s, her family often moved to other provinces and came to Phnom Penh for a small business to support themselves due to poor living conditions.
Later, in Phnom Penh, I continued my difficult life, I became a seller of oranges in the cinema or sell lottery tickets at the noodle shop.
So it means that in that family, all the children have to help their mother work, in addition to studying, they have to work to help support the family.
However, between 1975 and 1979, Cambodia experienced the flames of war, the genocidal regime of Pol Pot for 3 years, 8 months and 20 days. She was forced to do heavy labor, forcing her relatives to live alone in a children's mobile center.
But fortunately, after Cambodia was liberated from the dark regime, she received the news and was prevented from returning to her family in Phnom Penh.
After reuniting with her family, she tried to continue her studies at Baktouk High School by studying in the morning or during the day and doing housework to help her sister sew clothes to earn money to buy books and pay tuition until she graduated from college. Economics at the National University of Management.
Ms. Ke Sovannarot married Mr. Yim Sovann, a CNRP lawmaker from Phnom Penh in early 1988, with whom she had two sons.
She and her husband struggled through the life of a new family until they started a video recording and tile business while their core jobs were civil servants.
Being humane, I see that only politics in society can give me the opportunity to interact with people around me and share experiences and help solve their problems.
Ms. Sovannarot said that because she saw the actions and activities of Mr. Sam Rainsy, who defended justice and dared to sacrifice everything for the people and the nation.
In her life as a woman politician and becoming a Member of Parliament for Siem Reap since 2003, Ms. Ke Sovannarot has always visited local people to help resolve land disputes, health issues and other issues. Of local people and help organize party work.
She has also been threatened with clashes with local authorities, and has been criticized by more than 50 CNRP activists in Siem Reap for leading a partisan strike to demand her removal from the group. Jobs in Siem Reap.
We face the external environment and within the party we also face, but it is important that we be patient and strong.
Regarding the current political situation in Cambodia, she sees improvement through the use of a culture of dialogue, NEC reform, and more mature politicians, while technology has helped provide more information to people in decision-making. Elect the country's leaders.
She compared politics abroad and in Cambodia, saying that in their own country, the people's vote is for politicians to follow, unlike us, we get our vote for the people to follow, in their country, they let politicians lead the country. According to the will of the people, but in our district, we use it in reverse to get the people to follow us.
But now different from before, now people are starting to understand, even if there is a land issue, people are starting to ask us to solve it, whether we can solve it or not, the people are the ones who decide the future leaders through elections, traffic problems, health problems, alcohol problems, problems. Everything is conditioned by the people, now politicians have to start running after the people.
I believe that the political situation will improve because the people are starting to have power, so we can organize our country.
However, when asked if she has any desire to develop her role or ambition to be the next Prime Minister?
Ms. Ke Sovannarot replied that she had never had such ambition, leaving many senior politicians with experience in leading the country, but if given the opportunity and the circumstances required, she would Accept.
However, she confirmed:
Mam Sonando: Recognizing 55 seats is tantamount to cutting one's own neck
Mam Sonando is proud to own two bars in France
Despite being very proud to open the first bar in Paris among Cambodian, Chinese and Vietnamese expatriates living there after he easily made tens of thousands of francs a month from running a photo shop. Despite his image, the profits and civilized life in France did not make Mam Sonando forget his homeland, which he said had always been occupied by Vietnam at all times.
Mam Sonando, currently president of the Beehive Social Democratic Party and director of Beehive Radio, was born on February 13, 1942, into a middle-class family in Kampong Siem district, Kampong Cham province.
He had nine siblings, five sisters and a brother who were killed by the Khmer Rouge.
Currently, one of his brothers lives in Cambodia with another brother living in the United States and all of them live in France.
Sonando did not live long in his hometown, as his family had to move to live with his father, who became governor of Kanhchriech district in Prey Veng province in 1945.
After living in Kanhchriech district for about five years, during the 1950s, Sonando's family relocated to Phnom Penh's Veal Vong district.
After failing the exam in 1964, at the age of about 21, Sonando was lucky enough to study writing and filmmaking in France.
Passing the exam was not easy for Sonando because he had to study and find a job while Cambodian expats did not have the legal right to find work in France. He had only 1,500 francs in his pocket.
Sonando went on to describe his life story to the Post in an interview at a luxury villa, the headquarters of Beehive Radio in Meanchey district recently:
But after living a miserable life, both studying and working secretly during 1969-70, after studying for a diploma in writing and filming, Sonando applied for a job as a photographer at a company that worked for Advertised all over the world at the behest of a friend and was hired by the company after a day of testing.
After working for the company, Sonando was able to buy a house in the fourth quarter of Paris, the most expensive house in the district because it is the oldest and most attractive to tourists. Strong.
But unfortunately, or as fate would have it, the business of collecting francs easily did not last long because Sonando was shocked after receiving the news that Cambodia had been invaded by the Khmer Rouge. Occupied on April 17, 1975.
At that time, Cambodian students who used to receive scholarships, those who went to live and received financial support from their parents in Cambodia did not receive anything, only Sonando had the most prominent life.
In such a situation, Cambodians living in France met and urged Sonando to change his career from photography to opening a bar.
When he moved to open a bar, he had to go through a period of hard struggle because everything had to start anew, with even more dangers, while the Vietnamese and Chinese who had lived in France for hundreds of years did not dare to open. No bar business because the mafia came to kidnap money.
Sonando's bar was named Apsara, but was later renamed the Galaxy to Lebus Afi to attract French visitors, as some Cambodians who had been drinking caused loud noises to disturb the neighbors, and Sonando received a warning. Close the bar from the French police.
Although Sonando changed the name of the bar from Khmer to French to attract French guests, it was still not possible to end the problem, saying that the bar was a problem because the visitors came from Opium traffickers to thieves.
After a difficult struggle, Sonando succeeded in opening a bar, and he later opened a new bar.
But despite his continued success in business in France, Sonando never forgot his homeland, always hoping that one day he would have to return to his homeland.
Sonando finally decided to return to his homeland in 1994, one year after the election.
When he first returned to Cambodia, Sonando was just thinking about what to do to make money.
But after living and traveling to many places for about 5-6 months, he saw that Cambodia is miserable.
The tragedy is due to the deceitful Cambodian politicians, the ignorant people, and he thinks that if we continue to be under this kind of leadership, we could be in danger of losing our race.
Finally, in 1998, Sonando decided to form a party called the Beehive Social Democratic Party, which did not hope to win the election to become prime minister, but only to be known. There is a voice as the leader of the party to have the opportunity to speak out against other party leaders, which he said is always lying to the people.
After failing to win a seat in the 1998 national election, Sonando shut down his party.
Sonando, who is not big or small but looks strong because he exercises regularly, is also the president of the Association of Democrats, and in the last election, the association came out in support of the CNRP.
He confirmed that his association did not go to power with any political party.
After resolutely joining the CNRP, Sonando saw that the positions of party leaders led by Sam Rainsy and Kem Sokha were not strong enough to betray the will of the people, so he withdrew his support.
Sonando has finally reopened his party to run in the upcoming elections.
Some have speculated that Mam Sonando's return to politics may be due to the fact that he has been rewarded by the ruling party to split the growing popularity of the opposition. Strong.
At the same time, Sonando criticized the leaders of the CNRP, especially the hasty agreement to attend the meeting with the CPP without a clear contract in advance, especially the revision. The format of the National Election Committee, the amendment of the election law and some other issues, so that today the CPP can change at will.
Mam Sonando, who has been imprisoned up to three times, the first time in connection with allegations of arson, the second time in connection with criticism of the Cambodia-Vietnam Supplementary Border Treaty, and the third time in connection with the creation of a separatist zone. In Kratie province, he said he wanted to win the 2018 election.
Wanting to win the election because he sees that the Cambodian people will have no choice but to his Social Democratic Beehive party when the ruling party fails to address immigration and border issues and opposition leaders Only lies deceive the people.
Sonando, a dual Cambodian and French national, returned from Paris to Cambodia to marry a Cambodian wife in 1974 and was brought to live in France before Cambodia fell into the flames of war in 1975. .
Mr. Khem Veasna: I formed a party to serve my views
I formed a party to serve my views
Coalition for Democracy Party President Khem Veasna has been training thousands of young party members by embracing a new ideology full of sincerity, volunteering to die with the party leader, daring to sacrifice personal interests for the sake of the ancestors. The cause of the party and the goal of this party.
Mr. Khem Veasna called his human resources as an army or a new race, a different kind of person in the CPP, different from the youth of the CNRP and different from other parties.
In a recent interview with the Post at the headquarters of an apartment building in Tuol Kork district, Khem Veasna stated that his party must change the way Cambodians live on religious beliefs and lifestyles. How to teach young people to think critically rather than do what is advertised.
Mr. Veasna praised the youth in his party as well-qualified people who see through the solution of national problems.
They understand the dangerous political attitude of centralized power led by dictators.
Khem Veasna is a party leader who always has a different view from other party political views.
Mr. Veasna confirmed that:
Anyone who is different from us is like a black ant crawling on a white cloth.
Mr. Veasna added:
If you reach that level, wait for you to come and all that is done in a foundation in integrity.
The party president, who is often criticized by Facebook users, said that the people of the coalition movement must be willing to help the society and not be afraid of any criticism.
The teaching is not religious, but scientific.
The Coalition for Democracy is building good human resources.
He said his party, in contrast to the old and new parties, which generally only consider private interests.
The coalition movement is not only selfless, it is a sacrifice for the motherland.
Mr. Veasna suggested that Cambodian children should be able to sacrifice their personal interests to serve the race, and that the work he and his group were doing was not afraid of criticism.
Having practiced Buddhist Vipassana for a short time, Veasna understood some of the Buddha's teachings, and he criticized any monks who preached to defraud Buddhists.
In recent years, Khem Veasna has been widely criticized on Facebook for his actions against state-owned Buddhist institutions and for criticizing key political figures of government and non-government parties. The government, too.
Mr. Khem Veasna was born in 1965 in Peana village, Kdeung Reay commune, Kanhchriech district, Prey Veng province.
Khem Veasna is a good student in mathematics, physics and chemistry and has a strong artistic nature, which is why he has a rhetoric to attract fans.
In 1986, Veasna passed the Baccalaureate exam and entered the film department, spending four years in the field.
As an actor, Khem Veasna has a different view from some of the co-stars in the arts.
He is not as attached to luxury as his colleagues.
The movie industry is a place that brings people crazy and loves wealth.
Tired of stardom, Veasna enrolled in law school and graduated in 1997, the year he was lured by friends to join the Sam Rainsy Party.
He ran for the 5th National Assembly in 1998 in Prey Veng province, but did not win the National Assembly.
In 2003, Mr. Veasna re-ran for second place and was elected to the Prey Veng Provincial Assembly.
Joining the Sam Rainsy Party again is not a matter of patriotic intentions or wanting to become great, it is not clear in itself.
When he entered the political arena with bitterness and bitterness with the opposition Sam Rainsy Party less than a full term, he began to see injustice in his party's nest.
Criticism within the party upset the party, which eventually decided to fire him as he sought to start a new party of his own.
After the party ended its candidacy for the Prey Veng provincial parliament, Khem Veasna set up an NGO as a step to understand the needs of the people before he formed the Coalition for Democracy in 2006.
While he left the SRP, it was also when his mother became seriously ill, and much of his illness was related to Khem Veasna's political life in finding fault with him. Smooth, different from other politicians.
I have not yet made my mother happy, and my political career has affected my mother's illness.
The politician, who is known for his outspoken criticism in public, is not only dissatisfied with the Sam Rainsy Party, but also dissatisfied with the leadership of the Cambodian People's Party (CPP).
On the other hand, he chose to find people from outside, such as from the CNRP or other parties that he chose to Hun Sen, all of them are seeking personal gain, until then, such individuals are very common in this party. It's too late for the Prime Minister to change.
As the leader of a young party and never having a seat in parliament, Khem Veasna believes that in order for Cambodia to have peace and long-term development, Cambodia must adhere to true democracy, and the Coalition for Democracy has focused on On scientific reasoning based on three major principles.
The second is the practice of Buddhism, wanting people to take advantage of the lessons of the Buddha.
And thirdly, educating the people to find a way to think before making a decision.
These three principles are the basis for the supporters to choose to lead the nation.
Khem Veasna believes his party will have more supporters in the upcoming 2017 and 2018 election years.
Despite receiving frequent death threats from unknown individuals because he has always come forward to help protect the poor who have been subjected to land grabbing, he has also come forward to help prevent deforestation. Chhay Thy, a former civil servant at the Ministry of Agriculture, still refuses to give up his job as a human rights defender and is determined to continue working until old age.
Chhay Thy, who is currently the coordinating officer for the human rights group Adhoc in Ratanakkiri province, said that after joining the human rights organization, he often received death threats from his family because of his involvement. Protest with land victims and give them advice.
Thus, we find it very challenging to work as a human rights defender in this area.
Despite all kinds of threats, he still enjoys social work, saying that this is innate and can not see injustice happening to the same people.
Mr. Chhay Thy was born in 1967 in Sangkat Phsar Depot, Khan Toul Kork, Phnom Penh, to a family where his father was a restaurant chef and his mother was a housewife.
But four siblings and his mother died during the Pol Pot era, leaving him and a brother living together, while his father died in 1997 of old age.
Chhay Thy graduated from 10th grade in 2006 but has completed additional subjects such as Thai and is fluent in Vietnamese and some ethnic languages today.
He then joined the Department of Forestry as a civil servant, now transformed into the Forestry Administration, for five years from 1986 to 1991.
He was sent by the Forestry Administration to Ratanakkiri province to do business in Prey Cub, cutting down old trees and decaying wood.
At the end of his mission, he did not return to Phnom Penh, beginning to build a new life in Ratanakkiri since 1986.
Since then, he has made a living selling second-hand clothes, but he has always helped people when people are suffering and suffering injustice in land disputes.
He married a Laotian woman in 1990 and had five children, but divorced in 2006, claiming they were living apart.
Seeing his activities as courageous, hardworking and helpful to villagers, especially indigenous peoples, Thy became a volunteer human rights activist at Adhoc in late 2007.
But immediately after becoming a human rights activist, his life was filled with many different threats.
In addition, Mr. Chhay Thy has been charged with inciting people to protest, which is a serious charge.
At that time, in 2009, ADHOC decided to send him to work in Mondulkiri to alleviate the situation.
But because he thought he was just a human rights activist, a consultant and did not commit the alleged crimes, he decided to return to work in Ratanakkiri and the court did not arrest him.
Mr. Chhay Thy volunteered to be an Adhoc human rights activist in 2007 and became a full-time staff member and land investigator in 2010 and was promoted to Adhoc Coordinator in Ratanakkiri at the end of 2012.
Not only as a mediator in land disputes, he investigated cases of illegal logging and observed overnight logging in O'Yadao district to Vietnam without the supervision of the Forestry Administration.
He has asked the authorities to intervene to prevent forest crime and has made many reports, but the authorities seem to have ignored them.
According to Chhay Thi, nearly 30 years ago, during the month of January, he turned off the lights at night because it was very cold, and at 12 noon, people were still wearing winter coats, but now the weather is completely different.
He lamented the loss of large amounts of natural resources and called on the government to take effective measures to prevent deforestation.
Chhay Thy had a dream since he was a child to run a car repair garage on his own. He even studied repair for three years, but this dream did not come true as he had health problems. Work in the garage.
But he said that what he is doing as a human rights activist today is not a dream, it is a matter of temperament: he likes to get sick when he sees people suffering and suffering injustice.
However, in his work as a human rights defender to serve the society for more than eight years, he has achieved great success with the support of many people.
The former Phnom Penh resident, now 47, wants to continue working for human rights until his last breath.
Thida Kheu is an outstanding woman active in social work
Despite being lucky enough to leave for Thailand before Cambodia fell into the flames of war before 1975, Thida Kheu did not live in solitude for her hard work. A lot of humanitarian work to help support the hundreds of Cambodian refugees fleeing the war in Cambodia to live in developed countries.
Due to her activism as a civil society organization focused on promoting women's rights and good governance, Thida Kheu, now 64, can be considered a woman. A Cambodian who participates in changing the mindset of women, turns the stove around to become a woman who has the courage to participate in social and political affairs, not inferior to men.
Thida Kheu, Executive Director of Silakar from 1997 to the present and Secretary-General of the Committee for the Advancement of Women in Politics, left for Thailand before 1975, the country where her husband taught at AIT. In Thailand and later, she and her husband were recruited to work at the US Embassy in Bangkok on refugee issues.
Ms. Thida Kheu says:
During the war from 1974 to 1979, Cambodians fled to the Thai border in search of a chance to move to a third country.
Ms. Thida Kheu recalled that during the Cambodian invasion of the Thai border, her job was to interview, prepare documents, and deal with refugees on the Thai-Cambodian border.
It was not until 1979 that Thida Kheu left Thailand for the United States alone, a place not only for resettlement, but also for the establishment of the Cambodian Federation in the United States. She founded a non-governmental organization in Washington, D.C., where she served as executive director of the Khmer Rouge Congress, a group of Cambodian organizations that provide policy advice on the settlement of Cambodians there.
However, she did not want to live in a civilized country like the United States forever.
After the Treaty of Paris in the 1990s, Thida Kheu returned to her homeland to see how Cambodians in the United States like her could help a country that had just emerged from the flames of war. Massacre.
It was then that her organization, with the help of USAID Cambodia, brought in volunteers from the United States to work in Cambodia.
Her next job was to help NGOs in Cambodia get started by providing volunteers and teaching them how to plan proposals for funding.
Thida Kheu is recognized as a well-known Cambodian woman for promoting women's rights on an equal footing with men, promoting women's role in politics and decision-making.
For the work activities of her organization, she has helped to open training courses for NGOs to state institutions and citizens on gender, social accountability and good governance.
Thida Kheu was born in 1951 in Phnom Penh, where her deceased father was a civil servant who spoke French fluently, served in the Sangkum Reastr Niyum regime, and had a 97-year-old Cambodian mother who now lives in France.
She is the eighth of 10 children, four of whom have died. Currently, only one lives in Cambodia with her, while the other four live in France.
She married at the age of 17 while in high school with her husband, Khieu Chiv, who was the first doctor of mathematics from the United States during that time.
Her husband, who is also a dean of the Faculty of Science at the University of Phnom Penh and retired, is now 76 years old.
They have three children, a daughter, are married and have seven grandchildren, all of whom live in the United States, with only one grandson living in Cambodia with her today.
She emphasized that her organization was working independently to help Cambodian society.
Although her organization strives to work independently, does not belong to any political party, or is politically influenced by any group or party, it cannot escape prosecution.
That's difficult for us.
Ms. Thida led a campaign to petition the government and embassies in Phnom Penh in early 2014, representing about 160 organizations, demanding the release of human rights activists and factory workers who were unjustly arrested by authorities. Protesters demanded an increase in workers' salaries until five workers were shot dead on Veng Sreng Road in late 2014.
Due to her advanced age, Thida Kheu plans to transfer her management knowledge of Silakar as the next generation in the next year or two, and she wants to see more full cooperation between local authorities as well as the government. With civil society.
From poor ignorant children picking food on the street to becoming millionaires
The stage of human life can change from sad to bitter, it depends on perseverance by sacrificing physical and mental strength for one's dreams, which is the goal of life.
Meng Ly, a native of Battambang province, was born in 1969, a time when people of this age suffered greatly, having to go through the flames of war, especially during the Khmer Rouge genocide between 1975 and 1979.
As a child under the brutal regime of Pol Pot, there are no good memories to tell stories to the next generation, because life is full of indescribable bitterness.
Between 1979 and 1981, hundreds of thousands of Cambodians went through another tragedy as they crossed the Dangrek Mountains near the Thai-Cambodian border, risking their lives to cross into dangerous Thai territory.
This story is almost never revealed to the world.
At that time, Cambodian families who had survived the Pol Pot massacre crossed the Dangrek Mountains and were shot down by Thai soldiers.
Fortunately, his family survived.
Elderly men and women died of cerebral palsy, some with broken arms and legs, bleeding wounds, and in the water full of Cambodian corpses, it was a great tragedy.
But this bitterness strengthens people by turning those pains into self-improvement efforts.
In 1984, his family arrived in the United States and began a new life in California.
Struggling with an ignorant life in the United States at the age of 15 at the time.
Life is full of bitterness and a lot of flexibility that makes people strong.
Falling, those challenges led his eight brothers to struggle to survive and free themselves from ignorance, ignorance, and poverty.
Kouch Mengly described that in the mid-1980s, Cambodian children in the United States were like monkeys living in ignorance.
I myself used to drink and pour water in the toilet bowl because we do not know.
To develop myself first, I devote time to work and study from early morning until 3:30 in the morning, never knowing Saturday-Sunday.
He said that his family used labor as capital to work as ironing clothes, buttoning clothes and anything that could earn money to go to school.
In the end, he learned medicine.
Through many hardships and sufferings, he longed to help provide health care to poor Cambodians.
In 2002, he returned to the country as a volunteer doctor.
At that time, he and his wife started to open an English language clinic and school with a capital of only $ 20,000.
As a human resource educator and doctor, the tycoon aims to nurture the next generation of Cambodians to become new people with the knowledge and skills to compete in the national and international markets.
Dr. Mengly's American Intercontinental School needs to redesign the curriculum from the Cambodian Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports with the US curriculum, incorporating other essential skills that will enable students to stand out in a competitive market, especially integration. ASEAN economy, which just entered the end of December 2015.
The new curriculum includes:
Services and society by encouraging young people to understand the difficulties of others.
Teach young people about etiquette, gentleness, friendliness and study visits so that they can understand the social environment, arts, sports and social affairs.
The main goal of his training is to teach people how to judge, to think, to weigh cause and effect, to be tolerant and understanding of each other.
He also saw that political conflicts, corruption, poverty, crime, drugs, land loss and prostitution could slow down the Royal Government of Cambodia's economic policies.
He stated that Cambodia, we try to move forward 10 steps and back 11 steps.
Corruption is not only an obstacle to the country's decline, but also an obstacle for young people under the age of 34 to help develop the nation.
As far as I know, in Cambodia, I am full and still not calm and not tired.
He added that there are other main causes that cause the Khmer nation not to grow the economy is not progressive is all kinds of evil that is oppressing the Khmer morality.
Mr. Meng Ly praised the efforts of the Royal Government of Cambodia today to overcome all obstacles that hinder the progress of the nation.
But it is still overpowering individuals, officials and traders who are taking a deep breath, referring to corrupt individuals and lawlessness in Cambodia.
If a tree is half-broken, are we willing to stay stuck or be cut down?
If we love the nation, love Khmer children, Khmer blood, and do not let people look down on our Khmer children, we must unite and participate, we must not wait for one person to do it.
He added that until everyone does, we do not have to blame anyone, if you see corruption, shout, if you see injustice, shout, if you see something wrong, do not be afraid, do not panic, do not panic, because this is what makes Cambodia Unable to develop, our Cambodians are still miserable, their reputation is still degraded, and foreigners are still looked down upon.
In order to find a solution for the Cambodian economy in the medium and long term, Professor Oknha Kouch Mengly urged all members of society, especially young people, to pay attention to education, health, tourism and agriculture as pillars to compete in the ASEAN market.
Each individual must own his own.
If we strengthen education and health as the foundation of human resource production, young people will be able to build their own society, family and nation.
As a professional doctor from the United States, he was shocked to see the behavior of some Cambodian doctors as unethical, merciless and unkind to poor patients.
If you go to the hospital, I guarantee they will sleep on the sidewalk, that is what I see firsthand.
If there is an attitude of manners, pride, arrogance, captivity, how can our Cambodian children be unfortunate.
When he went for treatment, he almost knelt down and begged the doctor.
Obviously, the major national obstacles and the weakness of Cambodia is not caused by the policy of the Royal Government, obviously due to the lack of education and lack of conscience, love, morality and non-patriotism.
The professor stated:
And if our health is not good, we will be weak.
In light of foreign policy, while Cambodia has close ties with China, some analysts have suggested that the presence of Chinese influence in Cambodia could be more dangerous than beneficial to Cambodia, but Professor Kouch Mengly has the opposite view.
He said that China is more favorable to us Cambodians than the United States, and if the United States demands too much, too fancy.
American politics is open but closed.
The important thing is that we, the landlords, make laws to protect our Cambodian interests, we do not have to take their money and let them return it at will.
He replied that I could not be a leader and did not want to be a leader, I was a businessman, but if I could give advice, I would train Cambodians to be good people, I think we need to focus on knowledge, skills and ethics. .
Oknha Kouch Mengly is generally seen as sharing his knowledge and skills with society at a cost of hundreds of thousands of dollars.
He is different from many other tycoons who have not yet shared with society.
Do good, do good, do bad, do bad, but I think if we are kind, knowledgeable and patriotic, we must remember the bitterness of the past, the misery and the pain, so look in the mirror and ask yourself Why do we need to continue to make the next generation of Cambodians miserable?
From poor ignorant children picking food on the way to eating to becoming millionaires
Enter the river by turning into the country, but I do not enter the country in a corrupt way
Therefore, he chose other options that are more effective in changing our Cambodian society by strengthening the quality of education.
In an interview with the Post two weeks ago, the professor said he had decided to change his career from a civil servant to a researcher, seeing it as a way to help the nation.
I jumped out of politics because I have my own views and I believe that Cambodia is truly peaceful as long as we do our best to keep people from living in poverty.
Given a clear view of the current social situation, the gap between rich and poor, between the big and the powerful, and the powerless and the unjust still remain issues to be addressed, he believes. Education is the key to solving these crises effectively.
We know that corruption, as the Khmer word always says, enters the river by turning into the country by country, I do not want to enter the country by country, the way of corruption, the way of social injustice.
I am optimistic that I will still be able to maintain my ideals.
I want to see people live with equal rights and freedoms.
Professor Sovanna will never return to politics.
He says maintaining his ideals as a youth educator is an important part of his life.
As the co-author of the book Human Security and Land Rights 2015, published by the Khmer Institute for Cooperation and Peace, he discovered that one of the key keys to narrowing the gap between rich and poor in Cambodia is to develop together with the people. Land.
He said that in order to develop, each country needs to expand its economic potential.
If you look at the macroeconomic picture, Cambodia has an average annual GDP of 7%.
But the scale of economic growth has not narrowed the gap between rich and poor.
He says poverty may be linked to land rights.
At least half a million people are protesting with the government over the impact on their land ownership.
Figures from the human rights group Licadho show that about 500,000 people have been affected by the loss of their land and homes, while Adhoc has estimated that 770,000 people have been affected by the government's land concession policy.
The figures show that 7% of national economic growth does not share the share of growth with the poor.
According to his own research, the 2001 land law is still very well written, but the lack of practical implementation means that the land concession given to the investment company, the people living on the land do not have clear information about the development project. No.
For example, the people in the Areng area know that he has been on that land since the time of our great-grandfather.
Residents there said in an interview that he wanted to live the way his ancestors did, not wanting to know about the development that left him homeless.
Citizens have been seeing the scale of social injustice increase.
As a professor, I would like to remind you that during the Khmer Rouge era, Pol Pot propagated the people against feudalism, citing the gap between rich and poor as the starting point of the revolution.
If the government allows people to express themselves, a solution can be found.
In the minds of the people in the countryside, they always understand that the state is their oppressor, the state is their land grabber.
So we need to have enough time to explain to him.
Through research to reduce the gap between rich and poor in a prosperous society, they work together with their nations from the grassroots to the upper classes.
Citizens have an obligation to participate in solving it, he is not stupid in all matters, some things citizens are smarter than the leaders.
Citizens have not only the right to vote every five years, but also the right to do everything with the state.
Historically, it has been clearly stated that whenever the people of that nation have a duty to participate in solving all the problems that arise in the society of the nation, they will know the taste of prosperity and happiness.
As a Cambodian-American speaker, he helped change the minds of many young Americans, wrote many books, and was a master who understood the hardships of his staff and colleagues.
But in Cambodia, I find it difficult to change because everything happens out of thought.
In the last five years, as a professor, I have developed a reading habit for Cambodian children.
However, senior researcher Uncle Sovanna acknowledged that the Royal Government of Cambodia from 1993 to the present has done a lot to help the nation, especially to bring peace and economic development.
But the result of the nation's progress was limited to one faction.
He said the key to narrowing the gap between rich and poor is to give them opportunities, we will grow.
Cambodians do not have equal opportunities to improve their lives.
Professor Po Sovanna found the key to nation building, starting with the quality of education.
Especially young people who have an obligation to absorb as much knowledge as possible, because the world values education so much, it means that the thinking of young people is the foundation of nation building.
The main purpose of the professor for the next generation is to ask the Cambodian youth to live with hope, whatever they can do, we can do.
Young people can solve all problems if we want to.
Can not start with a nod of the head, we are determined and good heart to help the nation.
This intellectual was born in 1956 in a family of highly educated people in Koh Thom district, Kandal province during the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era after Cambodia gained independence from the French colony.
His father was the former governor of Svay Rieng and the governor of Kampong Thom during the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era of King Norodom Sihanouk in the 1960s.
He is the fourth of 10 siblings.
Professor Khmer is a very lucky Cambodian citizen who did not suffer in the Democratic Kampuchea regime between 1975 and 1979.
But four of his brothers and sisters, as well as his mother, died during the Khmer Rouge regime.
He moved from Paris to the United States in 1975.
To learn about the political situation in Cambodia, he volunteered at the Cypriot camp near the Thai border in late 1989.
The professor recalled that the Cypriot camp was a hell or a prison where there were strict orders from the Thai authorities.
At that time, the main goal of all political parties based on the Thai border was to get Vietnam out of Cambodia.
Everyone is willing, both soldiers and politicians have decided together that we must unite to solve common problems.
During the camp, there was a lot of gloom, especially for Cambodian children who lacked education.
From then on, I decided to work as an educator for the children in the Cypriots camp and translate for the resistance movement on the border.
He returned home briefly in 1993, and from 1995-1999 he served in the Ministry of Tourism as National Advisor and Under Secretary of State.
In 1999, he left Cambodia for the United States again to study and to break free from corruption and an unclean environment.
Currently, the professor is on a path of life that suits his conscience and ideals.
Mr. Sok Isan acted as a civil servant as predicted
Life was full of obstacles to study at the college level and working as a teacher during the Khmer Rouge genocide and hard work made Sok Isan a brilliant success in life as an official. The government even became a member of parliament for Prey Veng province and a prominent spokesman for the ruling party in 2015, according to what the prophet predicted his fate.
Mr. Sok Isan, a native of Svay Rieng province, was born in August 1946, the second child in a family of six siblings and one foster brother.
He graduated from Secondary School in 1967 and passed the exam as a junior high school professor, and worked as a teacher in his hometown until he became principal.
While teaching in Svay Rieng in 1971, he took the opportunity to continue his studies at two other universities in Phnom Penh: studying law at the Royal University of Phnom Penh and studying at the Faculty of Humanities. It did not end successfully, as in early 1973, Cambodia erupted into a civil war, cutting off road infrastructure due to Khmer Rouge shelling.
Mr. Sok Isan continued that his life story during the Pol Pot era, 3 years, 8 months and 20 days, forced him to leave his family and relatives to live in the forest of Kratie province, transplanting dams, digging canals and working like a machine. Ordered that it is an unforgettable memory.
But after the Pol Pot era, when he was informed that his parents and siblings were still alive, he returned to Phnom Penh by boat, although the road security was still in disarray.
At that time, the Ministry of Industry had only 16 people, and I gathered more than 100 engineers through the list of engineers at the paper factory.
From then on, he worked hard until he was promoted and became Deputy Minister of Industry.
But in 1993, Cambodia held its first elections, and FUNCINPEC was re-elected, changing the leadership of the ministry and a number of structures, forcing him to step down from his ministry to become an adviser to Chea Sim, the former speaker of the National Assembly. At that time.
Seeing the activism of the party, which he has been a member of the Cambodian People's Party since 1979, he was appointed by the head of the government in the second term, led by Prime Minister Hun Sen. He is also a personal consultant and at the same time engaged in the field of information as the Director General of Apsara Radio and Television for 12 years, from 1999 to 2010.
On the way to work, he has never had any major obstacles, only success and won gold medals, national construction, medals of the Kingdom of Great Regiment, the Kingdom of Sereywat and some other medals.
As his work progressed, he was encouraged by CPP leaders to run for Prey Veng constituency and held on to his aspirations in the 2008 by-elections. 2 mandates have come.
During 2015, he was appointed by the party leadership as a spokesman for the CPP, along with two other spokespersons: Preah Vihear provincial lawmaker Sous Yara and Chhim Phal Varun, president of the Institute of Civics.
As for the political situation, Mr. Sok Isan sees improvement from one mandate to another, as Cambodia has properly implemented the Constitution, which states that the Kingdom of Cambodia adheres to a liberal, democratic, multi-party political system and through the use of a culture of dialogue. Reflect the win-win policy!
Of the head of the Royal Government, which caused the Khmer Rouge genocidal regime to collapse and achieve peace and development to the present, as well as to enter into international integration.
At the age of 70, Mr. Sok Isan is determined to follow the leadership of the Cambodian People's Party forever for the cause of national defense and the people, and he has no intention of turning to business or any other business. Remain a civil servant until retirement.
In the near future, he also intends to celebrate his wedding anniversary to strengthen the bond of love for a family that has been holding hands for more than 40 years with four sons and daughters.
At the same time, he called on the younger generation to study hard to live up to the value of their degrees in order to participate in national development by avoiding alcohol, drugs and gambling.
Like my generation, there are so many friends gathered to study at home that we wonder where we explain to each other in our circle of friends.
So it will drown in a good way to learn, but if we concentrate on drinking, smoking, drugs, it does not improve our lives and future.
Preab Kol: Corruption is my enemy
The son of a poor farmer who was born in Svay Chrum district, Svay Rieng province, is determined to sacrifice his life for this nation and the nation to fight corruption, which is the root of many social crises.
He is not a politician, but a distinguished speaker, teacher, scholar in the field of transparency, social integrity, a Master of Political Science in Cambodia, and a specialist certificate in the field of anti-corruption from the International Center for Parliamentary Studies in the United Kingdom. English.
That is Preap Kol, the current executive director of Transparency International Cambodia.
Throughout his life, Preap Kol determined his destiny through three dreams, which for more than 30 years, Preap Kol strived to train to achieve his dreams: first as a teacher, second as a teacher As a leader in the top institutions and third, traveled around the world.
But there is another big dream that Preap Kol is fighting for victory: the fight against corruption.
The 34-year-old media activist has vowed since childhood that when he grows up, he must fight injustice, exploit the weak and bring peace back to society. Both physically and mentally.
In a recent interview with the Post, Preap Kol said emphatically:
From the time I was born until I died, if there was a future, I would continue to fight corruption.
Preap Kol's 18-year working life was largely in an NGO.
Mr. Preap Kol has worked in many international institutions such as:
European Union, National Democratic Institute for International Affairs, NDI, World Bank, United Nations, OXFORD Center for UK Leadership, and Transparency International Cambodia.
In addition, Preap Kol has a role in the Asia-Pacific region of Transparency International and the Coalition of Civil Society Organizations for the United Nations Convention against Corruption.
Work in large international organizations is a rich asset of knowledge and skills in good governance, advocacy, especially in the fight against corruption, promoting integrity and transparency.
The civil society activist has seen the great suffering of some victims of losing their families, homes, injustice, and others losing their lives because of corruption.
Mr. Preap Kol said:
His career and daily work are at high risk, but that does not stop him from completing this important mission.
As an anti-corruption official, he noted that the judiciary, the environment and natural resources, health, education and other sectors are all in the throes of corruption.
Road construction is also corrupt, starting with bids, and corrupt officials conspiring to obtain commissions from traders who need to reduce the amount of cement and gravel, thus leading to poor road quality.
Some roads, the state borrows foreign debt for less than three or four years, the roads are broken, so this corruption has a negative impact on all sectors of society.
Separately, other cases such as poverty, deaths in traffic accidents and crimes that have been happening almost every day are also caused or linked to corruption.
When a society is corrupt, the number of beneficiaries is very small.
Therefore, if Cambodia has assets that are common to a country, but only a small group of powerful individuals have the right to collect and own private property and families, the collective share should be lost.
On the economic side, people may lose the opportunity to do business, leading to poverty, loss of the national budget, as well as the future of the younger generation.
To combat the enormous scale of corruption in Cambodia, Preap Kol used Facebook and set up his own studio to deliver text messages, voice messages and other activities that could reach out to young people who support him. Understand corruption that is hindering political, economic and socio-cultural development.
It is not a call for the people to rise up against the corruption, but to warn the people, especially the youth, not to live in a state of corruption.
But most importantly, we want to relieve some suffering, not all.
Through his anti-corruption mission, he is likely to gain popularity, and he likely thinks that one day he can run for parliament in any constituency if circumstances allow.
But this time, Preap Kol never thought of getting involved in politics, despite the fact that civil society has dubbed him a professional politician.
If society needs and the right time and encouragement from the people to serve him in the form of a politician, I will consider.
Having traveled abroad and studied abroad for many years, Preap Kol aspired to see Cambodia have a truly liberal democracy, not just in the Constitution, not just in paper.
The ultimate goal is to see a corruption-free Cambodia. We want a truly full democracy with the participation of the people, with a clear separation of powers in the major institutions, a balance of power, a sense of responsibility. And there is mutual control.
However, make sure that the perpetrators of corruption face the law or face social punishment.
I want to see society open up to politicians who are willing to serve society and do not want to join any political party.
If Cambodia had a unified voting system at that time, people with good reputation, ability, integrity and popular support could register to run for parliament.
Although I am not a politician, I am currently involved in politics.
My politics is to make people aware of corruption, hate corruption, and stand up against corruption.
With a desire to see a developed country and the rule of law, Preap Kol wants to see Cambodia's politics change like Myanmar and Burma.
The political development in Myanmar, which has reached a stage, makes the people of Burma happy.
In fact, change in Burma is part of the change in human society.
Cambodian politics is no longer moving forward, it is getting old, it means it is no longer working, so we want it to work again, there is only one way to change it to a new form according to the pattern of natural law, otherwise the people will change.
A married man, father of two children and author of a book entitled Success and Meaning of Life, Preap Kol described:
In his 30s, Preap Kol found a successful and happy way of life, both physically and mentally.
Future Work Preap Kol aims to share his knowledge and skills with readers about the life of a poor child who started a life of drama from bitterness to the path of success and happiness as he narrated. In his book.
Politics makes Nhoy Chamroeun's marriage a crossroads
Due to spending too much time and resources on political affairs with the opposition, Nhoy Chamroeun's marriage has reached a crossroads.
The crossroads was ultimately decided by a US court to allow the opposition lawmaker, who was killed twice by the Weibo protesters, to divorce his wife.
However, the young lawmaker did not leave his heart empty, revealing that he now has a fiancé and that his fiancé is in the same patriotic clan as him. .
Nhoy Chamroeun, a former leader of the Student Movement for Democracy and now a CNRP lawmaker from Kampong Cham province, was born on April 30, 1974, to a poor farming family in a remote area in Battambang province His father, Prek Nhoy, died when he was 12 years old, and his mother, Sot Nath, died in 1998 while fleeing the country.
As a child, due to his poor family, Chamroeun was sent by his mother to live in Wat Vel to study.
After graduating from primary school in Bavel commune, Mr. Chamroeun had to continue his studies in Thmor Koul commune by asking him to stay at Wat Thomayut.
After studying for two years, Mr. Chamroeun had to go up to study in Battambang provincial town and stay at Wat Tuol Ta Ek.
During that time, in 1991, Chamroeun also passed the provincial poetry competition.
At that time, I joined the International Committee of the Red Cross and tried to learn English and later became an English teacher and saved money.
Mr. Chamroeun came to Phnom Penh in 1996 to apply for exams at a number of universities.
Due to lack of money, Mr. Chamroeun was forced to apply for an exam at a foreign language university after hearing that the manager was an Australian foreign teacher, and only those who really knew were selected.
In the end, the language university screened only 70 out of thousands of students who applied.
The Cambodian-American man went on to tell the Post about his background while sitting on a sofa in a small apartment in Sen Sok district on November 30, 2015:
But things did not go smoothly because of the injustice and corruption in society, especially the children of the poor could not afford to go to college like the children of the rich, even to the pagoda. Without wires, it was difficult to get a place to stay, during which time Chamroeun met a friend who took him under the stairs of the Techno School.
At the beginning of the third academic year, during 1998, Mr. Chamroeun joined the initiative of the Student Movement for Democracy with Mr. Saro Sivutha and Mr. Keo Hokly.
At the time, Chamroeun and other student movement leaders led protests against various social injustices and irregularities in the 1998 election, which led to the government's violent crackdown and arrest of protest leaders.
At the time, Chamroeun dropped out of college and was evacuated to Bangkok by the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights.
Later, due to the changing political situation in Thailand, the United Nations sent Chamroeun to the US Embassy, and Chamroeun was interviewed as a refugee in the United States in 2001.
Arriving in the United States, Chamroeun worked for the Cambodian Association in Philadelphia and continued his studies until earning a master's degree in anthropology in 2006.
He also worked for the US State Department's Refugee Assistance Program and at the same time was a Sam Rainsy Party activist.
During 2006, when Kem Sokha's Human Rights Party was formed, Chamroeun actively participated as chairman of the Human Rights Party in Philadelphia, as Secretary-General of the United States and Canada. CNRP in the United States and Canada.
Mr. Chamroeun returned to Cambodia before the 2013 election, running as a candidate for the Kampong Cham constituency and was elected.
Mr. Chamroeun is an active speaker who has been outspoken in his criticism of the inaction of the CPP-led government.
Perhaps because of this relentless criticism, on May 2, 2014, Mr. Chamroeun was severely injured.
Mr. Chamroeun went on to describe the injuries he had just received from a Thai doctor who underwent orthopedic surgery, and that he was still in pain when the CNRP signed an agreement to end the political crisis in July 2014. Like 54 other CNRP lawmakers, he entered the National Assembly as a member of the Fifth Committee.
However, despite being lucky enough to live in the United States, become a US citizen, study for a master's degree in the United States, have a good job and a high salary in the United States, Chamroeun never forgot his origins as a poor farmer. That too.
It is said that entering politics is faced with three problems: first, death, second imprisonment, and third, deportation, but for Chamroeun, he was ready for these three challenges before entering politics with the opposition to Leading to change the leaders who have ruled Cambodia for more than three decades.
He commented:
Think again, like me, that if there is no risk, there is no change, if one of our lives can save the nation, then it must be done.
He said the interception of him and another opposition lawmaker, Kong Saphea, was a systematic arrangement, as the day of the incident at the entrance to the National Assembly did not have the same police force as before. Security scanners for lawmakers were reportedly broken and CPP supporters were later heard protesting.
And in particular, our car is closed until the time to go out, it's only on our side, and on the other hand, all of us holding ICOM have communication with each other.
Mr. Chamroeun married a Cambodian-American woman in Las Vegas in 2004 and was divorced by a US court in 2012, giving his wife the sole custody of his children, but he was obliged to provide one month's child support. $ 465.
Mr. Chamroeun stated that the reason why his marriage had to reach a crossroads was that, firstly, because his wife did not seem to support my story, I became involved in politics, and then there were other things, but I do not want to talk too much about personal matters, but just that understanding is not the same, and I also sacrifice too much because of the national story that makes him unacceptable ....
After the divorce, he fell in love with the daughter of CNRP lawmaker Cheam Channy and got engaged in April 2015.
Because of this clarity, Mr. Chamroeun dared to swear the plane crash to anyone who said that his beating in the National Assembly was caused by a love triangle.
The military, the police, the judiciary must be truly independent ....
Chea Chamroeun says he also suffered from an independent judiciary
Although he graduated with a degree in law and political science and held senior government positions and was a member of the National Assembly, Chea Chamroeun stated that he was one of many victims of the Cambodian people. Because the judiciary is not independent.
This injustice was exposed to the public after Chea Chamroeun decided to resign from the ruling CPP, which he thought was full of partisanship.
I was accused of gambling in Naga, in Chrey Thom, in Prek Chak, so parents should not believe this accusation, they should call for questioning but not at all.
Chea Chamroeun told the Post in an interview at the Sunway Hotel on Wednesday.
He said that after suffering from injustice and wanting to seek justice for society, he decided to form a new political party called the Cambodian Freedom Party.
His party's main political program focuses on youth and occupation, injustice in the judiciary, where innocent people are imprisoned, and more local jobs are created for decent wages to repatriate migrant workers.
He added that his party was not referring to individuals, but to systematic, anti-corruption reforms, with individual factors, knowledge that matched salaries, livelihoods and the political ideology of each group that wins when people are no longer corrupt. Fair and good system.
Mr. Chamroeun's party will run in the upcoming commune and national elections, saying he is not worried about human resources so far because he has many students and students know teachers.
Mr. Chamroeun holds a Doctor of Technology and Innovation Management, a Doctor of Laws, a Master of Political Science, a Master of Laws, a Bachelor of Laws, a Bachelor of Laws in Public Administration and has been an Advisor to the President of the National Assembly, Government, Member of Parliament for Kandal Province, Member of the Election Commission and Vice Chairman. Chairman of the Inspection and Inspection Commission and a teacher.
He was also the founder and rector of Chamroeun University of Polytechnology, but was later transferred to another.
Chea Chamroeun was born on July 7, 1970, a false age to be able to attend school.
In 1969, my father was ousted by Prince Norodom Sihanouk until a coup in 1972. Lon Nol wanted my father back in Banteay Slek.
Mr. Chamroeun's father, Mok Chea Te, was not happy with the commander at Banteay Sleuk and was later killed at Wat Dambok Khpos.
I was born in Sangkat 7, Phnom Penh, an orphan living with my mother.
In 1975, during the Khmer Rouge regime, Chamroeun was assigned as a children's unit, and until after the liberation in 1979, he left for the Thai-Cambodian border with the intention of going to a third country with five family members.
He said that in 1981, the old camp along the Thai-Cambodian border was destroyed by Vietnamese troops.
Between 1981 and 1984, there were battles almost every day, no less than 100 encounters in battle at that time, then Dien asked me to join the group of children, soldiers, soldiers, children, then I learned myself And teach more than 300 orphans in the camp.
Until 1988, Mr. Chamroeun passed the exam as a Swarin candidate and went to train active officers in Lopburi, Thailand, ranking first among 107 people in the ninth generation.
This includes fighting and learning to push for the Paris talks.
We were young, inexperienced, very easy to use, if you know how many millions of dollars now do not go to war, but then one to 100 people dare.
Bored of the war in 1990, he fled to another camp, Say Thou, where Chamroeun set up a controversial Cambodian youth development organization, but he adapted to that society by training more than 3,000 children. Let people have knowledge.
In 1993, Mr. Chamroeun returned to Cambodia.
Chea Chamroeun was married in 1993 in Banteay Meanchey province, where his father-in-law was a member of the CPP.
After returning from the border with only his body and being an orphan, Chea Chamroeun had to take refuge with his father-in-law, whom his father-in-law helped him a lot after marriage, which was ត 27.
So far, Mr. Chamroeun has four children, the youngest of whom is a bachelor, and two are officers at the National Defense University, in the staff and senior commanders.
He did not want his son to serve in the army, but he said that one day the law would require him to do the same.
Ms. Chak Sopheap gave up her dream of becoming a doctor to take up human rights work
At the age of 30, Chak Sopheap became the Executive Director of the Cambodian Center for Human Rights (CCHR), which works to promote human rights.
After international pressure and a campaign for freedom of expression, all activists were released, and Kem Sokha resigned as center in 2007 to pursue a career as a politician.
This time, the position was transferred to Mr. Ou Virak.
After leading the center for many years, in March 2014, Mr. Virak resigned and Ms. Chak Sopheap was elected Executive Director of CCHR.
The fact that she is now the CCHR leader is due to the management and structure of the center, which mandates the change of leadership to provide opportunities for the next generation.
In her leadership role, Ms. Chak Sopheap must ensure that operations and missions for the protection and promotion of human rights are improved and that the principles of the CCHR structure are respected through the transfer of power to the next generation. Mandate.
Sopheap was also one of the outstanding women for Cambodia, who was honored by US President Barack Obama on September 23, 2014 at the annual meeting of the Clinton World Initiative in New York. Discussed with a number of senior officials and linked human rights work with civil society partners in 15 countries during her mission.
Since joining the human rights community at CCHR, Chak Sopheap has been involved in a number of campaigns demanding the release of Kem Sokha and a number of human rights activists who were imprisoned in late 2005, as well as joining the community. In demanding social justice, demanding the basic rights of citizens and promoting respect for human rights in Cambodia.
In addition to her career as a human rights defender, Sopheap has also written articles on social, political and good governance, which have been monitored by government officials.
However, since serving as a human rights defender for 10 years, she has not been subjected to any threats, but institutions and working groups have faced threats and confrontations with the authorities.
According to the graph, the turn of the human rights situation is like a wave, which means that sometimes respect for human rights is better and sometimes there is a serious decline, especially in the case of beatings of MPs. Opposition, an event like never before.
Ms. Sopheap said:
It does not exist, and the other is the arbitrary removal of parliamentary immunity in the National Assembly, it seems that it does not follow the rule of law at all.
If we want to promote Cambodia in accordance with the principles of democracy and the rule of law, and if we do not follow the law, how do we want to promote the rule of law?
Ah, that's what I'm worried about.
She also recalled the human rights situation during the 2013 election, which was seen as improving because the government was more tolerant, giving communities a chance to express themselves and gather, but after the election, some laws were issued. Manage NGOs, trade union laws, and plan to make Internet laws, etc. These laws restrict the basic freedoms of citizens.
Ms. Chak Sopheap suggested that politicians should consider the national interest, avoid using abusive words and criticize each other, which affects the human rights sector, investment and make the political environment gloomy and scary while the election. Coming soon.
Looking back on the future, Chak Sopheap still intends to contribute to improving the human rights situation in Cambodia by engaging in NGO work.
As the youngest daughter in the family and a young woman leader, Ms. Sopheap sent a message to all young people that we must dare to dream, which means that everyone has to have our dreams and what we have to do. To achieve our dreams and have confidence in ourselves is the most important thing that makes our work accomplished and no one can build trust in ourselves, only ourselves .
If we build a society that values and encourages people, then our society will develop.
Kim Sour Phirith: I want to see a change of leadership without violence
Mr. Phirith sees that the government seems to have very little in the way of preventing social violence, especially violence against political parties that oppose it or those who do not support it.
I am deeply concerned about Prime Minister Hun Sen's repeated statements that there will be war if the CPP loses the election.
Considering the possibilities of 2017 and 2018, Kim Sour Phirith said it was important that key political leaders begin to understand their duty to prevent Cambodia from slipping into violence. And war.
Mr. Phirith continued:
Genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes and crimes against minorities.
Cambodia once slipped under the Khmer Rouge regime, which killed nearly 2 million innocent people between 1975 and 1979.
The lawmaker stated that the beating of two lawmakers with immunity on October 26, 2015, MP Nhoy Chamroeun and Kong Saphea, meant setting a bad example in Cambodian politics. Serious concern about the political environment in the country today and a sign of slipping into violence.
In fact, the duty of each state to protect all parties, especially the MPs who are elected by the people every five years, should not be beaten near the fence.
Dr. Kim Sour Phirith jumped into politics with opposition leaders for the sole purpose of positive change.
The lawmaker, who has three children, said that change is important in each country for the people of each country, the change will bring positive changes for the development of both economic, social and lifestyle. Citizens.
Mr. Phirith stressed that:
For political views in Cambodia, Kim Sour Phirith sees the only way to think together is a good choice for the development of this poor country.
He says that Sam Rainsy is a person who deserves to play a large part in leading Cambodia to development, no one is perfect, and because everyone is not perfect, change is needed.
So I think if one has the ability, it can jointly push the country's leadership towards rapid development and make our country prosperous, and the subsequent changes will have a positive effect.
I think compared to what the government is doing today, in terms of economy, social affairs and other areas, Sam Rainsy really has a much longer vision.
Born in Kampong Cham City, Kampong Cham Province, Kim Sour Phirith attended primary school in Kampong Cham City.
He then went on to study at Sihanoukville High School in Kampong Cham province, and in 1973 when the Khmer Rouge invaded Kampong Cham, he continued his studies at Preah Yukanthor High School in Phnom Penh.
After passing the diploma exam, he entered the modern second grade and left Phnom Penh in 1975.
In 1982, Phirith studied for a doctorate at the Faculty of Science in Phnom Penh and graduated with a doctorate.
Tycoon Try Pheap, CEO of Try Pheap Group, accused by national and international civil society organizations of destroying Cambodia's forests under the right to invest in economic land concessions for deforestation The country gave the following special interview to the Post about this business:
The general public has suggested that the tycoon runs a logging business throughout Cambodia under the label of economic land concessions from the government. Can the tycoon explain this?
And invite organizations to go to Anlong Reap again, how much can be planted, how much pepper can be planted, if Try Pheap thinks only of the timber business, he can not build a fence, make a company to honor you to come and sit there, the fence is closed.
Asked if the land in Pursat pays a tax of 90 million US dollars, if the destruction of timber does not have to pay taxes, transport and sell, it is called deforestation.
So, the container transport service, there are customs, there are all institutions, government officials can participate, we have the right to transport and sell in China, I buy from all the people, clearing from the concessions, I buy from $ 700 to $ 1,000 per cube. US dollars, and we sell in China for only 2,500 to 3,000 dollars, down to export taxes, harvest, shipping, so the rest is only $ 300 per cubic meter.
Therefore, all sister organizations, please have pity on clear and honest business people.
Can the tycoon explain that the payment of $ 90 million is an annual tax or not?
Pay $ 90 million, yielding crops, for example, 1,000 cubic meters of timber, 2,000 cubic meters, we always pay taxes, so before exporting, we always pay taxes, not annually, so the government has determined that if we can do business, we pay taxes.
The first export, we pay legal taxes, the second export to other countries, we have the resources if the Cambodian people who collect wood from the concession to sell to us 200 cubic, 500 cubic or 1,000 cubic We buy accordingly and pay the export tax legally, so each month we produce 50 boxes, 150 boxes, 300 boxes, pay the tax to the Customs Commission as a package set by the Royal Government and check and measure very thoroughly.
Apart from exports to China, are there any other countries because there is a report that exports to China pass through the port of Vietnam?
Today, explaining the problem of January 7, Techo Sen and myself, I do not touch anyone, if I touch them, it is abject because I do not answer myself.
We chase in the collection of wood that the Minister of Environment, the Minister of Agriculture, Forestry Administration, we collect throughout the country.
There is no corruption, bribery, but civil servants do not get a riel, but all the riel is corrupt, the bidders are asked how much I have to say, honestly, the bidding is detrimental to the national interest.
Due to its notoriety for deforestation, opposition lawmakers also asked the National Assembly through Heng Samrin to delete the name of his company. What do you think about this?
If the name is bad and go to Prey Sar, they say it is bad, but if the name is bad and still stand and talk to you, there is something bad, it is very unusual, very influential, the Royal Government leads the country, who is good and who is bad, he knows The mistake that can easily get your claim denied is to fail.
He did not let us destroy the nation alone, he liberated the very difficult territory of Cambodia from the genocidal suffering of Pol Pot.
I stopped logging today, my truck is transporting timber, which is said to be destroying my forest, today I got it from the Forestry Administration to transport money to Cambodia, to pay taxes to Cambodia before transporting all the money.
Until I became a successful Cambodian child, my life was difficult to describe. I was originally born in Tbeng commune, Kandal Stung district, Kandal province, in the 80's, more than my grandmother's ghost, she died.
I climbed a palm tree, my chest was peeled off.
I paid them 1 riel for Khmer money, so all the farmers cut down the palm trees, planted rice fields, and reminded me of the original story, which reminded me of the unfortunate story, but the expensive story because of my glory.
My own struggle was born in honor of the title of Duke Try Pheap and to establish this company, Try Pheap Group, thanks to both great powers, Samdech Techo Sen and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, he trained me from the beginning to the end. The success of Try Pheap Group is the success of January 7, January 7, only made Teapheap stand with the honor of Tycoon Try Pheap and Try Pheap Group to this day.
Moeung Son still happy despite losing hotel
After receiving a royal pardon in April 2015, the 69-year-old Moeung Son once again expressed his joy, even during his more than five years in exile. Last year, he lost his hotel and company, as well as his land, his livelihood.
At the same time, Mr. Son recalled some brief histories of his life when he met Prime Minister Hun Sen during his first visit to Paris in 1985.
While Hun Sen was visiting France for the first time, Cambodians living in France at the time were dissatisfied with Hun Sen because they hated the Vietnamese colonial government.
At that time, only me and Hun Sen's relatives went to receive him, and at that time the Cambodian government did not have diplomatic relations with France, but he stayed at the Lao embassy.
Since then, Son has begun to engage with Hun Sen's government.
In 1988, seeing the political situation in Cambodia change as all Cambodian sides began to negotiate for peace, Son came up with another new venture to have a chance to help Cambodia, so he set up a business venture. Tourism.
In 1988, Son opened a tour company in Cambodia at a time when Cambodia was under siege.
At that time, a Cambodian who came to Cambodia with both his own money and a deposit of at least $ 20,000 each made the local people who had just recovered from the Khmer Rouge regime, and I was assigned to represent tourism in Europe. Bring Khmer tourists home.
At that time, it was difficult to provide services, travel and safety.
After the 1993 election, Son saw the destruction of wildlife and set up an organization called the Wildlife Conservation Organization for the purpose of conserving wildlife and forests.
In 1995, he was elected a member of the Chamber of Commerce in Phnom Penh, representing services until 2003, when he entered politics with the Sam Rainsy Party because he wanted to be a member of parliament to help the country.
During one year of his political career with the opposition, Moeung Son lost all customers who had bought airline tickets and travel services from his company, especially ruling party officials.
In 2008, Son founded the Foundation for the Support of Khmer Civilization, and in 2009 he criticized the excavation of Angkor Wat for lighting at night.
Mr. Son was born in 1946 to poor parents in Prek Reang village, Takhmao commune, Kandal Steung district, Kandal province.
In 1950, due to instability in the country, Son's family moved to Phnom Penh, and his mother started selling grilled seafood at a Chinese hospital.
It was not until 1967 that he returned to work in an oil factory in Kampong Som.
In 1970, the Yeak Kong bombed an oil factory that Son set on fire.
During the Khmer Rouge regime in 1976, Son was imprisoned for 45 days and severely tortured.
The Khmer Rouge tortured me until I fainted.
In September 1979, Son arrived in France alone and then wrote a letter to a Cambodian refugee camp seeking information about his wife.
Two daughters graduated in medicine and one boy graduated in engineering.
Son had five children who died during the Khmer Rouge regime.
In France, Son tried to sew clothes for the Jews for a few months, but stopped because of the stigma against Cambodian workers.
In 1985, he bought a new restaurant that was bigger than before and opened a tour company until 1988 when he came to Cambodia.
Son was tried in absentia and deported for more than five years.
During his more than five years abroad, he lost his hotel and company, as well as some land.
Son, who speaks French, English, Thai and Lao, says his last life will be to continue associating with the cultural movement.
Kim Sour Phirith, a member of the National Assembly's Committee on Legislation and Justice from the Cambodia National Rescue Party (CNRP), revealed his political background as a member of parliament for 20 years, with the sole political goal of wanting a change of leadership. By voting for the national interest.
Phirith began his political career after the 1993 UN elections during a time of internal divisions within FUNCINPEC, especially when Sam Rainsy was expelled from FUNCINPEC and removed. From the Minister of Economy and Finance.
His goal is to fight against injustice and corruption in society.
Before entering politics, Phirith graduated in 1989, at the same time as the Vietnamese army withdrew from Cambodia.
He said that due to political discrimination in 1998, he resigned as a doctor while he was a member of parliament in Banteay Meanchey after the 1998 election until the 2003 national election again. He was elected to the National Assembly for another term in the same constituency until 2008.
In the 2008 election, he was re-elected in Kampong Cham province until today.
I have a vision that if the leadership is in the same political party and comes from a source with a communist tendency, then I think Cambodia will face more problems, both social development and injustice, so that makes me Participate in politics to bring about reform by starting writing articles for the Voice of the Khmer Youth newspaper at the time.
What Phirith will not forget is the four grenades thrown at a non-violent crowd in front of the National Assembly on March 30, 1997, demanding judicial reform and justice because of the judiciary. Become a political tool and no longer a system that seeks justice for victims and society.
I was hit by two shrapnel, one on my back and left untouched so far, the other on my ankle and the third one on my thigh. Fortunately, at that time I had a cell phone and put it in my pants pocket, the bullet stuck to the battery of the phone, if I did not have a replacement phone, I would have lost my life.
This is my luck.
Mr. Phirith said that a number of people, including journalists, had died in the bombing, and that the perpetrators had not yet been brought to justice.
Mr. Phirith was born in Kampong Cham province. He started studying in 1965 with his father as a teacher.
During the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era until 1970, he graduated from primary school and in 1973, near the diploma exam, he fled the province due to the country's civil war and continued his studies in Phnom Penh at Yukunthor School until the year The Khmer Rouge invaded Phnom Penh, and he graduated from the first grade.
When the Khmer Rouge entered Phnom Penh, he was evacuated to Kampong Cham to work in the same mobile unit until 1979.
Phirith does not want his children to get involved in politics like he does, however, he says he has no right to ban them if they are capable.
Alex: I am ready to sacrifice my life for Cambodia
Recently, Facebook and national and international media reported on a well-known Spanish man in the case of protecting the Areng area, Cambodia's last forest of about 400,000 hectares. Located in Koh Kong province of Cambodia.
Who is Alex and why is his work hindered by the Royal Government of Cambodia?
Alex was born in Spain, but left Spain at the age of 16.
Due to the difficulty of adapting to the way of life in Spain, Alex came to live in England for two years and then continued to live in Thailand for a year and finally Cambodia.
Alex recalled:
I had applied for citizenship three times through the Ministry of Interior, but they told me that as long as I had $ 50,000, they would run away immediately, but if I did not have $ 50,000, they would not give me.
I think the story of nationality in the heart is there and the conscience is Khmer, the face is foreign, but the heart is pure Khmer, the Khmer passport is not there, but I say the passport is not important, the most important thing is the will, the conscience and the heart.
This 37-year-old man speaks Khmer fluently and has a good understanding of Khmer culture.
He now considers himself one of more than 14 million Cambodians and is ready to sacrifice his life for the country.
Alex says:
He is still unsure of the latest developments in relation to expulsions due to the expiration of visas, as no department or Ministry of Interior has been in direct contact with him.
The Cambodian government has urged the environmentalist and forester to leave Cambodia for a while and wait to be readmitted later.
Alex also did not believe that the Cambodian government would send him back to Spain by plane or at any border.
In this case, Alex says:
The new Alex seems determined to protect the Areng area because it is 100 times more valuable than his life and that of his family.
Alex generally does not have the same fear of assassination as Cambodian forestry activist Chut Wutty.
What we do every day, we have a way of making the government catch up, they see Alex Alex as the leader or the leader of the movement, they forget that there are still comedians who are more active than Alex. 100 times.
I can give an example like June 6, 2014 and delegated all duties to Cambodian forest activists, so if Alex is not in the forest protection movement, he will remain the same, as I said again and again, Alex is just a symbol. Only one.
For this reason, Alex has recently emerged as one of the most influential forest protection activists in the media.
Alex said:
The loss of Areng would be of great benefit to Cambodia, and Areng is not one of Cambodia's batteries, as the authorities claim.
He said that what his group is doing every day is just a symbol, and the Mother Nature movement is sacrificing everything for the Areng area. Kiri Stung Treng, we are starting to take the lead, soon we will see a stronger movement to protect the forest than we do.
Alex confirmed:
Last week, activists, environmentalists and monks filed a petition to the National Assembly to intervene to get Alex to stay.
In this case, Alex thought that they should transfer from wanting to help Alex to help Areng is better.
I am not a drug dealer, I am not a thief, I am shooting, I am telling the truth, protecting the forest from destruction, what is wrong?
Social injustice and corrupt conspiracy between senior government officials and court officials have forced an official who claims to be clean and honest to serve the nation to speak out about his or her suffering in the face of late Cambodia. Weekly of the Phnom Penh Post, although he has no name in the society.
Channy's case may be a rare case in which government officials dare to speak out against the same government officials and the judiciary, with some senior government officials often commenting on criticism that the courts are doing things wrong. According to the law, the judiciary is an independent power and no one can interfere ....
Mr. Channy has been on trial since mid-2011 for what he says has left him emotionally miserable as court orders continue to haunt him.
Despite a series of indictments against him, Mr. Channy stated that he would continue to serve the country as usual until now.
Mr. Channy said that out of love for his country, he left the United States to live in Cambodia during 1989 and joined politics for FUNCINPEC until after the 1993 election, the first of its kind. By the United Nations called UNTAC.
In the aftermath of the 1993 elections, political unrest erupted over the non-recognition of election results, which led to the formation of a coalition government with two prime ministers.
In the 2008 national election, Channy ran as the sixth FUNCINPEC candidate in Kampong Thom province.
In 2010, he joined the CPP and became Deputy Secretary General of the National Authority for Combating Drugs.
Channy was born into a middle-class family in Phnom Penh.
He graduated from the National Modern Sixth Grade before the Khmer Rouge regime.
After the Khmer Rouge army took control of Phnom Penh in 1975, his family was evacuated to Maung Russey district, Battambang province.
Channy lived in the Nong Chan camp for a short time and was selected to live in the United States in late 1979 as a refugee.
After studying for three years, Channy decided to return to Cambodia when it was open.
Out of love for my homeland, I decided to leave them, but still came and went as usual, even though I had a wife and children in Cambodia.
Channy's ex-wife agreed to marry Channy in 1998 in Cambodia.
Channy now has four children, two ex-wives and two new wives, all of whom now live in the United States.
In Cambodia, Channy has done humanitarian work in collaboration with the United States in searching for the remains of American soldiers who died in Cambodia during the 1970 war.
Vann Molyvann hides in Siem Reap for the rest of his life
On a small red dirt road along the Siem Reap River, about 500 meters from a main road that Siem Reap people call 60 meters, passers-by are fascinated by a French-style fashion house, despite the fence. Near.
When asked by neighbors, this traditional mansion was the residence of the famous architect of Cambodia during the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era, Vann Molyvann.
In just five minutes along the red dirt road, which is littered with ditches and small houses along the river, where people sell vegetables, fruits and a few groceries for a living, we will reach the mansion where Mr. Vann Molyvann came to stay with his wife to calm down in his last life.
But it is said that he was definitely one of the top Cambodian architects and the most famous in the Sangkum era, now he came to live here.
Upon entering the location that Mr. Vann Molyvann has chosen as a permanent residence, it is surrounded by beautiful nature, a quiet natural environment and a collection of all kinds of plants, large and small, which is a very desirable place to live.
The villa is located in Traing village, Slor Kram commune, Siem Reap district. It is a two-story stone house with light white limestone walls and a porch for sitting.
Few people in Siem Reap knew about Vann Molyvann's presence in the province as a major cultural tourist destination because he and his family did not want to surprise their neighbors.
During the weekend meeting with Cambodia in the luxurious book room, he appeared in a neat and tidy dress, but what is even more interesting is that he is almost 90 years old and he can still walk up the stairs one by one. There is no need for a mourner.
But there is also Uncle Choeun, a top person who is watching carefully from behind.
His soft smile was clearly visible on his serious face.
With each welcome but clear statement:
He confirmed:
He said slowly, looking out the window, looking away, as if to show that there are still many things stuck in his life.
He stated that he decided to move to Siem Reap because he had previously worked in the province as the head of the Apsara Authority in 2002.
By the time he received the royal decree, he had signed the decree, and he had issued another decree appointing me as his personal adviser.
Because he had many memories with Siem Reap, he brought his family back to live in Siem Reap in order to spend the rest of his life to live in peace from worries.
He confirmed that he has been living in Siem Reap for six months, since the end of July 2014.
Mr. Vann Molyvann said:
After he was removed from office by the Royal Government of Apsara Authority, Vann Molyvann spent about four years compiling a book entitled Borey in Southeast Asia, Past and Present, for future generations of Cambodians to explore.
This book was completed and published in 750 copies in Khmer and 1,000 in French in 2009 with the support of Samdech Preah Bat Norodom Sihanouk, Samdech Mey Monineath Sihanouk and Preah Bat Samdech Norodom Sihamoni, including Mr. Prime Minister Hun Sen.
Borey books in Southeast Asia, past and present, are being translated into English in London, England, which will be completed in 2015.
In addition to this documentary, Vann Molyvann has also compiled other books as a legacy for the next generation of Cambodian children who wish to follow in his footsteps.
Those books include the Phnom Penh Urban Planning Project and the Ancient Khmer City Planning Book.
Mr. Vann Molyvann added that previous Khmer history books did not focus on some areas that were important to Cambodia's prehistory.
At the same time, students do not always pay attention to the past, especially the Khmer Rouge era.
So the purpose of his documentary is to try to fill in the blanks in more detail than any previous author.
On top of the bookshelf are nine ancient Khmer statues, one of which houses the head of Jayavarman VII.
Next to the window, there is a leather sculpture hanging on the wall, which makes the book room even more beautiful and attractive.
Vann Molyvann was born on November 23, 1926 in Ream district of that generation in Kampot province.
Vann Molyvann received his high school diploma from Preah Sisowath High School in Phnom Penh in 1944 and received a scholarship to study law in Paris, France in 1946.
After studying there for a year, he transferred to study architecture at a school of fine arts in Paris.
In the 60's and 70's, Vann Molyvann designed many famous buildings in Cambodia, especially in Phnom Penh.
Some of his works stand out in harmony with the ancient buildings, pagodas and remnants of the colonial era.
During that period, known as the Golden Age, his major achievements were the National Olympic Stadium, the Independence Monument, the Chaktomuk Conference Hall, the Tonle Bassac Theater, the fire that destroyed the old cabinet building, and the Chamkarmon State Palace, Pochentong International Airport, Phnom Penh. .
He also designed many Cambodian pagodas and embassies abroad.
In 1962, Vann Molyvann designed the 60,000-capacity National Stadium, one of the most valued stadiums in Southeast Asia.
The stadium, built to Olympic standards, is still the single largest sports venue in Cambodia.
Originally built at high speed to host the 1963 Asian Games, it was later canceled and inaugurated in 1964 with a standing ovation.
But in 1971 he left Cambodia after the Lon Nol regime took over Cambodia in 1970.
He returned home in 1991, when in 1993 he was appointed by King Norodom Sihanouk as an adviser to the Royal Government.
Recalling his work during the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era, which today has suffered a series of devastation, he took a deep breath as if to express his deep pain over the loss of what he had worked so hard to build for a miserable nation. This one.
After reading a summary of Vann Molyvann's architectural work from 1956 to 1970, he briefly described his work, which was destroyed without remorse:
He just stood and watched as the works were destroyed, how many people could bear the pain like him.
However, now Vann Molyvann has come to live in Siem Reap, which could also be used as a medicine to help heal some of his trauma.
Today, Vann Molyvann has three sons and three daughters.
Five sons and daughters are living in France, while the youngest daughter is living in Cambodia.
Reminiscent of his work in Siem Reap, there are not many, only the first airport in the Angkor area, built in 1963, today has become an army barracks.
From 1958 to 1966, he hosted a light show on Angkor Wat, lighting and sound techniques for performing arts during a state visit to Siem Reap by Indonesian President Sukano, President Tito and General Charles de Gaulle.
In particular, he also drew blueprints in Siem Reap when he worked for the Apsara Authority.
Wanting to emphasize the permanent residence in this province, Mr. Nhim said that I have already found a place to build a stupa for myself and my wife, which is located in Wat Preah Indakosi or Wat Leu, an old pagoda and present. Ta Moeung temple in the pagoda.
Am Sam Ath Remains Strong on Human Rights Work Despite Death Threats
Despite the frequent death threats over the phone and the four accidents that resulted in injuries due to the vigorous activism in coming forward to help protect the abused people. Despite the abuse, Am Sam Ath remains very loyal to his work as LICADHO's Chief of Technical Investigations.
It is almost common for journalists, especially when it comes to LICADHO, to refer to Am Sam Ath, who is an outspoken critic of the government's negativity after Pung Chhiv Kek and Srey Naly Pilorge, the organization's top leader.
LICADHO is now widely known for its human rights support services in all capitals and provinces across the country.
Am Sam Ath was born on January 26, 1973, to a farming family in Kandal province's Ang Snuol district, with three siblings, one sister and the eldest.
After he was born in 1973, his family moved to Sangke district, Battambang province, where his father enlisted in the military under Lon Nol.
Under the Khmer Rouge regime, Sam Ath's family continued to live in Battambang province and suffered the same fate as the general Cambodian population.
When the Khmer Rouge regime was overthrown in 1979, Sam Ath's family moved to Banteay Meanchey's Mongkul Borei district.
At that time, Sam Ath was over 10 years old and also helped make jars and sell jars after taking time off from school.
In 1991, after failing the exam, Mr. Sam Ath left Mongkul Borei district for Phnom Penh to continue his studies, passing the exam at the School of Electrical Engineering.
During that time, he came to stay with relatives, but relatives also had a poor life, which could only afford to provide him with accommodation, so Mr. Sam Ath had to take some time to earn money to support himself. Study.
After graduating from the School of Electrical Engineering in 1997, Sam Ath began his career as an electrical engineer at the Sam Ling Plywood factory in Kandal province's Kien Svay district.
At the board factory, the company, especially the foreman, always persecuted the workers.
Mr. Sam Ath said that in the Sam Ling factory at night, company bosses often harassed many workers, while inspectors when inspecting the factory, they did not visit. Where the workers work, he only goes to the company's administration and then comes out without helping the workers who are oppressed by the company.
Immediately after leaving Sam Ling, Am Sam Ath went to work for a short time at the Takeo Provincial Department of Industry, but there was nothing to do.
Coincidentally, at that time, LICADHO announced the recruitment of his staff, and he hated the harassment and oppression of the workers at the Samling factory, so he applied for a job with LICADHO and was fired. He was selected to work in January 1998.
When he first started working, he was assigned to work in Kandal province, where he worked in both human rights and law.
Speaking strongly about his work as a human rights defender, Mr. Sam Ath said that he had been threatened by the military several times, especially in 1998, when there was a crisis. Extremely political tensions.
He says that when there is a demonstration or a problem, such as the shooting death of a journalist, a union, a citizen, or the authorities oppressing or threatening an innocent citizen, he must go to the base in time to gather information as well. Monitor those demonstrations or events.
Due to his activism, which was always present at almost every event, he was noticed by the authorities, the armed forces, who considered him his target, and sometimes he did. The summons accused him of inciting people to protest.
In the end, Am Sam Ath suffered four traffic accidents, in 2006, 2008, 2010 and 2014, which he claimed were caused by a group of people who often threatened to kill him. Fortunately, his age did not endanger his life, he was only injured and had to be hospitalized.
Mr. Sam Ath said that he did the wrong thing he learned, but he did the right thing that he loved, and that is why he worked without fear, despite the pressure. Must strive to help victims who need the help of human rights organizations like him.
I want to see our country have a real democracy and I want all people to understand their rights.
Because I was threatened many times, I was prepared for what would happen to me one day.
His wife, who now lives in Phnom Penh's Chroy Changvar district, runs a home-based business.
During his 17 years as a human rights official, he gained a reputation for his support from his wife and family.
However, he confirmed that:
But because of the explanation, he turned to support me to continue this work to help society.
Mr. Am Sam Ath stated that while working as a human rights officer at LICADHO for about 17 years, what he could not forget was that there were two points: First, I was happy and could not remember. Forgetting to see the victim receive human rights services, and secondly, death threats and intimidation, which caused him to have up to four traffic accidents.
However, until now, Mr. Sam Ath has not thought about whether he will stop working for human rights in the future to work as a civil servant or join any political party because he Given the current situation in Cambodia, poor people still need the support of human rights organizations.
Sisowath Thomico: If Cambodian society is divided, it is for foreign benefit!
Prince Thomico said:
Most importantly, he defended and supported Sihanouk's policy on national reconciliation and border issues.
The prince continued:
Prince Thomico points out that he was the founder of FUNCINPEC, founded by the King Father in Pyongyang, North Korea on March 21, 1981, which was later led by King Norodom Ranariddh since his victory. Until the failure of the next election due to the division and deviation of Sihanoukism, as opposed to bragging with the people.
He also tried to form his own party, the Socialist Party, in 2006-2007, but failed again and withdrew from politics due to the lack of support for the procession of His Majesty the King. Norodom Sihanouk during that time.
Suddenly, during the funeral procession, Phnom Penh was closed to the public, and I could see that they intended to eliminate the work of Samdech Ov and did not want the people to continue to love and respect him.
Therefore, I also looked at the fact that the CNRP followed Sihanoukism the most compared to other parties, so I decided to join this party.
Thomico said that before joining the CNRP, he had contacted Sam Rainsy and Kem Sokha, the party's vice president, to clarify their political stance on whether to follow Sihanoukism.
And they both promised to take into account the lives of the people and eliminate corruption to bring social justice like the Sangkum era, not only that he also observed that even if not The CNRP has at least 70 to 80 percent the same political stance as Sihanoukism, which is why he decided to return to politics.
Prince Thomico also said that communism adhered to two principles: first, neutrality, independence, territorial integrity, and the recognition of Cambodia's borders by all diplomatic countries.
The second is to adhere to the policy of leading to social justice in accordance with the theory of Buddhist society, taking the Dharma of the Buddha as the national religion as the basis for strengthening morality and social justice and the lives of the people.
At this point, he stressed:
As for what I say about Sihanoukism, the key is to eradicate corruption, which neither FUNCINPEC nor the CPP can achieve because corruption now grows out of the top leadership. Come and go.
On the other hand, Samdech Oun Sihanouk said that if there is corruption, it should be avoided in the two public sectors, education and health.
But today, corruption occurs from the top to the bottom of all institutions.
He also stressed that parties other than the CNRP could not eradicate corruption as a dictatorial leadership disregarded the rule of law, ignored the weak people, and this became even more apparent recently as the country's military chiefs. Mr. Sokha said he was pleased with the Nazi leadership, which was a testament to that.
When asked about his personal life, Prince Sisowath Thomico recalled that he was born in Phnom Penh at the age of 63 this year.
He had a father named Sisowath, a lawyer, and a mother named An Mary Issy, the daughter-in-law of Samdech Menor Norodom Monineath Sihanouk.
He is the eldest of six children, four surviving, one younger brother in Cambodia, one younger sister in Canada and the other in France.
He was sent by his father to study in France when he was four years old and was raised by his mother from an early age.
Before the coup d'état overthrew the nation, a revolution erupted among French students, and he returned to his homeland because his father feared he would join the communist revolution.
After the coup, he served as a soldier in Lon Nol's Khmer Republic in General Ith Suong's brigade.
In 1973, he was imprisoned because Lon Nol ordered the capture of the entire royal family when Sophotra, the husband of Princess Norodom Botum Bopha, the younger brother of Prince Norodom Chakrapong, flew a plane to bomb the Lon Nol Presidential Palace. Stay.
He was released a month later and returned to France to continue his studies in economics and political science under Chulong Somura, a year after Sam Rainsy and finally took up another IT position. .
While living in France, he worked for an American company to open two branches in France before Thomico married his first wife, had five children, two of whom are now dead, and the other three live in France.
He recalled that when he returned to Cambodia in 2000, Jaya refused to follow and broke up.
While in Cambodia, he married Jaya, a 40-year-old wealthy businessman who now has two children, a 7-year-old boy and a 2-year-old girl, who are engaged in handicraft business in Siem Reap.
The Prince recounted the good and bad experiences of his life:
This is a great victory and pride of Cambodia as a whole and in my life.
As for the bitter lesson, I was one of the founders of FUNCINPEC, but in the end, internal divisions weakened the party and subsequently failed.
Prince Thomico also stated that the CPP is leading against the Sangkum Reastr Niyum. With that tourism, thousands of families there may have a better life, but now the government is willing to expel thousands of people and fulfill the desire to have only one tycoon.
This is the difference between Sihanoukism and today's leadership.
Prince Thomico still expressed his intention that all his last endeavors were not for his own honor.
Another important thing is that I want to thank Samdech Ov Samdech Mey for taking care of me since I was a child by continuing the activities that he intended when he was there, to serve the people.
Therefore, I will continue to fulfill this royal wish of Samdech Ov, to accomplish this service of the people when I can, in order to be highly grateful to Him.
The Khmer Rouge will be proof because of internal divisions before the Vietnamese can attack ....
In the cause of education system reform, Mr. Hang Chuon Naron gets 3 hours of sleep per night
The quality of education, which has long been criticized, is becoming a test for new education minister Hang Chuon Naron, who holds a doctorate in economics in Russia.
Being in the process of embarking on a strategy of deepening reforms to the crumbling education system, the economist has been working day and night with almost no rest. Can sleep only 3 hours.
Hang Chuon Naron was newly appointed Minister of Education in the fifth term of 2013, replacing Im Sethy, who was to be replaced.
Chuon Naron received his PhD in Russia in International Economic Relations and wrote a book on economics in Cambodia, which was the focus of his life.
He told the Cambodian Weekend newspaper last month:
I became a recognized economist and wrote economics books for the next generation ... I'm happy.
However, changing the habits of students in Cambodia from not taking the exam to do it based on their own ability is an issue that the Minister is concerned day and night.
However, he has already vowed to improve the education sector, which has been increasingly criticized for its poor quality.
In fact, the reform of the bankruptcy exam in 2014 was largely welcomed by the minister's new ideas.
Mr. Chuon Naron said:
When I go back to sleep, I get up and read educational documents to find new ideas.
The Minister, who has a simple background, worked as a translator at the CDRI Training and Research Institute for the Development of Cambodia until he became a civil servant at the Ministry of Economy and Finance, welcomed the reporters warmly and friendly. Most of his office is along Norodom Boulevard on Sunday, a public holiday.
Because his father was a dean of the University of Fine Arts, he was also interested in dance, especially Yike and Bassac.
On April 15 and 16, 1975, as the Khmer Rouge invaded Phnom Penh, his family left their home to live temporarily in the building of the School of Fine Arts, which his father was in charge of. That, hoping to have a chance to escape to another place.
On April 17, 1975, the Chaktomuk River turned black as Khmer Rouge troops invaded Phnom Penh, the last day most people thought the war would end.
But things did not go as planned and as the general public hoped, and Khmer Rouge soldiers ordered the people to leave Phnom Penh, leaving only three days.
Later we crossed to the closed river east of the Mekong River in Kampong Cham province.
We crossed National Road 7 and lived as farmers for a while in Chreung village, Veal Mlou commune, Tambor district, Kampong Cham province, Tbong Khmum province today.
In December 1978, Chuon Naron's family was evacuated by the Khmer Rouge from Tambor district to Kandol Chrum commune, amid the formation of the Kampuchean United Front for National Salvation to overthrow the Khmer Rouge regime.
At that time, my family was sent to Santuk district, Kampong Thom province.
At that time, the Khmer Rouge required people to report the truth about their family situation.
Guarding the Home for the Minister of Education In January 1979, after the Vietnamese army came to help overthrow the Khmer Rouge regime, Chuon Naron's family returned to live on the outskirts of Phnom Penh around km 7.
A few months later, Chan Ven, the then Minister of Education, arrived in the village where Chuon Naron's family lived to recruit staff.
I started working night shifts as a housekeeper for the Minister of Education with other young people at the age of 17.
We also cleaned up the school, first of all Chaktomuk Primary School, to enter the school year in September 1979.
At that time, in 1980, Chuon Naron's father was the director of the Ministry of Education, the Deputy Minister, and the Ministry of Education was reorganized.
Mr. Chuon Naron said:
A few months later, I helped organize Preah Sisowath High School, which has grades 8 to 10 as high school graduates.
At that time, Chuon Naron worked hard to learn Khmer, math, English and other subjects.
It was funny after I recalled an English lesson ... I also started an English class to teach some friends.
Going abroad to study when he failed the exam in 1981, Chuon Naron attended the Institute of Technology of Cambodia for a year.
In 1982, he received a scholarship to study in the Soviet Union to study international economic relations.
Chuon Naron went to study in Kiev, which is now in Ukraine, although he did not speak Russian, but he worked hard to learn Russian and help teach new Cambodian students who did not speak Russian very well.
Learning Russian at that time without a dictionary for translation made it even more difficult for this Cambodian student.
Already at night, translating from Russian to French, writing down new words, translating every night.
In his third year, Chuon Naron was sent back to study in Moscow, adding French and English, making him even more difficult in terms of language.
Chuon Naron eventually graduated with a master's degree in economics in 1988 and went on to earn a doctorate in 1991, writing a dissertation on Cambodia's foreign economic relations.
Becoming an expert in language and writing the Prime Minister's speech while studying in Russia, Chuon Naron took time off to work for the Cambodian Embassy there to earn a salary and refine his knowledge of Russian, French and English.
He acted as translator for the high-level Cambodian delegation, especially Foreign Minister Hor Namhong, both in writing and orally.
Upon his return to Cambodia, he immediately worked at the Australian Embassy, then at the CDRI Institute as an English translator, and returned to the Australian Embassy as a Political-Economic Research Officer in 1993. Until 1998.
On January 1, 1999, he joined the Ministry of Economy and Finance at a time when the government needed someone who could write and speak English fluently.
Speech writers are a group of members of the Supreme National Economic Council who are tasked with writing speeches in both Khmer and foreign languages for the Prime Minister.
All of the prime minister's speeches came from a team of the Supreme National Economic Council, with Naron in charge of reviewing the English writing, with no foreigners overseeing the grammar.
He also accompanied the Prime Minister as a translator of speeches at meetings abroad.
Love and family Naron married in 1990 to Pen Wimol, now has two children, a 23-year-old son, Hang Virith, and a 20-year-old daughter, Hang Vimla.
Pen Vimol, now an official with the Ministry of Environment Planning Department, was the son of Pen Navuth, the former Minister of Education and Education from 1984 to 1990, who died and Ros Man.
Chuon Naron says that because he is so busy with work, it is difficult for him to allocate time for his family.
However, he did not want to remind the family much, stating that his wife clearly understood his workload as Minister of Education.
When I have free time, I always read books and do not get much rest due to too much work, but my family is very understanding.
Without the understanding of the wife, it would be difficult.
The education reform strategy is more praiseworthy than criticizing the reform of the Baccalaureate, but for the minister, this is a preliminary result and not yet successful, as more time is needed to do so. This work is to be accomplished with the participation of relevant partners in the field of education, both nationally and internationally.
The minister is just a strategic priority.
He added that further review of school inspections was also a priority.
In addition, there is training for teachers focusing on the ninth and twelfth grades, reviewing science teaching to select experts, and especially the development of teacher policies that must be done. In this mandate, in order to avoid taking students' money during public school hours, make sure that teachers have the honor, pride and quality.
Earlier this year ... we approved the teacher action plan, which is a beacon to implement until 2018.
The minimum salary of teachers will be more than 1 million riel, but we also need to increase the qualifications of teachers.
Yang Kim Eng, a student at the Royal School of Administration, but did not want to work in the government
Despite graduating from the first generation of the Royal School of Administration in 1998, the young man from the remote countryside of Angkor did not seek employment in any state institution due to low-paying government jobs and On the other hand, do not want to commit corruption.
Yang Kim Eng, 44, also graduated in Khmer Literature in 1995 and has spent most of his time working for NGOs, mostly on youth issues.
Yang Kim Eng was born in 1971 in Svay Sar village, Svay Sar commune, Varin district, Siem Reap province, far from the provincial town, which has no roads.
He has eight siblings and is the second child in the family.
Under the Khmer Rouge regime, Kim Eng was nine years old and was assigned by the Khmer Rouge as a mobile child.
After 1979, Kim Eng did not attend school because his hometown was often the scene of battles between the Khmer Rouge and Vietnamese troops.
Kim Eng told the Post over the weekend that as a child he had only fled the fighting between the Khmer Rouge and the Vietnamese state-backed army.
At the time, Kim Eng's cousins were in the second grade, and Kim Eng was enrolled in the second grade with his son.
Since then, I have known the value of learning, despite the insecurity, I farmed and studied and escaped the war.
Kim Eng's mother was shot dead in 1985 as Khmer Rouge and Vietnamese troops shelled each other.
Even though his mother died, Kim Eng continued to study until he finished high school and applied for a teacher exam at the age of 17.
At that time, in my district, each teacher always carried a gun, even while teaching students.
It was very quiet at that time.
Due to his underage age, Kim Eng did not take the exam to become a teacher, but he continued his studies until he passed the exam in 1991 and entered the university in Phnom Penh to study Khmer literature.
At that time, I asked the school to stay and I slept near the school stairs.
Sometimes I slept alone in the university until 1995 when I finished my exams.
After graduating in literature, Mr. Kim Eng went to apply for the Royal School of Administration after realizing that upon graduation at the Royal School of Administration, he could find a job as a district governor, provincial governor or other job at In government ministries.
Despite his poverty, Kim Eng changed his mind, not seeking employment for the state, because he thought that working for the state would only lead to corruption, if he hoped for a salary, he would not be able to survive.
In 2005, Kim Eng stopped working for the Khmer Youth Association because he had vowed to leave the association whenever he had the money.
As the leader of the Youth Association, Kim Eng has three dreams: first, to have a national policy for youth after he attended the Youth Conference in Austria in 1996, organized by the United Nations and supported by UNDP. .
He has an idea to set up a youth council to organize advocacy until 2011 to issue a youth policy.
Second, he thinks that the youth organizations are separate, so he wants all the youth organizations to work together to form the Cambodian Youth Council.
But Kim Eng always tries to persuade donors to pay attention to youth issues until donors give money.
After donors provided assistance, Kim Eng resigned as chairman of the Youth Council in 2004.
Third is the desire to have an international youth exchange program.
During 2004, Kim Eng began to take action against corruption, realizing that poor people often suffered because of corruption.
He added that all advocacy was difficult, with few people daring to come forward due to threats from the authorities, but he thought that if young people did not act, the space for democracy would shrink.
Despite knowing that advocacy can be dangerous, Kim Eng did not remain silent because he worked for society as a whole.
I see that the government has not yet provided the right solution to provide services to citizens, such as identification, birth certificates, if they do not give money, they do not process the documents that people have to be forced to bribe to get documents for use. To be legal.
Through the advocacy of civil society organizations, Kim Eng hopes that one day the government will be able to provide full public services to the people.
I want to help society, I do not want to be an official, I want to have a great merit.
My friend, who is from the Royal School of Administration, at least is now the district chief.
Yang Kim Eng is currently the Executive Director of the People's Center for Development and Peace, which was established in 2005 as a civil society organization advocating for human rights, democracy, youth issues and anti-corruption.
Kim Eng does not intend to give up his NGO work until he sees a prosperous and harmonious Cambodian society, democracy and full respect for human rights.
In 1998, Kim Eng worked for the Ministry of Social Affairs for about two weeks before resigning, thinking he could not survive on a low salary.
Wives walk from hospital to hospital in shock after four bomb blasts in a rally led by opposition leaders on March 30, 1997. Deaths and injuries piled up in pools of blood were one of the most memorable moments in Eng Chai Eang's life.
Entering politics from a young age until becoming the chairman of the first committee of the National Assembly, it is not a coincidence for this outstanding student of mathematics across the country, but it is a plan that has been thought out since So far, so good.
Eng Chhay Eang was born on November 10, 1965, to a well-to-do business family in Kampong Chen village, Kampong Chen Tbong commune, Stong district, Kampong Thom province.
He had nine siblings, but now only seven remain, one during the Khmer Rouge regime and the other in the early 1980s.
Chhay Eang, the third child in the family, left home in Kampong Chen village in 1980 to study in the provincial town of Kampong Thom.
Mr. Chhay Eang passed the third grade exam before the Cambodian state in 1987.
He said that when he was in school, he excelled in all subjects, but mathematics was the best.
Mr. Chhay Eang has been attending the Red School of Health Sciences since 1987 and graduated in 1993.
Upon graduation, Chai Eang did not take the medical knowledge he had been studying for about four years to work or engage in any business, but he did participate in political activities with FUNCIN. Pen.
He joined the FUNCINPEC Youth Movement in mid-1992 because he was very fond of the party's policies.
During that time, both politicians and opposition supporters were in grave danger of being shot dead.
Mr. Chhay Eang went on to tell Cambodia over the weekend in his office at the National Assembly on Wednesday that he first met Mr. Rainsy in December 1992, when he was a senior official. High FUNCINPEC Party.
When FUNCINPEC was elected in 1993, Mr. Sam Rainsy hired me to work with him at the Ministry of Economy.
Working in the coalition government at the time, Eng Chai Eang was Rainsy's personal secretary, who was then Minister of Economy and Finance.
At that time, Mr. Rainsy also told Mr. Chhay Eang that he would not return to France, he would continue to do politics to help the country and liberate the country from the dictatorship, make the country social justice and there will be Development.
At the time, Mr. Eang told Mr. Rainsy that he would continue to work with him forever.
The coalition government, with Prince Norodom Ranariddh as the first prime minister and Hun Sen as the second, finally ousted Rainsy from the economy on October 20, 1994.
At that time, Mr. Chhay Eang and a group of young people left the Ministry of Economy to work for Mr. Rainsy until Mr. Rainsy officially announced the formation of the Khmer National Party on November 9, 1995.
When the Khmer National Party was formed, Chai Eang was the director of Sam Rainsy's cabinet and a member of the board of directors.
Later, with a dispute over the name of the Khmer National Party, Rainsy formed another party under his name.
In the 1998 election, Chhay Eang resigned as director of Sam Rainsy's cabinet, and in late August 1999 the party's board of directors voted him secretary general until he resigned.
Mr. Chhay Eang entered politics until he became a Member of Parliament, and in the National Assembly of the fifth mandate, he is the Chairman of the First Committee of the National Assembly, the Commission on Human Rights, Investigation, Complaints, and National Assembly and Senate Relations. It is not a coincidence.
It is no coincidence that he has marked his path to politics since he was a student.
The bean-colored man underlined:
And then, after going through the Khmer Rouge communist regime until Vietnam came to Cambodia, I had to do research, what did it come from?
The continued politics with Sam Rainsy from the departure of FUNCINPEC to the formation of the Khmer National Party to the Sam Rainsy Party and finally merged with the Human Rights Party into the current CNRP is due to Mr. Chhay Eang sees Mr. Rainsy as a clean politician.
And at that time, knowing that our salary was not enough, he took his National Assembly salary and gave it to us to share to make a living ....
Eng Chhay Eang and his team followed Sam Rainsy's instructions and worked for the Ministry of Economy, successfully earning a reputation for both the minister and the minister's associates. There was no scandal.
Because during Chai Eang's political career, fresh politicians were shot, and he was well aware of the challenges he would face one day. Tell your family in advance that if you are shot for political reasons, do not blame anyone, because this is your personal decision.
At that time, his family fully supported him to enter politics.
Mr. Chhay Eang also described that his politics is very challenging from external opponents who always consider us as an opposition party as an enemy, so there are bitter events and Internal competition as well.
Mr. Chhay Eang went through many bitter stories, but what he will never forget is the tragic event of four bombs being thrown into a crowd of protesters led by Sam Rainsy on March 30, 1997. More than 10 people were killed in the pool of blood and nearly 200 were seriously injured.
He went to Calmette Hospital, but I could not find him because he was being transported to the hospital.
Inside the party, there have been many challenges since Eng Chai Eang joined the Sam Rainsy Party.
He said that there were more fabrications and accusations against him than allegations that he was a spy for Prime Minister Hun Sen, which was hidden next to Sam Rainsy.
During his tenure as Senior Minister and Minister of Economy and Finance, no official or businessman could have met directly with Rainsy, and officials and traders had to go through all secretaries except the state. Same officials.
These are the special points of the leader that Mr. Chhay Eang described as clean and different from others, if others are clearly embraced for their own benefit ....
Mr. Rainsy refused to accept bribes when he was bribed, and he used all his time as a minister to serve the interests of the nation and always forbade subordinates to travel or receive them. When he goes out or comes back, he can only pick up the car and pick it up so as not to waste time.
Mr. Rainsy also does not like to meet anyone who asks to meet him and tell other people's stories, but he likes to meet everyone if the national issues to discuss with him and do not take the burden to help talk. Help find a solution.
Mr. Chhay Eang continued that he had nothing to charm Mr. Rainsy to make Mr. Rainsy like what some people say, but Mr. Rainsy is a clear person and he is also a clear person to continue working. Together so far.
Although Chhay Eang has been involved in politics for the opposition for more than 20 years, he emphasized:
Eng Chhay Eang was married in 1990 to his wife in Siem Reap and now has four children, two sons, a 22-year-old eldest and a 12-year-old youngest.
Chea Leang, the first female prosecutor to be appointed by the King
Many graduates continue to live in the developed West to escape the flames of war in Cambodia in the past, but it was not Chea Leang's choice, even though she had the opportunity. Using her legal knowledge, she graduated from Germany in 1995 and helped build the country, starting as a regular employee and becoming the first female attorney general in the Supreme Court.
Chea Leang was born on September 9, 1969, now 45 years old, and is known to most ordinary people for her role as Co-Prosecutor of the Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia. During the Khmer Rouge regime to bring to justice.
After graduating from Toul Tompong High School in 1988 and becoming one of the best students in the field of morality, she was awarded a government scholarship to study law in Germany and spent seven years earning a bachelor's degree. High Master from Martin Luther University, 1995.
Many well-known Cambodian friends continue to live on their own land, escaping instability in Cambodia, which did not have full peace in 1995.
But Chea Leang recalls the period of 3 years, 8 months and 20 days when the Khmer Rouge regime massacred many intellectuals.
So after that period, we can only build a nation together.
But I came back to Cambodia because my heart was not broken.
Upon her return to the country, she joined the Ministry of Justice as a civil servant, then was appointed Prosecutor at the Court of Appeal in 2002.
Until 2006, the Khmer Rouge Tribunal was established by the King to be the National Co-Prosecutor.
In 2009, he was appointed as the Attorney General of the Supreme Court.
In the title of Lok Chumteav, a high-ranking official bestowed by the King, Chea Leang lived a simple life.
She married Dr. Sokhom Rithivuth, currently Deputy Secretary General of the Board of Engineers and Deputy Director of the Audit Department of the Ministry of Health in 1993 in Cambodia.
Although life is not poor, this family knows the value of the struggle, the struggle to live alone, from the study of the couple to the birth of twin girls in Germany.
Back in Cambodia, the couple worked hard and raised their children on their own until they were taken to work with.
She recalled:
She has linked her life as a high-ranking official and her husband as a government official to the lives of ordinary people, without many left-to-right servants like some of the big ones, because she used to study abroad. Live an independent life.
Even with family, we have to understand each other, if we have the same attitude, we can not be together.
So far, she reminisces about the Khmer Rouge era, when millions of Cambodians, including her family, had to go through a bitter ordeal, building dams, as children, until their families dared to steal poisonous cassava. Die almost to support the hungry stomach, face the risk of being killed.
She described:
Thus, they are investigating the Khmer Rouge regime searching for ex-soldiers to kill.
Thus, life is very risky.
Studying national and international law, reading many books on Khmer Rouge history, and working as a prosecutor, dared to accuse the Khmer Rouge leader Kaing Guek Eav alias Duch, former Tuol Sleng prison chief Khieu Samphan and Nuon Chea. Imprisonment for life is both applause and criticism of the entire Khmer Rouge tribunal, but she remains optimistic about her work on the national side, with a steady stream of international coordination.
Speaking from the Office of the Co-Prosecutor of the Khmer Rouge Tribunal on National Road 4 in Phnom Penh, she said:
The Khmer Rouge tribunal has often been accused of being under pressure from the government, making it difficult for them to extend the case to a number of officials who are thought to be involved.
Ms. Chea Leang denied this.
She cites the case of crimes in Yugoslavia, where the law allows open access to lower levels, but the law in Cambodia does not exist.
Prior to the law, the United Nations had already agreed with the government to pass the law, the Senate and the Constitutional Council.
The international community said that it was exaggerated, but I said no, I follow the civil system.
That is an investigation.
She also felt that Cambodians today were less interested in the Khmer Rouge tribunal, but should also understand that this is a historical story for the younger generation.
The reason for the dismissal was because the Chamber split the case, so the facts that happened during that regime have not been decided yet.
The trial is to show the facts that happened in that regime, show the national and international public how cruel the regime is?
The National Co-Prosecutor does not seem to have any intention of leaving the legal profession in the future to help her husband, who has a part-time job as a dentist.
She still wants to pass on the experience gained from the Khmer Rouge tribunal to the younger generation, including law students, judges, prosecutors who need experience in this area, such as participating as speakers at universities, regardless of their needs. From the budget.
Death will leave the honor and knowledge of the next generation destroyed by the Pol Pot regime.
Today, Chea Leang and her husband have three children, two of whom were born in Germany, Sokhom Sothyta and 19-year-old Sokhom Socheata.
They both study at the University of Phnom Penh.
The youngest son, 14-year-old Sokhom Rithirut, is pursuing a high school education in New Zealand.
Kem Kunavath's successful life starts from pure struggle
Phnom Penh was liberated on January 7, 1979, but Kem Kunavath's family escaped the clutches of the Khmer Rouge assassins in Pursat province in May.
Arriving in Phnom Penh for the first time, Kem Kunavath sought accommodation and applied for a job and joined National Radio on June 22, 1979. Today.
In more than 30 years of state service in the field of state media, he went from being a low-level employee to the current director general of TVK.
Mr. Kem Kunavath took a moment to recall his memories with Cambodia over the weekend that he first joined the National Radio of Cambodia because he was young, he was assigned to plant mulberries at Stung Meanchey station for the generation of Mr. Chan Navuth Chea. Leadership.
At that time, Mr. Un Dara, the principal, called me to ask how many grades I had studied. The first in Cambodia at Wat Sampov Meas, with Mr. Un Dara as CEO, Mr. Veng Sambo, General Manager and I, Deputy Director in charge of Training and Livelihood, and the first session had 40 staff and the last session had 120 staff members for a year and a half. .
It was not until 1983 that the state planned to establish a state television station, which planned to cut off broadcasting from Vietnam for one day and three hours, and to broadcast live in Phnom Penh.
He said that at first, the National Television had a total of 48 staff members, including Mr. Lok Chumteav Chanthy, Mr. Sam Sarin, Mr. Tep Sakhon ... and had four skills: news team, cameraman, production team, technical team and readers. To study for 45 days in Can Tho, Vietnam.
The first national television station was launched on December 3, 1983, broadcasting four hours a day, with a capacity of 1 kilowatt of PAL, color television, and a total staff of more than 60, including staff from previous broadcasters. And located around Silip Market, next to the State Council warehouse.
He said that for his role at that time was the camera man.
He said that during that time, it was a tense period in the battle on the Thai border and at A-5, where there was recruitment and volunteering of young people, but for him, his superiors refused to serve in the army, but also Struggling in difficult work in battle, such as in the Pailin area, Samlot, Swar Slor, Tiger, Roar ....
During his life as a television photographer, he met and worked for Hun Sen in 1984.
During the peace talks, he was still very knowledgeable in the field of journalism, but at that time he was instructed by His Excellency Dith Monty, His Excellency Sok An, and His Excellency Sin Song to die. To this day.
Kem Kunavath in the public eye He is an attractive commentator and is well known in the country, especially in commenting on major national events such as the Royal Water Festival. .
He said of the experience in this field that the commentator must first know the environment around them, look into the eyes of those who look at us and be simple.
What he has achieved also depends on a passion because he has been interested in literature and reading as a trinity since he was a student.
He said that in the past, he had also been criticized for commenting on his honor, position and some personal life, but not much.
Through more than 30 years of work experience from information staff to manager, he has a lot of experience in directing staff management, program production, broadcast management.
He also said that during the intense competition between political parties, of course, there was some criticism on national television of bias or impartiality.
But in fact, state television must implement government policy goals.
In a lifelong devotion to the state, Kem Kunavath said his last wish after retirement was to have the state have a digital television, as in other countries in the region. There is also support from the state because it uses a very large budget.
In addition, he wants the state to have a standard journalism faculty with training in electronic media skills.
He said Cambodia should also have a national news organization that oversees journalists in the same way as the labor court.
As for his ambition in his political career, it may be a gift from an angel, and he did not dare to say that first, but other than that, he will spend the rest of his time writing a book about the realities of the transition in Cambodia.
Kem Kunavath has a son who died of dengue fever after 1979. Today, he has two children and a family.
One son works for the Supreme Court and one daughter is studying law and is working in the same law but in the private sector.
What he wants from them is that he wants his children to be good citizens in society and to be responsible.
Kem Kunavath was born in October 1957 in Phnom Penh during the Boeung Keng Kang period, an undeveloped lake.
His father, a farmer, lived in Kak village, Svay Rumpea commune, Svay Teap district, Romduol district, before Svay Rieng province and passed the exam as a teacher officer at that time.
The mother, the eldest daughter of the Sisowath lineage, was able to relocate to the Boeung Keng Kang area, where officials and the royal family lived, serving as primary school teachers.
Depending on him being the same teacher, the two of them shook hands as a family until they had nine children.
In the pre-1970s, his father transferred from teaching to work at the Ministry of Interior and did a lot of administrative work at the newly established Kratie and Mondulkiri provincial halls.
He said his father had joined the creation of the new province with Khieu Bun, the uncle of Minister Khieu Kanharith, in 1966.
Between 1968 and 1969, he was elected to the National Assembly in Mondulkiri and came to work in Phnom Penh.
In 1970, his father left the National Assembly to become the governor of Kampot province's Kampong Trach district during the time of General Khlot But.
In 1973, while entering the flames of war, his father moved to Phnom Penh to work as an administrative officer at the city hall.
During the Pol Pot regime, when he was evacuated to his father's hometown, he was detained at Wat Chantry, Chantry commune, Romeas Hek district.
He and his mother and 10 siblings live in misery, no different from other Cambodian families who are considered subordinates.
Then, his family was sent to Pursat province to suffer and die.
He and his family were able to escape the clutches of the black-clad monster by escaping to the line of the Liberation Army and Vietnam in early May 1979.
We must be prepared to be simple, smart and always try.
Due to political ambitions, Mao Monivann gave up his career as a doctor
His passion for political work and his ambition to lead the country to true democracy and the rule of law have prompted Mao Monivann to relinquish his career as a surgeon in military medicine. In 1997, he turned to a passionate political career until today, when he became a member of parliament for the CNRP.
Mr. Mao Monivann described that after failing the exam, he entered the medical school from 1986 to 1992, he graduated and was sent to work in Kampong Cham province in the military medical unit in the region or region.
He worked as a doctor at a military hospital in Phnom Penh from 1993 to 1997, specializing in abdominal and chest surgery.
During his studies at the Faculty of Medicine, he recalled:
Mao Monivann told Cambodia over the weekend that in the 1990s, he led a group of medical students to protest against the then-ruling Cambodian state party, while officials conspired to sell national property.
The confrontation between medical students and the armed forces at the time, Mr. Monyvann claimed, had left many students dead and missing, with three missing and 20 missing.
Mr. Mao Monivann said:
He said that after the Paris Agreement for the parties to the Cambodian political conflict to go to the polls in 1993, organized by the United Nations, seeing a good opportunity, he pushed the protest movement to become. Political action to promote electoral change.
But seeing the misfortune of FUNCINPEC overthrowing these two people, it shows that the leadership of Norodom Ranariddh did not adhere to its policies, and I stopped supporting it from then on.
Mr. Monyvann said that he had almost lost the opportunity to participate in political activities, and that Mr. Rainsy had formed a new political party to compete for power in the 1998 re-election.
He also returned to politics with Sam Rainsy during 1996.
But you know, suddenly Mr. Sam Rainsy was able to return home, I started active political activities as the party president in Kampong Cham until the day I left in 2011 and joined the Human Rights Party with Mr. Kem Sokha. .
Mao Monivann stated that the reason for leaving the SRP in 2011 was because he saw that within the SRP, influential people in the party did not want to be united. Cheat no.
Mao Monivann is currently a member of the CNRP's Kampong Cham constituency and vice chairman of the National Assembly's Ninth Committee in charge of public transport, telecommunications, postal industry, mining, energy, trade, land management, urban planning and construction.
He was born in the hospital at km 46, which is his home.
He was born in 1964 as the eldest child in a family of six siblings, three sisters.
When the nation reached school age in the civil war in 1970, his family fled to the provincial town of Kampong Cham. He attended Dey Douh Primary School until the ninth grade, when the Khmer Rouge invaded the city. Kampong Cham and also moved people to live in the area of liberation, his family went to live in his hometown in Suong from 1973 until 1975, when the Khmer Rouge attacked Phnom Penh.
He said that during the Khmer Rouge regime, he was only about 13 years old and only a herdsman.
His family continued to live in the ministry until Vietnam liberated the country from the Khmer Rouge in 1979.
He recalled from the post-liberation period on January 7, 1979 that in 1981, Suong Secondary School was reopened, and he continued to study until the end of the second grade until 1983, when he moved to Phnom Penh to study. Daun Penh High School, now called Preah Sisowath High School, passed the exam and continued to attend medical school.
For the future, he thinks he will do his best in his political career to build the nation until the people are happy.
Ms. Mam Sitha spends most of her life serving a democratic society
In a modern café opposite Phnom Penh University, 50-year-old Mam Sitha described to Cambodia over the weekend how she had spent most of her life in civil society or NGO work. It is not the government that has sacrificed everything for the common good of society, democracy, human rights and the rule of law.
Mam Sitha, director of the Cambodian Independent Committee against Corruption and former chair of the human rights organization Transparency and Peace, described working with Dr. Lao Mong Hay, then executive director of the Khmer Institute for Doctrine. Democracy.
She has been promoting democracy, human rights, law and citizenship to many people in some districts in Kampong Cham since early 1995.
We have been communists for a long time, and when democracy came to Cambodia, it changed my heart, I loved and enjoyed democracy and human rights.
I work in Kampong Cham province, which is active in promoting democracy, the law and the people.
She said that during 1996 he finished his work in the province and also came to work in Phnom Penh as a missionary.
He volunteered to do this work because he saw the enormity of impunity in Cambodian society.
Later in 2000, he established the Cambodian Independent Committee Against Corruption, which is mainly due to the fact that in Cambodian society, the poor often suffer from corruption, such as land issues. .
Ms. Mam Sitha also stated that the expression of concern about corruption came after the Anti-Corruption Unit presented its position on preventing or eliminating corruption in society and the mechanism of Prevention and control do not seem to be responding to the spread of corruption, both in law and practice.
Ms. Sitha says that each political party has set out its anti-corruption policy for the people or supporters of that political party.
But on closer inspection, corruption has not diminished as promised, instead it is spreading from the grassroots to the national level.
However, she has stated that she will not give up her civil society work if she still has the opportunity to do so.
Mam Sitha, born in 1963, is the son of a farmer in Kampong Thmor village, Santuk district, Kampong Thom province. She is the fourth child in the family.
She said she left the area for Phnom Penh in 1984 during the mobilization of soldiers fighting the tripartite forces and the mobilization of civilians to site A-5 along the Thai border.
He said that he came to Phnom Penh to escape coercion and wanted to continue his education.
Ms. Mam Sitha also confirmed that she changed her date of birth to 1969, which was not a scam, but a social need of the day.
He said that he attended the class until 1992, graduated with a Bachelor of Laws in Administrative Law in 1992, he came out as a professor of lower education.
He taught for less than a year, then the Ministry of Education withdrew from the higher education system and had to train at the University of Vedic University in Prey Veng, working there for two years, 1994-95.
She stated that because the teacher's salary was too low, she left the teaching profession and left without regret and started a new career.
I have trained thousands of students.
Kong Bora divorces his wife due to different political tendencies
At the age of 40, the next generation of opposition politicians, Kong Bora, broke up their marriage due to conflicting political tendencies between him and his father-in-law, a senior official in the ruling party.
Bora married the son of a senior CPP official during 1994 and divorced in 1997 due to political differences.
He stated that living in harmony, I decided to divorce in 1997, until now I have not remarried.
For my father-in-law, he is not a political discriminator, but in Cambodian society, because of the tendencies of political parties, it is disturbing, and I realized that I should not make it difficult for my father-in-law because of me, he decided Divorced with his wife.
The divorce came because Kong Bora did not want to tarnish his father-in-law's position.
So far, Bora is still responsible for raising one of his daughters.
Bora was born on September 20, 1974 in Kork Lveang village, Ponhea Krek district, Kampong Cham province.
His father, Kong Koam, is currently a senator, and his mother, Seak Oy.
During the Khmer Rouge regime, Bora did not have a bitter history, as the Khmer Rouge allowed him to herd cattle.
After the fall of the Khmer Rouge, Bora's family left their homeland to live in Phnom Penh in 1980.
He attended Chaktomuk Primary School.
However, since his father was appointed ambassador to Hanoi, Vietnam in 1981, Bora followed his father to live there until 1982, when he returned to Cambodia. And continued his studies at Sothearos School.
He took the junior high school exam in 1990.
He then entered Chamkar Dong University until 1995, graduating with a degree in production and veterinary medicine before joining the civil service.
In 1996, Bora, a civil servant at the Ministry of Agriculture, became the administrative manager of the Department of Production and Veterinary Medicine.
In 1993, Bora went to the polls organized by the United Nations, where he voted for FUNCINPEC.
After the election, he began to monitor the country's political events and came to understand the purity of the work of Sam Rainsy, then Minister of Economy and Finance and Prince Norodom. Serey Vuth, former Minister of Foreign Affairs, quota FUNCINPEC.
Political outrage, mixed with unjust pain, erupted against Bora as the two top ministers were removed.
It was then that he lost faith in FUNCINPEC and waited for an opportunity to enter politics.
In 1995, the Khmer National Party was formed, led by Sam Rainsy.
I just help find support, just follow my father in Prey Veng.
Until 1998, I ran for parliament in Prey Veng province and was elected in this constituency until now.
Bora later became a network leader and mobilizer of pro-opposition activists in Phnom Penh and took over in Prey Veng.
He says any quest for support has been so intimidated by ruling party activists that he has been threatened with death by grassroots surgery and ridicule.
From 1997 to 1998, during and after the coup d'état, forces loyal to Prince Norodom Ranariddh and forces loyal to Hun Sen fought bloody battles in the center of Phnom Penh. The most dangerous time for the opposition.
I have never used money to buy incentives and votes.
Bora says that as an opposition lawmaker, he is most concerned about the influx of Vietnamese illegal immigrants and has always thought that this was Vietnam's political plan to get Cambodian territory as much as they wanted water. The land of Kampuchea Krom is the same.
He noted that at present in Chbar Ampov Neak Loeung, some villages are more Vietnamese than Khmer.
We do not hate the Vietnamese, but we hate the Vietnamese government, which has the ambition to take over Cambodian territory, we are not the only generation I have heard from birth.
As a Cambodian, Kong Bora does not want to see Cambodia sink in with the growing number of Vietnamese immigrants and all forms of corruption, as he is now. The good deeds of my leader, the opposition party will be able to win the election to lead the country soon.
Pol Horm: Even if the opposition loses, it still wins
A staunch veteran politician who has always enjoyed doing politics on behalf of the opposition, Pol Horm has taken a stand on how to get Vietnam out of Cambodia, and even if the opposition loses, he still thinks Win because the opposition party has participated in educating the people to understand the rights and freedoms and democracy.
After the second national election in 1998, Pol Horm, originally from Son San's Buddhist Liberal Democratic Party, lost his seat in the Kampong Thom provincial constituency.
He then spent about 10 years working from place to place in civil society and the United Nations to earn a living before returning to politics in 2003.
When the Constituent Assembly became the National Assembly, I also became a member of the National Assembly from 1993 to 1998.
At that time, Son San's National Front for the Liberation of the Khmer People became the Buddhist Liberal Democratic Party.
In the 1998 election, the party did not win a seat, so I was no longer a member of parliament.
The party merged with FUNCINPEC and I became the chairman of the Oddar Meanchey provincial working group.
In 1998, Pol Horm resigned from FUNCINPEC to work as a United Nations short-term contract worker in East Timor.
He was later moved to Kosovo.
After that, Mr. Horm changed his job, working with NGOs, the Cambodian Institute of Human Rights and the Cambodian Center for Human Rights in the media and advocacy and politics with civil society organizations.
He later returned to work for the United Nations, working in the United States and Africa, and finally retired in late 2008.
When the Human Rights Party was formed, chaired by Kem Sokha, it had similar policies to that of the former Buddhist Liberal Democratic Party, led by Son San in March 2010. Decided to join the party.
I organized the party's work in Takeo province, and I was appointed to join the CNRP with the Sam Rainsy Party, with the role of interim vice president until the congress re-elected Kem Sokha as vice president.
In the July 2013 national election, the CNRP won 55 seats and Pol Horm won a seat in the Takeo provincial constituency.
Horm is also the chairman of the party's board of directors, the third most powerful person in the party.
He said that after a July 22 political agreement between the CNRP and the CPP to end the political crisis, he became a member of the National Assembly and chairman of the National Assembly's Third Committee on Agricultural Investment Planning. Rural development, environment and water resources.
He has nine siblings, five sisters and is the fourth child.
Pol Horm attended the Krasang School in Kampong Thom because he had to travel with his father to work.
He graduated from high school in Kampong Thom and in 1966 he passed the exam as a student teacher and came to study in Phnom Penh.
He studied in Phnom Penh for a year before returning to teach in Kampong Thom.
It was not until 1971 that Horm came to Phnom Penh again to continue his studies.
When the Khmer Rouge came in, I pretended to be a worker.
When the Khmer Rouge took over Phnom Penh in 1975, Pol Horm, like many other Cambodians, was evacuated from Phnom Penh by the Khmer Rouge to Koh Thom district in Kandal province.
During the Khmer Rouge regime, he was forced to carry seedlings and grow brown.
Mr. Horm lived in Pursat province until 1979, when the Vietnamese came in and I was selected to be the commune chief.
At that time, during the regime of the People's Republic of Cambodia, many people's cattle were killed by gunfire between the Khmer Rouge and the Vietnamese army.
Horm, the commune chief, ordered the villagers to dissect the carcasses of the dead cattle. The Vietnamese army arrested the villagers and he protested.
In the same year, 1979, Pol Horm left his homeland for the Khmer-Thai border area and joined the National Liberation Front with Ta Son San.
I also helped in the health sector because there were not many educated people at that time.
Later, Horm set up a radio station, the Khmer People's Liberation Front Radio, which was broadcast in villages in camps along the Thai border.
I hold the radio to broadcast news to Cambodia.
Horm attended the meeting to find a solution to end the flames of war until the Paris Agreement of October 23, 1991.
Horm returned to Cambodia in 1991 after the Cambodian side signed a peace agreement in Paris.
In addition to his media skills, Pol Horm also graduated with a degree in Philosophy and Law in 1973, during which time he was able to enter the Royal School of Law as a lawyer.
Horm, who also studied political strategy in Germany, also took time to teach good governance, the rule of law and free and fair elections, as well as advocacy skills.
I like to do politics from a young age because when I was teaching and studying, I encountered many social problems that I did not like, even in the time of Sihanouk.
Mr. Horm said his participation in the opposition was due to the fact that the ruling party was backed by Vietnam at the time.
He explained that if the opposition wins, we can lead the country in accordance with the principles and ideals of the opposition, but if we lose, I still think the opposition will still win because we have participated in educating people to understand their rights. Freedom and democracy.
Because his father was just a small, low-paid police officer, living in Phnom Penh before 1970 was very difficult, as he had to ride a cyclo to study and work as a teacher.
However, Horm is the only child of nine siblings who are lucky enough to be the most educated.
Mr. Horm was married on February 3, 1965, and so far he has eight children and 15 grandchildren.
