Using 10 fingers instead of money to salute people in every village in Prey Veng province, Chea Poch was finally elected as the youngest member of the National Assembly. 123 people.
Becoming the youngest opposition lawmaker in the third term of the National Assembly in 2003 was what Chea Poch described as ending an culture of discrimination against young politicians like him.
The reason why Chea Poch decided to suspend his job as a tax official and enter the political arena with the opposition party facing many dangers is because he thinks that politics can help society more than work Humanitarian.
Chea Poch, 29, who was elected to parliament in 2003, recently spoke to Cambodia over the weekend:
I struggle in the work of the Voice of the Khmer Youth and civil servants as opposition journalists.
This is a cornerstone of Chea Poch's life story, in which he bravely dared to join the struggle of the opposition against the ruling party to find what he saw as justice and a life of equal dignity. Each other in society.
Chea Poch was born in Kien Khleang village, Chroy Changva commune, Phnom Penh, and attended Kien Khleang primary school and then went on to study at Chroy Changva secondary school, Russey Keo high school and Norton college.
In 2000, he graduated from the College of Local Economic Development and has never studied abroad.
Seeing that FUNCINPEC, led by Prince Norodom Ranariddh could not help the country, Chea Poch decided to pursue a career as an opposition journalist to write about the coalition government for corruption. The rot they committed.
Until Sam Rainsy opened the Khmer National Party, I became the founder and activist of the party since 1995.
He decided to get involved in politics because he thought that it would help the society and the people a lot and make the country prosperous.
In 1998, Chea Poch ran for the Sam Rainsy Party in Prey Veng, but was not elected.
He says that because the party has good policies and President Sam Rainsy is clean, which is the key to the political strategy to seek the support of the people, the party is starting to gain more momentum.
At the time, Poch was chairman of the Prey Veng provincial Sam Rainsy Party council.
He faced many obstacles because he was young and had to manage a lot of people, starting to structure from scratch, to organize from the provincial level to the village level.
I have to use psychology to explain to them.
Because of that achievement, Mr. Chea Poch is valued without discrimination, whether old or young.
He says the challenge of his political rivalry was that he was stripped of his parliamentary immunity on February 3, 2005 for using a public forum to attack Prince Norodom Ranariddh for corruption. Luy and bribery, including the purchase of helicopters.
He was later transferred to Kampot for political activities in Kampot province.
In the July 28, 2013 election, Poch won the Kampot Provincial Assembly.
When all 55 elected CNRP lawmakers entered the National Assembly after a year-long boycott, Chea Poch served as a member of the National Assembly's First Committee on Complaints and Human Rights. Relations between the National Assembly and the Senate.
Chea Poch says lawmakers from both parties, the National Rescue Party and the people, have agreed in the National Assembly to stop talking about the party, only about the first commission. What should be done?
He says at the moment, many people from the provinces have filed complaints with the National Assembly's first committee, most of which are related to land disputes.
His father was illiterate, but he struggled to send his children to school.
Poch was married in 1999 and has three daughters so far, studying in Phnom Penh.
Chea Poch says he does not determine the future of his three daughters, leaving it up to them to decide their own course in life.
Regarding his involvement in politics with the opposition, which has always been a major challenge to the ruling party, Chea Poch emphasized:
This is a source of pride for me.
Chea Poch was also one of tens of thousands of people who escaped death after four bombs were thrown at a crowd led by Sam Rainsy in front of the National Assembly on July 30. In 1997, 20 people were killed and nearly 200 were seriously injured.
Mr. Nhim Vanda enjoys humanitarian work to help the poor
Nhim Vanda has struggled and endured a miserable life as a child since 1950 in a pagoda in Prey Veng province's Kamchay Mear district, where there was only one monk and only one pagoda boy.
But human destiny is invisible.
Today, this rural pagoda boy has become a veteran politician and has received many national building thumbs and medals.
Currently, Vanda is a four-star general and vice chairman of the National Committee for Disaster Management.
In a private interview in his office with Cambodia Weekend, an entertainment and educational newspaper affiliated with the Post, Nhim Vanda said:
My mother died when I was six years old, with my father working as a rubber plantation worker in Kampong Cham province in 1958, when it was occupied by the French.
Mr. Vanda studied hard at Wat Sangke for six years there until 1963, when he passed the entrance exam to Kamchay Mear Secondary School in Kamchay Mear district.
At about 16 years old, Nhim Vanda had friends like Sar Kheng and Yim Chhayly, now the deputy prime minister, and other friends at the same school.
Mr. Vanda said that his life was very difficult, even without a bicycle when traveling, it depends on friends.
Due to his hard work, at the end of 1968, he passed the diploma exam at Ang Duong High School in Prey Veng province. Bak Ang and Bak Dup because they want to be teachers to get a salary.
On March 18, 1970, a coup d'état was staged by General Lon Nol to oust Prince Norodom Sihanouk.
At first, he worked as a noodle worker for almost a year, and then he passed the exam to become a private teacher in the same rubber plantation.
The French hired him to teach Khmer children in the rubber plantation village.
He was later entrusted to the French plantation manager and given a job as a food supply manager.
Entering the Mackie forest is a struggle without knowing the exact leader, having to live in the forest to get a job as a grower and distributor to the people.
Unfortunately, due to the fighting between the guerrilla movement of Mackie and the Lon Nol Republican army at that time, Nhim Vanda injured his left arm and was amputated, leaving him paralyzed in one arm. Days.
Between 1972 and 1975, Nhim Vanda went to work at the Kamchay Mear district unit in charge of culture as a typist and typist.
Mr. Vanda said that it was not until 1975 that the Khmer Rouge evacuated people from the city, when he was taken into custody by the Khmer Rouge on charges of being partisan, but he denied any knowledge. No.
In the same year, Vanda was relocated to Prey Lumpeng village, Smaong Khang Cheung commune, Kamchay Mear district.
In early 1977, the Khmer Rouge released Nhim Vanda and allowed him to return to his warehouse and continue typing.
The Khmer Rouge later married his wife of 25 couples at the same time.
He also escaped death because he was not well known.
Prey Veng was liberated on January 5, 1979, and on January 22, Vanda was called to work for the Ministry of Economy and Livelihood in Phnom Penh.
Until 1981, Vanda came to work at the Ministry of Planning and became director of the department.
One year later he was promoted to Deputy Minister and in 1985 he was promoted to Deputy Minister of Defense.
After the 1993 election, he stepped down as Minister of Commerce and took over as special envoy of Co-Prime Minister Hun Sen and Prince Norodom Ranariddh, retaining the role of four gold stars.
Later, he also served as the Minister of Youth Rehabilitation in charge of child education.
In 1996, he was appointed First Vice President of the National Committee for Disaster Management with the rank of Senior Minister and a member of the Central Committee of the Cambodian Red Cross.
In 1998, Mr. Vanda was elected to the Prey Veng Constituency Assembly until today.
Mr. Vanda stated that due to his efforts in all hierarchical tasks, both political and humanitarian, he received patriotic thumbs and honorary doctorates from India, South Korea and the Soviet Union.
Vanda holds a Master of Science in Economics from the Soviet Union, a Master of Science in Political Economy from Korea, and a Master of Science in Business Administration from India.
He was appointed by the King for the second time as a four-star general in 2007 after being ousted as one of several military officials.
In addition to government work, Vanda has two zoos in Kampot province with 105 and 50 species, and Prey Veng with 80 and 40 species.
Both food and treatment techniques are his own budget and donations from various organizations amount to about $ 60,000 a year.
Vanda has signed an agreement with a Japanese company, ARIS, to grow mixed ginger for export.
Vanda divorced his wife, who was married during the Khmer Rouge regime, and later remarried.
They already have a family.
Vanda, now 72, is about to retire, but he insists that Hun Sen still wants him to continue working and that he enjoys it.
Vanda added that both of his zoos were his last job when he retired.
Puy Kea is a world-renowned professional journalist who has taken time off from work to write a book, although he has always dreamed of becoming a diplomat. .
