In addition to being a journalist, he has also been a commentator for the Voice of Khmer America, and has been a United Nations Development Program (UNDP) media and public relations consultant and guest speaker. Educational institutions, as well as television and radio, and he is also a compiler and publisher of outstanding books.
From 2003 to 2014, or in the last 11 years, Puy Kea has researched, compiled and published more than 10 books, but only five of his books are available in markets and bookstores. Only the head and all get a lot of support from readers.
Those books include:
Most of the other books related to the press and law that the embassy and foreign organizations asked him to write, research, translate and publish were not sold in the market.
A country without professional journalists and without the freedom to provide and receive information, the people living in this country will become illiterate in the 21st century and will also shrink the knowledge of the brain. And make it difficult for people to make decisions.
In general, Cambodian journalists are not valued by the general public, despite their excellent role as daily news feed providers.
At this point, Mr. Puy Kea said that was not an individual assessment, but the role of journalists was valued more.
The Cambodian press is divided into several categories, including journalists who are politically inclined to the ruling party, the opposition, and centrist journalists.
Most politically-oriented journalists, especially to the ruling party, are successful in life and socially influential.
However, Mr. Puy Kea emphasized that:
Puy Kea used to face difficulties and challenges where you had to provide information, but he refused to tell or provide documents, and even more difficult is that they turn off the phone and do not talk to each other.
Journalists are sometimes kicked out of their coverage.
Therefore, journalists are trained to be bold, hardworking and agile in order to be successful.
In his personal experience, Puy Kea said that successful journalists are those who read, listen, ask questions and follow the news, and there must be many sources in the ruling party, the opposition party. Civil society, companies or private individuals, that is, must try to find sources and keep more sources is better.
Puy Kea's daily job is to keep track of all events in Cambodia, write them in English, send them to Tokyo, Japan, and then publish the articles throughout Japan and around the world.
In addition to his daily work as a journalist, Puy Kea also has a mission in a large family, as a guardian to help care for one child and 10 grandchildren to ensure that they are educated. Recite correctly and become a good person in society.
He said that journalists are hunters for information every day, whether open or closed.
Dayat: Circumstances forced me to leave political life No one forced me
After entering politics for about 20 years with Prince Norodom Ranariddh, who became the first Prime Minister after the 1993 national election organized by UNTAC, Noranarith Anandayat saw that Engaging in Cambodian politics is very risky and has decided to return to a new career in education in order to train quality grapes to succeed in bamboo.
Ananda Dayat, at the age of 61, decided to leave politics during 2012 and embark on a new career in education at Norton University, which he sees as the ultimate happy life of nation-building. .
Ananda Dayat was born in 1961 in Phnom Penh's Choam Chao commune to a family whose father was a soldier and mother was a businessman.
Before the Khmer Rouge regime in 1975, when he was only 14 years old, he did not study regularly because he had to travel with his father, who moved from province to province.
Fortunately, before the events of April 17, 1975, when the Khmer Rouge took over Phnom Penh, he left Cambodia with his father-in-law for the United States to live there.
Despite being nurtured and cared for by his father and mother, Ananda Yat still works part-time as a habit to earn a living.
He has been studying in the United States since primary, secondary and tertiary education.
After graduating, he worked for Microsoft for two years, then worked as an environmental scientist for another company contracted by the US Department of the Environment.
During the 1980s, receiving information about the political situation in Cambodia changed, many innocent people fled to settle in Thai territory or along the Thai-Cambodian border. Make Ananda Day decide to do humanitarian work for the first time.
During his three-month vacation, he voluntarily travels from the United States to Cambodian refugee camps along the Thai border to work on human rights at the Royal Office of the Crown Prince in Asia. Prince Norodom Ranariddh is the royal representative.
It was not until the 1990s that Ananda Day began his political career as political negotiations led to the signing of the Paris Peace Accords.
He mobilized human resources and funds to support the FUNCINPEC resistance movement until 1993, when he participated in Cambodia's first general election, in which FUNCINPEC won the election.
However, after the election, he returned to the United States to complete his master's degree and continue working as usual.
In 1996, when the situation in Cambodia became very unstable, Ananda Dayat decided to sell all his property in the United States to come and live permanently in Cambodia to help Prince Norodom Ranariddh again. At the time, he was the first prime minister, and Dayat then served in the cabinet of the first prime minister.
In the same year, he served as Undersecretary of State at the Council of Ministers, but not for long, with dissenting views from insiders and outsiders, unable to find common ground at work. There was also a conflict.
He said that from 1996 to 2006, political events were very dangerous and in 1997 he was trapped in the Prime Minister's Office for two nights and three days before escaping to hide in the Singapore Embassy. A US embassy car came to pick him up.
Several high-ranking officials in the cabinet also died in the incident.
I have now ended my life as a politician.
Mr. Dayat resigned from politics because he saw that today's politics is mature and that Cambodians know how to resolve political disputes on their own and in a Cambodian way, and the social situation is stable, we can walk by. My heart is rich in food, so it was one of those moody times where I would break into endless soliloquy with myself.
Dean of the Faculty of Social Sciences at Norton University, majoring in law, business, finance, hotel, hospitality and tourism, Anand Dayat is also involved in teaching students.
He co-organizes curricula every year to reform and update curricula in line with Cambodian cultural traditions and to keep pace with regional and international developments. High quality.
He said that both of these factors have been trained for the past 18 years and that working in the education sector is more comfortable than in politics because politics is more risky and dangerous.
Ananda Yat said he has been an American citizen since 1979, so the United States is his second home and he can come and go at any time with his US passport. .
He says his ultimate goal is to share knowledge from the United States to help Cambodian children develop a patriotic spirit through Norton University.
Dayat married in 1992, has a daughter who lives with his mother in the United States and is studying there.
Dayat has always been very sympathetic to the poor Cambodians, and he has always done what he can to help the poor as much as he can.
Ananda Dayat says he has been traveling by motorbike, taxi and tricycle since 1996, except when traveling to meet with officials.
