Thach Setha spent most of his life doing things for the Khmer Krom
The Khmer Kampuchea Krom issue, which was severely persecuted by the Vietnamese authorities, has always been in Thach Setha's heart, and he often sheds tears when he thinks of Vietnam capturing your scholars or intellectuals. Khmer patriots are imprisoned, tortured or killed because they dare to defend Khmer religious traditions, customs and literature.
Mr. Thach Setha was born on May 14, 1953 in Anchanh village, Ta Sek commune, Tra Cu district, Preah Trapeang province, Kampuchea Krom territory in present-day southern Vietnam.
Setha has struggled most of his life to preserve Khmer traditions, culture and literature.
Mr. Setha told Cambodia over the weekend that he grew up in Kampuchea Krom and graduated there according to the Khmer curriculum in Cambodia.
In 1970, he came to Cambodia to continue his studies at the Faculty of Humanities and Literature in Phnom Penh, but did not finish, having to return to Kampuchea Krom the same year and graduate from a local Buddhist high school. Born and became a professor there.
Mr. Setha described the painful background that Vietnam committed against Cambodian politicians during 1984, who were arrested, tortured and unjustly killed.
Setha will never forget that in 1984, a campaign by the Vietnamese authorities began to capture all Khmer intellectuals in Kampuchea Krom.
At the same time, all schools were closed to learn or teach the Khmer language because the Vietnamese authorities needed to inspect and control all the schools that were previously controlled by the Khmer.
In the same year, about 4,248 Cambodian teachers and scholars, some 6,000, were imprisoned and tortured by the Vietnamese, and some are still disabled today.
The pain has been ingrained in his heart and has prompted him to join the Cambodian resistance movement in politics since then.
Setha began work on the school two days after the arrest of the Khmer Krom political leaders at the time.
Vietnamese authorities have accused Cambodian political leaders of conspiracy since 1972.
At that time, Vietnamese authorities sought to arrest him, but he fled to Phnom Penh.
Mr. Setha fled to Cambodia with a constant following, but because of his last name change, he escaped to Phnom Penh in 1985 and found a temporary job.
After the 1993 election, he became a member of Ta Son San BLDP's political party in Kandal province, and at the same time worked as a teacher there.
Mr. Setha is one of the founders who co-founded the opposition newspaper, Khmer Consciousness, Voice of Youth, Khmer Freedom, Nokor Phnom and Khmer Ideals.
In 1995, Sam Rainsy was expelled from FUNCINPEC and later formed the Khmer National Party to run in the 1998 election.
Because of his newspaper article, he had the opportunity to meet Sam Rainsy.
In a bloody explosion in central Phnom Penh on July 5-6, 1997, between forces loyal to Prince Norodom Ranariddh, the first prime minister, and forces loyal to Hun Sen, the second prime minister. Setha, like other opposition politicians, fled to Thailand, passing through several battlefields, but without danger, reaching Thailand safely.
In the 1998 election, Setha became president of the Prey Veng Khmer National Party and was re-elected to parliament in Phnom Penh and Kratie, but did not win a seat.
After the election, in 1999, the party appointed him as a senator.
In his political activities, Setha is not interested in the power of Bon Sak because he has always been attached to the Khmer people in Kampuchea Krom.
In 2000, he organized the first Millennium Memorial to commemorate Kampuchea Krom at Chaktomuk Hall, and this event made the Khmer-Krom movement more recognizable.
He said that during the reign of His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk, he was very supportive, as he always assigned representatives to participate in all Khmer Krom festival activities.
Mr. Setha said he was often threatened when he organized ceremonies related to the Khmer Krom.
Mr. Setha did a lot of work for the Khmer Krom and visited many Khmer Krom communities abroad and established the Khmer Kampuchea Krom community in Cambodia, where he is now the chairman and celebrates the anniversary of the loss of Kampuchea Krom territory every day. Last year, it continued to demand that the government declare the day France ceded Kampuchea Krom to Vietnam as a national holiday.
In 2003, Setha submitted a letter to the French National Assembly and Senate requesting that the superpower repeal 49,733 laws, as it was the one in which France ceded 21 territories to Kampuchea Krom for unjust control of Vietnam. Between Vietnam and France on June 4, 1949.
Like the demonstration in front of the Vietnamese Embassy in the past, it is to remind Cambodian children to love our country and our land.
In addition to working for the Khmer Krom, Setha has been one of the founders of the opposition party since 1995 and is currently a member of the CNRP Standing Committee.
Mr. Setha has been a senator for eight and a half years and also a member of the Capital Council.
Setha was married in 1999 and has four children.
Setha led an anti-Vietnamese demonstration to demand that a Vietnamese embassy spokesman publicly apologize to the Cambodian people for historically falsely claiming that Kampuchea Krom was Vietnamese territory had long been considered Death is common in his movement in relation to the Khmer Kampuchea Krom.
