Kong Koam is a hot politician for Vietnam
The word Vietnam or Vietnam is still a hot topic in Cambodian politics for opposition politicians such as Sam Rainsy Party senator Kong Koam.
Since 1979, Kong Koam has been the first Cambodian delegation to Laos, Vietnam, Soviet Union, Cuba to join the Non-Aligned Movement.
He said that since 1951, the CPP has always been under Vietnamese influence, and Vietnam came to organize the party to revive in 1979.
He said that he still had anti-Vietnamese communist ideas that could not stay with the CPP, could not sell ideals, so decided to leave the CPP.
In 1992, there was the Sixth General Assembly to organize the CPP before the 1993 election.
At that time, Mr. Kong Koam resigned from the party and did not join any party, but voted for FUNCINPEC at that time.
In his personal life, Kong Koam began his political career after 1979, when the Vietnamese army took control of Cambodia after the overthrow of the Khmer Rouge regime.
It was not until January 1979, Kong Koam, one of the most knowledgeable people in Kok Lveang village, that Vietnam appointed him village chief and, with the support of the village elders, agreed to be the village chief.
At the same time, chaos ensued as people armed with knives searched for cattle and kept pointing out that the cows belonged to them.
At that time, he also participated in solving to avoid killing each other.
At the same time, Kong Koam also faced personal insecurity as he often protested against the Vietnamese army when people complained about the Vietnamese stealing their chickens.
Concerned that the Vietnamese would retaliate, Kong Koam decided to flee to Phnom Penh.
Fearing Vietnam will hurt us again when we sue and punish each other, so it is very insecure for me to continue living in my hometown.
At that time, I decided to come to Phnom Penh by car.
Arriving in Phnom Penh, I was also one of the first people that Prime Minister Hun Sen ever described as only a few dozen people who first arrived in Phnom Penh.
With friends who used to live in the same village and worked with Chea Sim, Kong Koam has been working and living with the CPP since 1979.
Hun Sen called me to ask questions and write a resume for him, and he sent a few people from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs to take me to the Ministry to find a room for me to rest in, because at that time I was resting in the Ministry of Food and Agriculture.
In the same year, 1979, when Vietnam was preparing for the trial of Khmer Rouge leader Kong Koam, he was appointed director of the Press Department to prepare foreign journalists, mostly from the communist bloc, for the trial. This.
After the trial of the then Khmer Rouge leader, Kong Koam was appointed director of the Foreign Ministry's policy department, called the Consolidation Department.
He later became an assistant to Hun Sen's foreign minister and was appointed ambassador to Vietnam from 1981 to 1982 before returning.
First as Deputy Foreign Minister until 1984, I was appointed First Deputy Minister with the rank of Secretary of State, and at the 1985 General Assembly I was elected a member of the Central Committee of the CPP.
After Hun Sen became prime minister, he was appointed foreign minister at the time, probably in late 1986 and continued until 1988.
From the point of view of unification at the time, Kong Koam strongly opposed the party's policy, which allowed him to hold a press conference to name those who could not be reunited: Khmer Rouge leaders such as Pol Pot, Ieng Sary, Nuon Chea, Khieu Samphan Ta Mok Son Sen.
In 1989, Kong Koam was appointed Minister of Public Affairs after it was considered that his opposition was not too strong.
At the Party Central Committee meeting on September 9, 1989, it was decided that the Vietnamese army should withdraw from Cambodia, which was the first time the meeting had been held without the Vietnamese presence, which Vietnam was just off the stage.
Kong Koam disagreed with the document that the CPP set out as a strategic military measure to achieve complete and final victory over other Cambodian resistance movements, as the fighting resulted in the deaths of Cambodians on all sides. Is a strategy to kill Cambodians.
After years of resigning from the CPP, Kong Koam decided to return to politics in 1995 after Sam Rainsy was unfairly expelled from the party by FUNCINPEC.
When a bloody coup took place in central Phnom Penh in 1997, Kong Koam became a scapegoat for the CPP, forcing him to flee Phnom Penh to Bangkok, Thailand. He mediated only after he returned to the country and became a senator in 1999 until today.
Kong Koam was born in 1941 to a farming family in Kork Lveang village, Kandol Chrum commune, Ponhea Krek district, Kampong Cham province, now Tbong Khmum province.
He has three siblings, two brothers.
He married in 1965 and has six children, four boys, all of whom now serve in the government, as members of parliament and senators.
He passed the lower secondary school examination in 1957, then in Kampong Cham province.
Later, he continued his studies at Suong High School until 1960 with a diploma and continued his studies at Kampong Cham High School until he graduated in 1963.
In 1964, he passed the examination as a lower secondary school teacher to teach at Prek Sandek Secondary School, Sithor Kandal District, Prey Veng Province from 1965 to 1968.
Later, he moved to teach at Samdech Chuon Nath Phnom Pros Phnom Srey School in Kampong Cham province.
From 1968 to 1970, there was a coup d'état overthrowing Prince Norodom Sihanouk by Lon Nol.
Later, around 1972 or 1973, the secondary and high schools in Kampong Cham province were not safe, so there was a gathering in Kampong Cham High School, which is called a special high school, where teachers gather and all teachers are mobilized. As a commando to serve the Lon Nol regime.
Kong Koam also lived a miserable life under the Khmer Rouge regime, like most Cambodians.
He knows French and English, but not much.
Chou Bun Eng was lucky despite being forced to marry by the Khmer Rouge
Chou Bun Eng was one of many Cambodian women who were forced by the Khmer Rouge to marry a man she had never met before.
However, she was lucky to be married to a man who was educated, meek and diligent.
Ms. Bun Eng is a successful woman who has progressed from a math teacher at Preah Sisowath High School to the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior, a key government ministry, while the number of women working in government institutions remains. Still low.
Chou Bun Eng was born in 1956 into a farming family in Battambang city, Battambang province, with 10 siblings, but so far four have died.
Ms. Bun Eng is currently the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior, Secretary-General of the National Anti-Human Trafficking Committee (NEC) and Vice-Chair of the Cambodian National Council for Women (NCWC). Initiated the idea of promoting the role of women in state institutions, starting with the Ministry of Interior.
She said that at each meeting at the Ministry of Interior, from the departmental level onwards, she observed that there was only one or two female representatives among the 400 to 500 men.
Seeing the small number of women holding positions in government institutions, Ms. Bun Eng took the opportunity on International Women's Day, March 8, 2009 to ask Deputy Prime Minister Sar Kheng to consider increasing the role of women at least. Available from Deputy Director General down.
A week later, there was support from Prime Minister Hun Sen, who instructed other ministries to follow the example of the Ministry of Interior.
Looking back, Chou Bun Eng said that in 1974, after graduating from high school, she decided to continue her studies in Phnom Penh.
But with the shelling war, she decided to take a temporary job in a factory when she was 19 years old.
Shortly afterwards, the Khmer Rouge army bombed Phnom Penh in April 1975.
She was later detained at the 32nd Education Department near present-day Phnom Prasidh, where she was beaten for questioning, claiming to be a military police officer, to the point of claiming to be a CIA agent.
When questioned, they found out that I had finished school and was beaten because we were intellectuals.
So my punishment is always heavy and they want us to say that we are a military police.
Until one day, she was made to stand alone while the family who ran with her to another place.
Living under the Khmer Rouge regime for 3 years, 8 months and 20 days, the black shirt regime that killed nearly two million Cambodians, Chou Bun Eng was eventually forced by the Khmer Rouge to marry a man who She had never known her before.
Forced to marry in groups of five and not to be told in advance, the immediate marriage took place in Otaki commune, Thmor Kol district, Battambang province in 1978.
After the marriage, she did not live with her husband, even the first night because the new husband had a fever and later separated for a while, he had to take care of his elderly mother.
However, the separation did not allow her to forget her husband, as she saw that he was not a cruel, meek, and hardworking man.
She later inquired about his background and learned that he was an educated learner and a mechanic.
Ms. Chou Bun Eng lived with her husband, who was forced to marry by the Khmer Rouge, and had four daughters.
Unfortunately, her husband died in a car accident in 2006 leaving her a widow to care for her four children.
When the Khmer Rouge era was overthrown, Chou Bun Eng worked as a mathematics teacher at Sisowath High School and continued her studies until graduating from Phnom Penh University in 1991.
In 1994, she was transferred from the Ministry of Education to the Secretariat of State for Women's Affairs, with the rank of Deputy Director of the Department of Information.
After a brief stint as Secretary General of the Women's Association, Chou Bun Eng returned in 2005 and was appointed Director General of Women's Affairs and Social Development, where she contributed a number of books related to Promote social morality, values of women and Cambodian families.
Ms. Bun Eng used to compose poetry essays for the Buddhist Institute and write stories for the National Radio.
In addition to writing novels and poems, she also wrote educational articles for the Ministry of Women's Affairs.
She used to be the editor-in-chief of Forward Magazine.
Moving from one ministry to another to become the current Secretary of State for the Ministry of Interior, working with many men, Ms. Bun Eng is confident in her abilities, although in Cambodian society there is still a mindset. That women's abilities are still not comparable to men's.
But I can say that the environment is an evaluator, in fact, women rarely make mistakes.
I am proud to fulfill the role of the Royal Government.
In her senior position in the Ministry of Interior, Ms. Chou Bun Eng has never forgotten the merits of the CPP in sacrificing fresh flesh and blood to liberate the country from the genocidal era of Pol Pot.
During the Pol Pot era, I was beaten, injected to death, lost consciousness, or interrogated with a rubber band over my face.
Thus, taking care of a life is almost impossible.
But in the end, we are still alive today because there are dead people who are called CPP fighters.
Because of this understanding, Ms. Chou Bun Eng joined the CPP and has been working to support the CPP to this day.
She explained why she could not join the other party, stating that the other party acted as an opponent like us, to his left, to his right, to ours, up and down.
Ms. Bun Eng said that in the fifth term government is working to reform in all areas to improve and be able to regain its popularity, and the leadership of Prime Minister Hun Sen has announced that. Brushing out all past inactive officials.
Ms. Bun Eng, who also holds a Master's degree in Political Science, said:
She said that in her future political career, she would remain loyal to the CPP because it had revived her and had everything to this day.
Thorn Saray: I sacrifice a lot for society
For the sake of human rights, for the sake of a democratic society, a white-haired man with a tiger's heart, who has a patriotic heart, has to live with his family and swim through all obstacles to open a human rights association called Adhoc after Released from a political prison during the communist era, commonly known as Thorn Saray.
Mr. Thorn Saray is currently the President of Adhoc Human Rights Defenders Association, which has 24 provincial offices working in the field of human rights to provide legal advice to the general population who have problems and help prepare citizens' complaints.
A white man, a child of a subsistence farmer, was born in Orang Ov district, Kampong Cham province, but grew up in Sihanoukville and continued his studies in Phnom Penh in 1969 until graduating from law school. Science and economics.
He said that at first he was not a human rights worker, but after graduating he worked for the Ministry of Finance for 10 months before the Khmer Rouge came to power.
Then, during the Khmer Rouge regime, he was evacuated to his hometown.
It was there that he was imprisoned for training in the Department of Corrections for allegedly being a junior for 10 months, and he was again evacuated from his hometown to Sambo district. In Kratie province, as a newcomer, at the age of 24, he worked hard to adapt and adapt to a new life in order to escape the Khmer Rouge massacre.
After the fall of the Khmer Rouge regime, he returned to work in Phnom Penh, working at the Institute of Sociology until 1989 and publishing a number of books, such as the History of Money in Cambodia and the Khmer Economic Journal. Initially in Cambodia, and because of his stubborn work, he published what he had researched, much to the dismay of Vietnamese experts at the time.
Later, a group of people invited him to join a political party. At that time, he was just looking at a document to find out their principles. A month later, Vietnamese experts reported his name to the Ministry of Interior, which was the arrest. Jail again as a political prisoner.
The 39-year-old man, who did not know how to be under pressure, was jailed twice.
